/* GENERATED by tools/meta/dissemination/build_microcosm_public_site.py; lazy governed term previews. */
window.__MICROCOSM_TERM_PREVIEWS__ = {"schema_version":"microcosm_term_preview_index_v1","term_count":343,"terms":[{"object_id":"term:above_and_beyond","preferred_label":"above and beyond","reader_preview":"In ai_workflow, above and beyond means bounded follow-through that makes the requested result durable, then stops at a named boundary.","reader_card":"It describes a finished pass that did the immediately useful follow-through, not one that wandered off into new scope. When a task surfaces something that would otherwise go stale or be hard for the next person to find, the extra work is to make that one result durable: name the boundary, do the smallest improvement that prevents rediscovery, run a focused check, and leave evidence. The boundary is part of the trait: the work has a source case, a place it belongs, a check, and a clear stopping point.","reader_rule":"When a pass is described this way, take it to mean the result was made durable and was bounded, not that scope was expanded; the record of what was checked tells you how far the follow-through actually reached.","reader_deep":"It is the active form of care and standards held together: care protects whoever reads the result next, and the safety condition is that the extra work stays bounded, with a recorded stopping point rather than an open-ended campaign."},{"object_id":"term:accountability_layer","preferred_label":"accountability layer","reader_preview":"The accountability layer is the set of evidence records, result records, source links, and scope limits that lets a reader trace what a mechanism ran and where the resulting claim stops.","reader_card":"This layer sits around a mechanism rather than replacing it. It connects the visible claim to the recorded run or replay, the source that owns it, the kind of evidence attached to it, and the boundary beyond which that evidence says nothing.","reader_rule":"Use the accountability layer to follow a claim back to evidence and source; do not treat the page that presents those links as the authority itself.","reader_deep":"The layer makes a claim inspectable by preserving provenance and limits together. A record without a source route or a scope limit is not enough to establish accountable public evidence."},{"object_id":"term:actor_axes","preferred_label":"actor axes","reader_preview":"Actor axes keep separate how an actor accesses the system, what it can do, how it is invoked, and what role it plays.","reader_card":"Rather than ranking actors on one scale from weak to strong, the system measures them along several axes at once. The primary axis is substrate authority, which marks whether an actor has live, direct access to the system's own files and tools (Type A) or works from an interface, an API, an injected copy of context, or the public web without that direct access (Type B). The other axes, including capability tier, invocation surface, topology role, evidence access, purpose lane, and reasoning budget, are recorded separately so that being clever, being expensive, and being delegated never get confused with having authority over the real material.","reader_rule":"When an actor is described by where it sits on these axes, read each axis on its own. A capable or costly actor is not automatically one with direct authority over the system, and a delegated helper with live access can hold more authority than a powerful service that only sees a copy.","reader_deep":"The decisive axis is substrate authority: a tool-using agent with live access to the repository counts as Type A, while operator dashboards, bridges, hosted models, and anything reasoning over injected or public context count as Type B unless they too hold live access to the private material."},{"object_id":"term:admitted_proof_step","preferred_label":"admitted proof step","reader_preview":"An admitted proof step is a gap left deliberately in a proof: a step assumed rather than proved, which the proof checker accepts on request and flags.","reader_card":"It is the single most important number to check in any formal corpus. One admitted step in the wrong place can carry an entire result, so a large corpus with an admission may establish less than a small one without. Formal systems make this checkable rather than a matter of trust, because the checker records every admission.","reader_rule":"Check the admitted-step count first. Everything downstream of an admission is assumed, not proved.","reader_deep":"The reason to publish the count rather than the intention is that admissions are normal during development and easy to leave behind. The number is a fact about the corpus as shipped."},{"object_id":"term:affordance_row","preferred_label":"affordance row","reader_preview":"An affordance row is one selectable navigation entry that says what an artifact is, why to open it, and where its source evidence lives.","reader_card":"The system organises navigation as rows, not as monolithic documents. Each affordance row is the atomic unit of that navigation surface: it states one option, the next thing an agent or reader could open, along with enough structured detail to decide whether to open it. A row can be shown at different depths, from a one-line flag through a fuller card to a detailed view with the exact command that produced its evidence, so a choice can be made from a budgeted menu instead of by searching through source files.","reader_rule":"When you meet an affordance row, read it as one option on a menu: it tells you what the thing is, what opening it costs, and what backs it, so you can decide your next read without scanning the underlying files.","reader_deep":"The row compresses choice rather than content: it turns \"what can I do with this artifact\" into source-derived structure, with each row being projectable at several depths, linked to its evidence, marked with a lifecycle state, and typed by what it is allowed to claim."},{"object_id":"term:agent","preferred_label":"agent","reader_preview":"On this site, an agent is a software worker that can read the public repository, run a named command, and leave a result, still subject to a separate check.","reader_card":"Agents are co-workers of the public slice: clone the repository, run one component, inspect the evidence it leaves. Fluency is not authority. An agent that cannot point at a source path and a check has not yet done public work.","reader_rule":"Judge an agent by the command it ran and the record it left, not by how confident the write-up sounds.","reader_deep":"Different agents may share a model and still differ in tools, mutation rights, and what they are allowed to see. Type A and Type B name those authority differences. The public pages do not host an agent service; they tell a local coding agent what to run."},{"object_id":"term:agent_reliability","preferred_label":"agent reliability and safety","reader_preview":"Agent reliability and safety is the question of whether an AI agent's report of what it did matches what it actually did, and whether it stays inside the limits it was given.","reader_card":"An agent can say it committed a fix, ran a test, or finished a task, in fluent prose, with none of it having happened. Reliability work checks the report against the record the action would have left. Safety work checks the second half: whether the agent stayed inside its sandbox, refused what it should refuse, and did not quietly widen its own permissions.","reader_rule":"A component in this area checks a specific failure it can reproduce. None of them establishes that an agent is reliable in general.","reader_deep":"It is the largest area of the public release, with 20 components. The worked public example belongs to it: an audit that runs real git and pytest against notes carrying three planted forgeries, and is required to name each refusal."},{"object_id":"term:agent_seed","preferred_label":"agent seed","reader_preview":"An agent seed is the durable, attributed record of an AI agent's own observations, synthesis, and drift signals, kept separate from raw seed so agent interpretation cannot be mistaken for the operator's original words.","reader_card":"When a coding agent working inside this system learns something worth keeping, such as a pattern it noticed or a correction it made, it deposits that into an agent seed rather than into the operator's raw seed. The deposit is stamped with the agent that wrote it and the sources it drew on. It is treated as useful but lower authority than the operator's own statements of intent, so the system can retain what an agent learned without that material being mistaken for the operator's voice.","reader_rule":"Take an agent seed as an AI agent's recorded reasoning, attributed to that agent, not as the operator's intent. It carries who wrote it and what it drew on, so weigh it accordingly.","reader_deep":"Keeping agent-authored synthesis in its own seed is what lets the system absorb an agent's learning while the operator's raw seed stays the higher authority on intent, with neither layer impersonating the other."},{"object_id":"term:agent_trace","preferred_label":"agent trace","reader_preview":"An agent trace is the recorded sequence of what an AI agent actually did during a session: the calls it made, the files it touched, and the order it did them in.","reader_card":"An agent's own account of its work is a summary written by the thing being audited. The trace is the record underneath it, and the two can disagree. Keeping the trace separate is what makes it possible to check whether a reported action occurred, which is a different question from whether the report is plausible.","reader_rule":"Compare an agent\u2019s closeout with the captured trace, but read the trace only within its instrumentation coverage: it is evidence for recorded events, not proof that every action was observed.","reader_deep":"This is the same discipline the rest of the site applies to itself: prose describing a run is not the run. An agent is simply a case where the prose is unusually convincing."},{"object_id":"term:ai_native","preferred_label":"AI-native","reader_preview":"AI-native describes a system or workflow designed around AI agents doing bounded, inspectable work through explicit tools, records, permissions, and checks.","reader_card":"The phrase describes architecture, not intelligence or correctness. Agents are expected to inspect, build, maintain, route, or validate artifacts, while human direction, source authority, and separate checks still constrain the result.","reader_rule":"Read AI-native as a description of how the workflow is organized, never as proof that the workflow is autonomous, reliable, or correct.","reader_deep":"The system makes agent context, tool access, durable state, handoffs, and validation first-class because agent work must survive beyond one chat and remain open to inspection."},{"object_id":"term:annex","preferred_label":"annex","reader_preview":"An annex is a curated account of reusable patterns from an external framework, repository, or document, translated into this system's vocabulary without importing the outside source as local authority.","reader_card":"Annexes live under the `annexes/` directory and hold shape-level notes about external sources: what problem each one solves and which patterns are worth borrowing. They exist so that before a piece of machinery is built from scratch, the system can check whether the same shape has already been solved elsewhere and translate the idea in, rather than re-deriving it or forking another project's structure. Each annex is a reference point for a pattern, never a binding rule the system must obey.","reader_rule":"Treat an annex as a pointer to outside prior art, not as part of the system's own authority. It tells you where a pattern came from and how it was adapted, not what the system is required to do.","reader_deep":"The governing discipline is adapt, do not adopt: an external primitive is compared against the local one and a more coherent version is synthesised when the evidence supports it, so the system can learn from open work without taking on its ontology."},{"object_id":"term:anti_principle","preferred_label":"anti-principle","reader_preview":"An anti-principle is a named shortcut the system is built to refuse, written down so the refusal can be checked against the obligation it protects.","reader_card":"Each anti-principle names one tempting skip: a way to look done without carrying the basis, the checker, the typed refusal, or another required field. It is a governing object, not a style note. The doctrine page lists the kind; the cards below and the doctrine reference show the individual refusals.","reader_rule":"Read an anti-principle as the shortcut being refused, then find the obligation it would have skipped. The name alone does not prove the refusal ran.","reader_deep":"Positive support and anti-principle rejection are separate. A passing case does not establish that the wrong case was tried and refused."},{"object_id":"term:apache_2_0","preferred_label":"Apache-2.0","reader_preview":"Apache-2.0 is a permissive open-source licence: anyone may use, modify, and redistribute the code, including commercially, provided they keep the required notices.","reader_card":"It is one of the permissive licences, meaning it does not require anyone who redistributes the code to publish their own changes, which is what separates it from the copyleft family such as the GPL. What it does require is that the notices travel with the code: the licence text, the copyright line, and a statement of any file that was modified. It also grants every user a patent licence from the contributors, and withdraws that grant from anyone who brings a patent claim alleging the work infringes.","reader_rule":"The licence governs what a recipient may do with the published files. It grants nothing over the private system those files were taken from, makes no warranty, and carries no claim that the code is correct.","reader_deep":"The choice matters for a reader deciding whether they can build on this. Permissive terms plus an explicit patent grant is the combination most commonly accepted inside companies, so the published components can be run, modified, and embedded without a legal review of the kind a copyleft licence would trigger. The reference page states the terms, the authorship, the no-affiliation note, and the point where the public source slice ends."},{"object_id":"term:appeal_lifecycle","preferred_label":"appeal lifecycle","reader_preview":"An appeal lifecycle is the governed path for formally challenging a label or decision: from dispute, failure classification, and repair ownership through recorded reasoning to a resolution that either closes or reopens the judgement.","reader_card":"When the system makes a judgment, such as labelling a route or expecting a particular result, that judgment can be contested. The appeal lifecycle is the path that contest follows: the challenge is classified by cause, such as a model failure, a weak prompt, a lost packet, a wrong expected label, a stale projection, or a gap in the underlying scheme; it is handed to whichever plane is responsible for repair; it can hold back affected changes until it is settled; and it ends with a written rationale and, where needed, a test that proves the fix.","reader_rule":"Read where an appeal sits in its path before trusting the judgment it contests. A pending appeal means a judgment is under dispute and may be holding back the changes it touches; a closed one carries the rationale that settled it.","reader_deep":"The point of an appeal lifecycle is that disputed judgments turn into cumulative learning rather than one-off corrections: each appeal names the kind of error it found, so repeated challenges expose patterns in where the system gets things wrong."},{"object_id":"term:apply","preferred_label":"apply","reader_preview":"An apply is the governed write step: it changes a named source target under a stated operation and validates the result.","reader_card":"Apply is the execution half of the observe-then-apply pair: observing gathers candidate changes, applying is the only step permitted to alter the system's files and records. Each apply names what it touches and what operation it performs, validates the state before and after, and where possible records how to undo it. It is the disciplined gate through which any modification to the substrate must pass.","reader_rule":"When something says it was applied, expect a specific target, a stated operation, and a before-and-after check behind it, not an unbounded edit. Observations alone do not change anything until an apply runs.","reader_deep":"Apply exists to keep autonomous mutation disciplined: the system can surface many candidate insights, but only this lane is allowed to change source state, and only under explicit operation, validation, and rollback posture."},{"object_id":"term:artifact","preferred_label":"artifact","reader_preview":"An artifact is any durable file, row, packet, page, record, or other inspectable object produced, maintained, or consumed by the system.","reader_card":"Artifact is the broad category; its kind determines how it should be read. Source artifacts may own truth, generated artifacts project other sources, and result artifacts preserve what a run found, so the word alone says nothing about authority.","reader_rule":"When something is called an artifact, identify its kind, producer, authority posture, and freshness before relying on it.","reader_deep":"Treating every durable object as an artifact gives the system one inventory language while preserving the differences between source, generated, evidence, navigation, and compatibility roles."},{"object_id":"term:artifact_kind","preferred_label":"artifact kind","reader_preview":"An artifact kind is the category an item belongs to (component, paper module, standard, principle, or doctrine card), used to choose the right collection before browsing its individual rows.","reader_card":"The system is not one flat list of files. Its objects are grouped into a fixed set of kinds, and each kind has its own page where every member of that class is listed together. An artifact kind names one of those classes. Choosing a kind narrows the whole system to one type of thing, for example all components or all paper modules, before you open any single entry.","reader_rule":"When you meet an artifact kind, read it as the category you are inside: it tells you what type of object every row on that page is, and the boundary of what that page can contain.","reader_deep":"Each kind also carries a support status, recording whether its members can be browsed as full rows with source evidence or are only listed by name, so the kind sets your expectation of how much you can inspect."},{"object_id":"term:assimilation","preferred_label":"assimilation","reader_preview":"Assimilation is the spine step after explanation: a checked result is written back so the next pass can use it, rather than leaving the finding as a one-off note.","reader_card":"The architecture page walks a shared path that ends evidence, explanation, assimilation. The tour calls that last turn an assimilation loop that feeds the next pass. Pattern assimilation is the public component that performs one such step on a named pattern, not a claim that the whole private system has been absorbed.","reader_rule":"Treat assimilation as a recorded write-back with a source and a check, not as a vibe that the system now knows.","reader_deep":"Without this step, a successful check dies in the session that ran it. With it, the next agent can start from a durable record instead of rediscovering the same finding. That is why the spine puts assimilation after explanation rather than after the first idea."},{"object_id":"term:atlas","preferred_label":"Atlas","reader_preview":"The Atlas is the full-system contents map used to select a target and route from overview to source, work record, and evidence.","reader_card":"The Why walkthrough starts with the Atlas as the full system, most of which is not released. Surface Atlas and Code Map are both described as derived from the backend rather than hard-coded. The public architecture map is a published slice of that idea, not a claim that the unpublished operator Atlas is on this website.","reader_rule":"Read Atlas as a map for choosing where to look, then follow the named source or component. Do not treat the map as the source.","reader_deep":"A contents map that is generated can stay coupled to the repository. A hand-drawn one cannot. The public pages inherit that posture only where a generated map is actually shipped."},{"object_id":"term:audit","preferred_label":"audit","reader_preview":"An audit here is a check of whether a report matches what actually happened, rather than a check of whether the work itself was correct.","reader_card":"It is a narrower thing than the word usually implies. An audit compares an account against a record, so it catches an action reported but not taken, or taken and not reported. Whether the action was a good idea is a separate question the audit does not touch.","reader_rule":"An audit tells you whether a report was faithful, not whether the work was right.","reader_deep":"This is the check most systems lack, because a component's own summary is normally the only account of what it did, and it is written by the thing under examination."},{"object_id":"term:authority","preferred_label":"authority","reader_preview":"Authority is the recognized right of a particular source, rule, or actor to settle a stated kind of fact or permit a stated action.","reader_card":"Authority is always scoped: a registry may own an identifier, a theorem checker may settle proof validity, and an operator may authorize publication. None of those roles automatically owns the others.","reader_rule":"Ask who or what is allowed to settle this exact question before treating a statement as final.","reader_deep":"A useful system keeps authority close to the artifact that can actually maintain the fact and prevents summaries or generated views from silently inheriting it."},{"object_id":"term:axiom","preferred_label":"axiom","reader_preview":"In mathematics an axiom is an assumption taken as given rather than proved; on this site's software pages the same word names a small set of always-binding system constraints, not mathematical axioms.","reader_card":"If you are reading a maths page, start with the ordinary meaning: an axiom is a starting assumption of a theory, such as Lean's three standard axioms (propext, Quot.sound, Classical.choice). If you are reading the doctrine or rules-and-ideas pages, axiom means one of this system's primitive software constraints (currently about seven, still provisional) sitting above principles in a governance lattice. Those two uses share a family resemblance (something taken as given) and must not be collapsed: a Lean axiom is a logical assumption a proof may rely on; a system axiom here is a design invariant the software is built to preserve.","reader_rule":"Ask which page you are on. Maths: which logical assumptions were used. Software: which system constraint is being named. Do not read AX-7 as a mathematical axiom, and do not read Classical.choice as a Plectis design rule.","reader_deep":"The analogy 'an axiom is to a principle as a mathematical axiom is to a theorem' is how this system's doctrine describes itself. It is an analogy, not an identification. Lean kernel acceptance relative to standard axioms is a mathematical fact. Whether a software axiom is a good design choice is a separate judgement."},{"object_id":"term:band","preferred_label":"band","reader_preview":"A band is one named depth of description, from a one-word signal up to full evidence, that says what a short view must include, may omit, and must link onward to.","reader_card":"Many records on this site are written as a ladder of bands rather than a single block of text: word, phrase, flag, card, context, deep, and evidence, depending on the profile. The same item is available at each band, and the bands sit one inside the next like a telescope or a set of nested dolls, so a short browse can open into a working summary and then into full detail. A band is governed by a contract, not by a word count, so what appears at each level is fixed by rule rather than by how much someone happened to write.","reader_rule":"A band tells you the depth of what you are reading, not just its length. If a band does not answer your question, follow it down to the next band rather than assuming the detail is absent.","reader_deep":"The depth ladder belongs to a compression profile, which declares the full set of bands and what each one must hold; an individual record then fills those bands in. This is why a description can be expanded or collapsed in a predictable way, and why drift is flagged against one specific band rather than the whole file."},{"object_id":"term:barrier","preferred_label":"barrier","reader_preview":"A barrier is a proved result showing that a particular route to a problem cannot work, closing off an approach rather than advancing towards a solution.","reader_card":"Barriers are the most useful negative results and the easiest to overstate. One that closes two approaches can read as closing the subject, and the difference matters entirely: an approach still open after a barrier is where the remaining work is. Every barrier recorded here is required to state what it does not rule out.","reader_rule":"For any barrier, read what it leaves open. That is the part that decides what to do next.","reader_deep":"This requirement came from a real error rather than from caution. A parity obstruction was carried to the edge of publication as closing the certificate engines for one problem; a third, weaker engine in the same file was untouched by it and was found by accident. No surface recorded which engines existed, so nothing could have caught it."},{"object_id":"term:base","preferred_label":"base","reader_preview":"In these papers, a base is the number whose successive powers organize a numeral expansion or appear in the denominators of a series.","reader_card":"Base two gives powers 2, 4, 8, and so on, while base three-halves gives rational powers. Changing the base can change the arithmetic enough that a theorem at one base says little about another.","reader_rule":"Check which base a series or digit argument uses before transferring a result.","reader_deep":"Integer bases support ordinary positional digit expansions, while rational bases introduce extra denominator structure that can defeat arguments built for whole-number bases."},{"object_id":"term:basis","preferred_label":"basis","reader_preview":"A basis is a smallest collection of building blocks from which everything in a space can be assembled, each thing in exactly one way.","reader_card":"Exhibiting an explicit basis is stronger than knowing one exists, because it lets a computation replace an argument: every element becomes a finite list of coefficients.","reader_rule":"A basis for one space says nothing about a different space until a map between them is given.","reader_deep":"The Erd\u0151s #249 work gives an explicit rational basis for the dyadic sections of Euler's totient, with dimension one at level zero and exactly two to the e plus one at every level e at least one, which is Lean-checked. That is a theorem about the coefficients of the series, not about its irrationality."},{"object_id":"term:binary_expansion","preferred_label":"binary expansion","reader_preview":"A binary expansion is a representation of a number using powers of two and digits limited to zero and one.","reader_card":"For numbers between zero and one, the digits appear after the binary point just as decimal digits appear after a decimal point. A rational number has an eventually repeating expansion, which turns some irrationality questions into questions about whether repetition is possible.","reader_rule":"When an argument uses binary digits, ask whether it concerns the exact infinite expansion or only a computed prefix.","reader_deep":"Finite computation can reveal a long prefix but cannot prove that no repeating tail ever begins, so digit evidence alone does not establish irrationality."},{"object_id":"term:bound","preferred_label":"bound","reader_preview":"A bound is a limit past which a quantity is shown not to go, in one direction or the other.","reader_card":"Bounds are how finite work says something about an infinite question. Checking every case is impossible; proving that any failure must occur beyond a certain point, and then checking everything below it, is not. Much of the computation here has that shape.","reader_rule":"A bound tells you where something cannot be. Whether the remaining region is searchable is a separate question.","reader_deep":"The useful bounds are the sharp ones. A bound that leaves an infinite region unexamined converts a problem into a smaller problem rather than closing it."},{"object_id":"term:bridge","preferred_label":"bridge","reader_preview":"A bridge is a one-round transport that sends a bounded context-and-instruction packet to an external worker and returns its response, without giving that worker independent browsing, iteration, or mutation control.","reader_card":"A bridge carries one prompt packet out to an external worker, such as a separate reasoning model or research run, and carries one response packet back. The worker sees only what the controlling lane chose to send, and the response returns as candidate evidence that the owning lane then validates, imports, or discards. The same packet can be sent to many workers in parallel, which is what makes a bridge useful for research, parallel reasoning, and large population work.","reader_rule":"Treat anything a bridge returns as a worker's answer to a fixed packet, not as a decision the system has already acted on. The surrounding lane owns what was sent and what is done with the reply.","reader_deep":"The orchestration around a bridge, choosing the context, holding the schema, reading results back, validating them, and deciding on any change, belongs to the controller or mission runner, never to the bridge itself."},{"object_id":"term:bundle","preferred_label":"bundle","reader_preview":"A bundle is a component's evidence packaged as files you can open: the source bodies it ran, the fixtures it read, and the result record it wrote, exported together so the run can be inspected without the private system.","reader_card":"A claim is easy to make and hard to check. The bundle is the checkable form: rather than describing what a component does, it ships the actual bodies, inputs, and outputs of a run in one place. That is the difference between reading an account of a check and holding the check. Bundles are metadata-only where the underlying data is private, so what you get is the shape and the verdict, not the substrate.","reader_rule":"When a component claims something, open its bundle. If there is no bundle, you are reading a description rather than evidence.","reader_deep":"Bundles exist because this site publishes components whose full source is not published. Without them a reader would have to trust the summary. With them the trust moves to something narrower and testable: that the exported bytes match the source they claim to come from, which the build checks by digest on every run."},{"object_id":"term:canonicalisation","preferred_label":"canonicalisation","reader_preview":"Canonicalisation, in Terence Tao's account of mathematics in the age of AI, is the late stage after digestion: restating a result in its natural form, with its right proof rather than its first proof, and absorbing it into the standard toolkit.","reader_card":"Tao's pipeline is generation, verification, exposition, publication, digestion, then canonicalisation. Canonicalisation is later than a green check, later than a readable paper, and later than other mathematicians starting to use the result. It is the point at which the community treats the statement as the usual way to say the fact, and the proof as the usual way to see why it is true. This repository cannot award itself that status. A first Lean proof, even a correct one, is typically not the canonical proof.","reader_rule":"Do not read a formal verification, a public PDF, or a problem page as canonicalisation. Those can assist later canonicalisation; they do not complete it.","reader_deep":"Tao separates digestion (other mathematicians take the result in) from canonicalisation (the result becomes the standard object). A project can keep the record that would help both (the failed routes, the exact remaining obligations, one fixed public version) and still be nowhere near either. Claiming canonical status from inside the producing repository would be a category error."},{"object_id":"term:care","preferred_label":"care","reader_preview":"Care is the judgment trait of treating correctness, human stakes, and future upkeep as part of the work, so a result is not merely produced but left findable, trustworthy, refreshable, and safe to extend.","reader_card":"Care is what turns a stake into upkeep. An agent acting with care makes maintenance, continuity, and the comprehension of whoever comes next into visible obligations: it fixes route gaps, preserves provenance, avoids bloating adapters, and leaves explicit closeout records of what was done. It is not a decorative value word. On this system it attaches to concrete actions such as running validators, probing routes, and recording receipts, so the care can be seen in evidence rather than merely asserted.","reader_rule":"When work here is described as done with care, look for the upkeep it left behind: a closeout record, a validator that ran, a preserved trail. Care that leaves no such trace is just a word.","reader_deep":"Care is one register within the broader judgment surface that governs how agents act on the system. It sits under the standards rather than floating free of them, so it can justify extra validation, clarity, and propagation work only when that work is anchored to evidence commands and recorded outcomes."},{"object_id":"term:catalog","preferred_label":"catalog","reader_preview":"On the public architecture page, catalog is the spine step after project: the governed collection of patterns the system is allowed to bind.","reader_card":"The shared path is written project, catalog, pattern, standard, route, work, event, evidence, explanation, assimilation. Catalog is the inventory of bindable patterns, not a reading list. The architecture area also speaks of a pattern catalog behind the Pattern Binding Contract, which is the same object in operational form.","reader_rule":"When the page says catalog, look for the pattern inventory in the spine, not for papers.html and not for the agent skill list.","reader_deep":"A catalog that is generated from source can stay coupled to what the repository actually contains. A hand-maintained list cannot. Binding a pattern that is not in the catalog is how private shorthand leaks into a public claim."},{"object_id":"term:certificate","preferred_label":"certificate","reader_preview":"A certificate is a finite piece of data that makes a fact checkable by direct computation, so a machine can confirm the case rather than follow an argument.","reader_card":"Certificates are how a formal corpus grows to this size. Rather than proving each case by argument, a generator emits the data for each one and the proof checker confirms it. That is why declaration counts run to six figures while the number of distinct human ideas stays small: most of the corpus is checked, not written.","reader_rule":"A certificate establishes its own case. A family of them does not establish the general statement unless something proves the family covers it.","reader_deep":"This is the exact gap between a very large verified corpus and a solved problem, and it is where the remaining work on these problems sits."},{"object_id":"term:challenge_solution_pair","preferred_label":"Challenge/Solution pair","reader_preview":"A Challenge/Solution pair is one Comparator or Palomar entry: a self-contained Lean challenge with the solution that is supposed to meet it, packaged so an external checker can run that pair alone.","reader_card":"A Challenge/Solution pair is the Palomar and Comparator lane, not a Python component card. The pair is the unit you can hand to a stranger without also handing the rest of the private tree: one Lean challenge together with the solution that is supposed to meet it.","reader_rule":"Open the named pair. Do not infer a solution from a neighbouring problem or from a paper precis.","reader_deep":"The point of the pairing is external verifiability: a challenge without a solution is a question, a solution without the challenge is a dump. Together they are what the companion Lean repository can be judged on, problem by problem, without claiming the whole catalogue is solved."},{"object_id":"term:check","preferred_label":"check","reader_preview":"A check is a command you can run that tests one stated claim and writes down what it found.","reader_card":"Checks are the unit of evidence here. Each is a real command rather than a description of one, runs on your machine rather than a server, reads committed inputs, and leaves a result record behind. That is the whole mechanism: the claim says what should be true, the check finds out, and the record is what remains afterwards.","reader_rule":"Run the check. Reading about a check establishes nothing that running it would.","reader_deep":"A check is only as informative as its ability to fail, which is why the negative cases matter more than the passing ones. A check that cannot fail is a formality with the appearance of rigour."},{"object_id":"term:checker","preferred_label":"checker","reader_preview":"A checker is a tool or procedure that evaluates an input against explicit rules and reports whether those rules are satisfied.","reader_card":"Checkers make repeatable judgments over a bounded property such as syntax, links, types, or proof validity. Their result inherits both the strength and the blind spots of the encoded rules.","reader_rule":"Read a checker result together with the rules it implements, the inputs it examined, and its failure behaviour.","reader_deep":"A checker can be fully correct about its own rule set while that rule set remains incomplete for a broader real-world goal."},{"object_id":"term:circuit_break","preferred_label":"circuit-break","reader_preview":"To circuit-break a loop is to interrupt it so its output can no longer keep becoming its input.","reader_card":"A circuit-break is a stop, not a verdict. The interruption has to come from a source that is not already feeding the loop. An automatic check run by the same people or tools still belongs to that loop. Someone outside looking at the public record and naming what they see, including that it is not what it thinks it is, is the kind of stop the word is for.","reader_rule":"Read a circuit-break as an interruption of self-confirmation, not as a substitute for the interrupted process and not as a certificate of value.","reader_deep":"A loop can be technically healthy and still closed. Automatic filters conserve attention; they do not count as an outsider stopping the loop. The useful interruption is a named person inspecting the public record and saying what it is, including that it is not what it thinks it is."},{"object_id":"term:claim","preferred_label":"claim","reader_preview":"A claim is a stated assertion about what something does or establishes, written down separately from the evidence for it so the two can be compared.","reader_card":"Every component here states its claim in one place and ships its check in another. Keeping them apart is what makes the pair checkable: a claim that drifts wider than its evidence becomes visible as a mismatch rather than blending into the surrounding description. The wording of a claim is treated as an artefact in its own right, and edits to it are checked.","reader_rule":"Read the claim and the check together. A claim with no check behind it is a description.","reader_deep":"The failure this guards against is gradual. Nobody rewrites a claim into a lie; wording widens a little at a time until it covers ground the evidence never did, which is why the relationship is registered rather than remembered."},{"object_id":"term:claim_registry","preferred_label":"claim registry","reader_preview":"The claim registry is the reviewed list of what the formal work is asserted to establish, kept separately from the proofs themselves.","reader_card":"A proof corpus does not say what it means. The registry is where each public assertion is written down with its status, which formal statement supports it, and how far it reaches. Keeping it separate is what allows a check that the wording still matches the mathematics after either changes.","reader_rule":"The registry states the claims. The corpus states the theorems. Compare them.","reader_deep":"Most of the corpus is deliberately not registered: checking a declaration does not promote it into a reviewed public claim, and treating those as the same thing would let unreviewed work inherit reviewed standing."},{"object_id":"term:clone","preferred_label":"clone","reader_preview":"To clone a repository is to copy it, with its full history, onto your own machine so you can run and inspect it locally.","reader_card":"Cloning is the first step in every route on this site that produces evidence rather than description. It needs no account and no permission, which is deliberate: the checks are meant to be run by people with no relationship to the author.","reader_rule":"Cloning is how you stop taking this site's word for anything.","reader_deep":"A clone is a copy at a moment in time. It goes stale as the published repository moves, which is why a result from an old local copy can describe a version nobody else can obtain."},{"object_id":"term:closed_route","preferred_label":"closed route","reader_preview":"A closed route is an approach that was tried and shown, by proof or exact computation, not to reach its target.","reader_card":"The long working records list closed routes beside the results. A route is recorded as closed so that the next person does not spend time rediscovering the same dead end, and so that the reason it failed can point to a different approach.","reader_rule":"Read a closed route as evidence about a method, not about the problem.","reader_deep":"A route closed under one representation of the problem can reopen under another, so each closed route names the coordinates it was tried in."},{"object_id":"term:closeout","preferred_label":"closeout","reader_preview":"Closeout is the end of a unit of work: the point at which what changed, what is still open, and what was deliberately not done are recorded, before the work is handed on.","reader_card":"Work that ends without a closeout leaves the next reader to reconstruct it. The closeout records the landed change, the residue, and the boundary of what was attempted, so a session can be picked up without archaeology. It also names what was left alone on purpose, which is otherwise indistinguishable from what was forgotten.","reader_rule":"Read a closeout for what it says was not done. That is usually the part that matters.","reader_deep":"The hardest thing to record honestly is the deliberate omission, because it reads like a gap. Naming it is what separates a bounded piece of work from an unfinished one."},{"object_id":"term:code_map","preferred_label":"Code Map","reader_preview":"Code Map is the derived dependency view of the system's code clusters, generated from the backend rather than hard-coded.","reader_card":"Code Map is the example of the core principle: do not hard-code the picture, derive it from the backend. The clusters come from real dependencies. Surface Atlas sits beside it as the wider contents map. Agents are supposed to reach the same objects through kernel routes rather than by scraping the screenshot.","reader_rule":"Read Code Map as a generated view of dependencies, then open the named cluster's source if you need the files themselves.","reader_deep":"If the map were drawn by hand it would drift from the import graph. Deriving it is how the inspection layer stays honest as the repository grows past one person's head."},{"object_id":"term:coding_agent","preferred_label":"coding agent","reader_preview":"A coding agent here is an AI tool that can edit files and run commands on a machine (Claude Code, Codex, or Cursor), as distinguished from a chat assistant that can only read and quote these pages.","reader_card":"A coding agent can edit files and run commands, so a report can rest on runs that happened. A person can use one to work a bounded mathematical task from a public revision; an agent can clone a repository and execute the stated checks. A browser assistant that only reads these pages can orient, but it establishes nothing about whether the code works. The two routes are separated so that a quotation is not mistaken for a run.","reader_rule":"Ask what the agent actually ran. Commands, exit codes, and files read are evidence; a summary of the website is not.","reader_deep":"The asymmetry is not about model quality. A capable assistant that cannot execute is still limited to restating what the repository says about itself, which is exactly the claim under test."},{"object_id":"term:cofinal","preferred_label":"cofinal","reader_preview":"A property is cofinal when it keeps recurring however far out you go, so past any index you name there is always a later one that has it.","reader_card":"Cofinal is the word that separates a computation from a theorem in this corpus. Checking a property up to index three hundred thousand shows it holds there. It does not show that the property keeps recurring past every bound, and that gap is usually the whole remaining difficulty.","reader_rule":"A finite check never establishes a cofinal statement, however far the check reaches.","reader_deep":"Several papers here isolate the missing step as exactly a cofinal supply: for Erd\u0151s #68 a cofinal step that no proof supplies, for Erd\u0151s #251 cofinal non-integrality for every fixed shift length, and for Erd\u0151s #249 no cofinal supply of certificates. Naming the missing statement exactly is what those papers deliver; proving it is not."},{"object_id":"term:cold_agent","preferred_label":"cold agent","reader_preview":"A cold agent is a capable software agent beginning with no conversation history or memory of earlier work, forced to orient entirely from the durable files and routes available on disk.","reader_card":"A cold agent is a coding or reasoning agent that opens this system fresh, without the context a previous session would have carried, and without any unwritten shared knowledge to fall back on. To act safely it has to read the source, the standards, and the recorded state, and from those work out what each thing does, what opens it, what proves it, and what comes next. The system is designed so that this is possible: orientation comes from the artifacts, not from folklore held in someone's head.","reader_rule":"When a page assumes a cold agent, it is assuming you arrive with no prior context and everything you need is on the page or one click away. Take it at face value: if something cannot be worked out from the written artifacts, that is a gap in the system, not knowledge you were expected to already have.","reader_deep":"The cold-agent frame is a deliberate test the system applies to itself: every artifact should name what it does, what opens it, what proves it, and what comes next, so that an agent with no memory can make the next safe decision without re-deriving the whole system. It is the discipline that lets work continue across a wiped session, a switch of machine, or a handover between people and agents."},{"object_id":"term:cold_clone","preferred_label":"cold clone","reader_preview":"A cold clone is a fresh copy of a repository on a machine that has never seen it, used to check that the published thing works for a stranger rather than only for its author.","reader_card":"Software usually works on the machine that built it. A cold clone removes that advantage: nothing is cached, no local configuration is present, and no undeclared dependency is quietly satisfied. It is the cheapest way to find out whether what was published is what was tested, and it routinely finds gaps that pass on the author's laptop.","reader_rule":"Use a cold-clone result as evidence that the named published revision and declared commands work without the author\u2019s local state; do not extend it to environments or behaviors the probe did not exercise.","reader_deep":"This matters more here than usual, because local checkouts of a published repository drift quickly. A result taken from a stale working copy can describe a version nobody else can obtain, which is a claim about the author's disk rather than about the release."},{"object_id":"term:collaboration","preferred_label":"collaboration","reader_preview":"Collaboration here means contributing to the public record (a proof, a correction, a closed route) with credit kept for the person who did it.","reader_card":"The hero offers a place to collaborate and keep credit for what contributions lead to. Filing a concrete mistake, a failed check, or a Lean patch is treated as that kind of work. A private conversation or an unrecorded suggestion is not yet on the public record.","reader_rule":"If the site promises collaboration, look for the credit route and the artifact the contribution would change.","reader_deep":"Credit is recorded against a contribution that lands, not against interest in the project. The contributor-credit page is the public explanation of that bookkeeping."},{"object_id":"term:combinatorics","preferred_label":"combinatorics","reader_preview":"Combinatorics is the area of mathematics that studies finite or discrete arrangements, selections, counts, and structural patterns.","reader_card":"Combinatorial questions often ask how many configurations exist or whether a particular arrangement must occur. Proofs use counting, extremal arguments, probability, algebra, and graph structure.","reader_rule":"Identify the objects being arranged or counted and which configurations are considered distinct.","reader_deep":"Many problems associated with Erd\u0151s connect combinatorics with number theory, probability, and discrete geometry."},{"object_id":"term:common_sense","preferred_label":"common sense","reader_preview":"Common sense is governed, situation-aware judgment that combines the current state and surrounding rules to infer the right bounded action when no bespoke instruction spells it out.","reader_card":"In this system common sense is the live judgment that connects fixed governance to the case in front of an agent. It reads the axioms, standards, routes, and recorded state, applies ordinary intelligence to the specific situation, checks whether the names and routes it assumes actually resolve, and acts on the cheapest truthful surface available. When the work reveals a reusable gap, such as a missing route, standard, or recorded fact, the lesson is folded back into the artifact that owns it, with the originating case cited. It is the non-brittle part of the system's intelligence: source-backed and testable rather than improvised.","reader_rule":"When a result is described as reached by common sense, read it as a judgment derived from the system's stated rules and live state, not as a guess. It should be traceable back to the routes, facts, and standards it drew on.","reader_deep":"Its defining discipline is that the judgment must stay reusable: routes are expected to resolve through the system's own lookups, facts that can change are projected or queried rather than frozen, and a durable local lesson is propagated up into the owning artifact so a later agent can rely on it."},{"object_id":"term:compact","preferred_label":"compact","reader_preview":"Compact describes material reduced in size or detail while preserving the information needed for its intended use.","reader_card":"A compact view omits lower-priority detail so a reader can scan or choose quickly. It should retain routes to fuller evidence and make its omissions or boundary understandable.","reader_rule":"Treat compact material as a navigation or decision aid and open the deeper source when omitted detail matters.","reader_deep":"Good compression preserves decision-relevant distinctions; merely making text shorter can erase the very boundary a reader needs."},{"object_id":"term:comparator","preferred_label":"Comparator","reader_preview":"Comparator is a tool from the leanprover organisation that checks whether a submitted Lean proof proves a statement declared separately from it.","reader_card":"The separation is what gives it force. The statement to be proved is written down independently, so a proof of some adjacent easier statement is rejected rather than quietly accepted.","reader_rule":"Comparator checks the match between a proof and a declared statement. It is not peer review, not a novelty assessment, and not a claim that any problem is solved.","reader_deep":"In this corpus Comparator is pinned to a fixed commit and run over nineteen propositions, using no axioms beyond propext, Quot.sound and Classical.choice. The same job runs a second fixture built to fail, with one statement altered, and counts as passed only when the checker rejects it by name."},{"object_id":"term:compatibility_route","preferred_label":"compatibility route","reader_preview":"A compatibility route is an old URL or identifier retained solely to redirect readers to the current Plectis surface, preserving access without reviving the retired name as the live one.","reader_card":"When a site is renamed or reorganised, links and identifiers that people already hold would otherwise break. A compatibility route is the machinery that keeps those old paths resolving: a tombstone page, a redirect, an alias, or a manifest entry that takes the visitor to the current location. It names the old route, the new route, the behaviour, and the scope, so the move is visible rather than silent.","reader_rule":"Treat a compatibility route as a forwarding link, not as a second live site. Where it leads is the current surface; the old address it came from is retired.","reader_deep":"A compatibility route is public migration infrastructure: it prevents broken links and keeps the history of earlier addresses inspectable, while making the current identity, Plectis, unmistakable. It does not rewrite the past or imply that the old name is endorsed."},{"object_id":"term:component","preferred_label":"component","reader_preview":"A component is one bounded unit of machinery with a defined job, explicit inputs and outputs, and a limit on what it may claim.","reader_card":"Each public component has its own card: it names the unit, states its job, gives the evidence line that backs it, links to its source, and marks where its scope stops. The catalogue lists them so each component can be opened and inspected on its own.","reader_rule":"Take a component as one bounded unit doing one job. On its card, read the evidence line and the scope limit to see what it is responsible for and where that responsibility ends.","reader_deep":"Public pages say component; some source files still say organ. The public word lets the object model be read without the internal working vocabulary. The source registry, atlas, object map, and evidence profiles decide what each component is allowed to claim."},{"object_id":"term:compression","preferred_label":"compression","reader_preview":"Compression is the governed reduction of larger source material into a smaller navigable view that records what it preserves, what it omits, which profile and band shaped it, and how to reopen the authoritative source.","reader_card":"In this system compression is governed, not casual. Every compact summary is produced under a declared profile that fixes how much detail a given band carries, so a one-line entry, a card, and a full module are different rungs of the same material rather than arbitrary trims. Each row records what was preserved and what was omitted, and keeps a path back to the source it stands for, so a reader can always open the fuller layer beneath any summary.","reader_rule":"Treat a compressed row as a faithful but partial view: it carries an omission record and a route back to source, so when detail matters, follow that route rather than relying on the short form alone.","reader_deep":"Compression here is the write side of navigation: standards declare the readable shapes, profiles declare the band contracts, and the resulting summaries are generated surfaces that let later readers spend attention deliberately. Where no profile governs a row, the system records a profile gap instead of inventing an ad hoc summary."},{"object_id":"term:compression_profile","preferred_label":"compression profile","reader_preview":"A compression profile is the repeatable rulebook for shortening one artifact kind, specifying its depth bands, mandatory content, allowed omissions, and forbidden collapses.","reader_card":"Each profile is tied to a specific artifact kind, such as a voice record, a relational map, a peer-knowledge note, or a term definition. It names the bands the artifact can be projected into, the schema each band must follow, the evidence policy, and the validation check, so that a cheap automated worker, a larger model, and a person all shorten the same artifact into the same shape. Four profiles run today, covering voice records, relational maps, peer-knowledge notes, and vocabulary terms.","reader_rule":"When a piece of content here is shown at varying lengths, the compression profile is what fixed which details had to survive and which were allowed to fall away; read it to know what a short form is guaranteed to still carry.","reader_deep":"Profiles are registered together so that compression behaves as a governed primitive: the stated design target is that every artifact kind eventually names its own profile, so that any worker producing a band yields an identical row shape."},{"object_id":"term:computer_science","preferred_label":"computer science","reader_preview":"Computer science, abbreviated CS, is the academic and engineering study of computation, programs, data, and the systems that run them.","reader_card":"CS people are invited alongside mathematicians and lay users of coding agents: not to own the theorems, but to help upgrade architecture, checks, and the public record so other people can run and inspect the work. CS here is the ordinary discipline. It is not a claim that this site is a computer-science department, and it is not a ranking of intelligence.","reader_rule":"Read CS as the field that builds and studies programs. A CS contribution is software, architecture, or tooling, unless the same person also supplies a mathematical result.","reader_deep":"The abbreviation is search-friendly because the landing says CS rather than spelling the field every time. Hover should still open the discipline, not a local product name. Formal verification is one CS-adjacent method used here; it does not exhaust computer science, and computer science does not exhaust the mathematics."},{"object_id":"term:concept","preferred_label":"concept","reader_preview":"A concept is a curated vocabulary entry that gives a recurring object, abstraction, or class of meaning one stable identifier and definition for other system records to reference consistently.","reader_card":"Concepts are the system's defined nouns, as distinct from its procedures. Each one fixes a single object or idea, gives it a stable identifier and a short description, links to where it is defined in the source, and connects to the mechanisms and principles that use it. Together they form the shared ontology that mechanisms act on and principles reason about, so that a recurring object keeps one settled meaning across the whole system.","reader_rule":"Treat a concept as the settled meaning of one named object. When something else on the site refers to it, read the concept to learn exactly what that object is, not how to do anything with it.","reader_deep":"A concept is curated and source-linked rather than improvised, which is how the system keeps its vocabulary from drifting as it grows. It names a thing; the steps that act on that thing live in mechanisms and skills, not in the concept itself."},{"object_id":"term:conditional_reduction","preferred_label":"conditional reduction","reader_preview":"A conditional reduction proves that one statement would follow from another that is not itself proved, converting a problem into a different problem rather than solving it.","reader_card":"Reductions are real work and are routinely mistaken for progress towards a solution. What a reduction establishes is that the target now rests on a named, unproved hypothesis; whether that is easier than the original is a separate judgement nobody has made. The registry records these separately from unconditional results for that reason.","reader_rule":"For a conditional result, find the hypothesis it rests on. Nothing is settled until that is proved.","reader_deep":"The honest accounting is that a reduction moves the difficulty and reports where it went. Counting reductions alongside proofs would make a portfolio of open problems look like a portfolio of results."},{"object_id":"term:conditional_theorem","preferred_label":"conditional theorem","reader_preview":"A conditional theorem proves that a conclusion follows from a hypothesis that has not itself been proved.","reader_card":"The proof of the implication can be completely rigorous, and machine-checked, while the conclusion remains unavailable because nobody has established the hypothesis. Reading only the conclusion of a conditional theorem is the commonest way to misread this corpus.","reader_rule":"Always read the antecedent. A conditional theorem asserts nothing about its conclusion until the hypothesis is proved.","reader_deep":"The Lean repository holds 259 conditional theorems. Their hypotheses are lifted out as 101 standalone propositions, 23 of them substantial, with 144 implications between those proved in Lean. Seventeen of the 23 turn out to be equal in strength to the open problem they were meant to approach, so that list is one problem restated many times."},{"object_id":"term:conjecture","preferred_label":"conjecture","reader_preview":"A conjecture is a precise mathematical statement believed or suspected to be true but not yet proved or disproved.","reader_card":"Conjectures are informed research targets, often supported by examples, computation, analogy, or partial theorems. Those supports do not change the conjecture's unproved status.","reader_rule":"Treat a conjecture as an open claim until a valid proof or counterexample settles it.","reader_deep":"A conjecture may be reformulated into equivalent statements or divided into cases without becoming solved, and a catalogue can update its status as the literature changes."},{"object_id":"term:context_packet","preferred_label":"context packet","reader_preview":"A context packet is the bounded bundle of routes and material an agent uses to reach the same objects a human reaches through the visual front end.","reader_card":"Agents reach the same objects through kernel routes and context packets. The point is parity: the inspection layer is not only screens. A packet that dumps the whole repository defeats the bound. A packet that omits the source route leaves the agent quoting the page.","reader_rule":"When a sentence mentions a context packet, expect a bounded set of routes, not an unbounded paste of the site.","reader_deep":"The public AI packets on this site (the concise guide, the reviewer hologram, the downloadable JSON maps) are the published relatives of that idea. They are still projections. Source files remain authority."},{"object_id":"term:contradiction","preferred_label":"contradiction","reader_preview":"On the maths pages here, a contradiction is an impossible conclusion (a statement and its negation both holding) derived from a set of assumptions.","reader_card":"In a proof by contradiction, the target is established by assuming its opposite and showing that assumption forces an impossibility. The proof is only as strong as the chain from the assumption to the contradiction.","reader_rule":"Identify the assumption being rejected and the exact impossible statement it produces.","reader_deep":"Irrationality proofs commonly assume a series is rational, extract a finite denominator or repeating pattern, and then show the required arithmetic structure cannot exist."},{"object_id":"term:contribute","preferred_label":"contribute","reader_preview":"To contribute is to give work the project can inspect and, if accepted, keep: a result, a correction, a route, or a better way to continue.","reader_card":"Anyone can contribute; credit is recorded only for accepted work, and it is never awarded to a model. Mathematicians can give insight and direction; lay people with coding agents can mine that direction; CS people can upgrade architecture; other backgrounds can help in whatever way actually lands. Contributing is the act. Contributor credit is the named residue after acceptance.","reader_rule":"Treat a contribution as offered work until it is accepted under the written credit policy. An issue or pull request is delivery history, not yet the credit line.","reader_deep":"The public repositories are the place contributions go. The site cannot accept work by hover or by a kind message. If you solve a result, the result is yours; the project asks for no priority on it. The hope stated on the page is only that this system helped facilitate it."},{"object_id":"term:contributor_credit","preferred_label":"contributor credit","reader_preview":"Contributor credit is the durable named record of who supplied accepted work, including credit for what that work then leads to.","reader_card":"The credit policy in the Lean repository keeps solvers, collaborators, prior work, tools and infrastructure distinct. Credit follows accepted work: an issue or pull request is delivery history, not yet the credit line. If you solve a problem, the result and the credit are yours and your collaborators'. If the project materially helped, the request is that you cite the release and say so. Credit is not automatic paper coauthorship. Following the same line Tao takes from the Leiden declaration: credit and responsibility stay with humans; a model can be disclosed as a tool, it cannot be listed as an author.","reader_rule":"Expect credit that names your accepted contribution as what it is, including later results it leads to; do not expect coauthorship by default, and do not expect a model to receive the credit line.","reader_deep":"Tool use is disclosed separately from credit, so a contribution made with an AI agent is credited to the person who directed and stands behind it. Where a prior source cannot be named cleanly, the honest move is to say so rather than invent an attribution. Acceptance of a credit receipt is not a claim of correctness, novelty, or peer review."},{"object_id":"term:convergence","preferred_label":"convergence","reader_preview":"Convergence means the partial sums of an infinite series approach one fixed finite value as more terms are included.","reader_card":"A convergent series has a well-defined sum even though no finite stage contains every term. Convergence says that the value exists; it does not say whether that value is rational, algebraic, transcendental, or easy to compute.","reader_rule":"Separate the question of whether a series converges from the question of what kind of number its limit is.","reader_deep":"Proofs control the tail left after a partial sum to show that all later sums remain arbitrarily close to one value, replacing an informal picture of settling down with a quantified statement."},{"object_id":"term:coprime","preferred_label":"coprime","reader_preview":"Two integers are coprime when their greatest common divisor is one, meaning they share no prime factor.","reader_card":"Coprime does not require either number to be prime; 8 and 15 are coprime even though both are composite. The condition is what lets modular inverses and multiplicative orders exist.","reader_rule":"Check coprimality with respect to the exact pair of integers named in the result.","reader_deep":"When a base is coprime to a denominator, repeated multiplication by that base cycles among invertible residue classes rather than collapsing to zero."},{"object_id":"term:corpus","preferred_label":"corpus","reader_preview":"A corpus is a fixed body of material treated as one collection, so results can be quoted against a known set rather than an open-ended one.","reader_card":"In Plectis, corpus means an enumerated body against which a count, proof, or check is made: for example, the published PDFs, Lean declarations, or another manifest-backed collection. Fixing membership makes the claim reproducible; a result over one corpus does not extend to future files or excluded material.","reader_rule":"Locate the manifest, revision, or membership rule before reading a corpus-wide count or coverage claim.","reader_deep":"Corpus is a measurement boundary, not a claim of representativeness or completeness. Plectis reports both raw declaration counts and deduplicated statement counts because the same fixed corpus can support different legitimate units."},{"object_id":"term:counterexample","preferred_label":"counterexample","reader_preview":"A counterexample is a single case that fails a general statement, which is enough to disprove it.","reader_card":"The asymmetry is total: proving a general claim requires covering every case, while refuting it requires exactly one. Several results here work by narrowing what a surviving counterexample would have to look like, which is progress even when none is found, because each constraint shrinks where one could hide.","reader_rule":"A constraint on counterexamples is a real result even when no counterexample exists.","reader_deep":"Narrowing is the usual shape of progress on a problem that resists both proof and refutation: the space of possible failures gets smaller without closing."},{"object_id":"term:critic_posture","preferred_label":"critic posture","reader_preview":"Critic posture is a repeatable way of testing an emerging idea by asking which abstraction is weak, which route has no answer, and which governing standard is missing, rather than offering a one-off objection.","reader_card":"Critic posture is one of the system's named judgment traits, used when people and an agent are working an idea out together. Instead of agreeing and moving on, it pauses to test the idea: it names the candidate term being introduced, checks whether the system can actually route to it, and asks what a newcomer would miss. When a criticism holds up under that test, it is not left as a passing remark; it is turned into something durable, such as a refined definition, a recorded rule, or a standard.","reader_rule":"When you meet critic posture on this site, read it as the discipline of pressing on an idea's weak points and then recording what survives, not as a separate reviewer or a verdict on the whole system.","reader_deep":"Its characteristic move is the failure question: what would a fresh reader miss here, which route currently fails, which standard is absent, or which single case is being treated as a general rule. A criticism that stays stable is then converted into a governed artifact rather than remaining a chat objection."},{"object_id":"term:declaration","preferred_label":"declaration","reader_preview":"A declaration is one named item in a proof library: a single theorem, definition or structure, as written in the source.","reader_card":"It is the unit the Lean corpus is counted in. Declarations are not equally interesting: one may be a substantial theorem and the next a mechanical step generated in bulk, and a count that does not separate them will mislead. That is why the corpus is reported both as raw declarations and as the far smaller number of distinct statements they reduce to.","reader_rule":"A declaration count measures size, not content. Ask how many distinct statements it reduces to.","reader_deep":"Counting declarations is the easiest metric to inflate and the least informative to inflate, because generated steps are cheap. The interesting figure is what survives deduplication."},{"object_id":"term:demo_take","preferred_label":"Demo Take","reader_preview":"Demo Take is the integrated capture console that records screen, transcript, and frame stills so demos can be compiled without hand editing.","reader_card":"The Demo Take Console is the capture surface walkthroughs are recorded on. It is a bit like OBS, but integrated into the system, using Whisper for transcripts and timestamps. The published demo videos are the output of that capture, not a separate edited film.","reader_rule":"When a page refers to Demo Take, treat it as the capture path for a demo, then watch the published video or read its transcript.","reader_deep":"Integrating capture is an inspection move: the recording is supposed to stay tied to the same system being shown, so a claim in the video can be walked back to a file. It does not make the video itself evidence that a check passed."},{"object_id":"term:denominator","preferred_label":"denominator","reader_preview":"The denominator is the bottom number of a fraction, the one you are dividing by.","reader_card":"If an infinite sum turns out to be rational, it has an exact denominator, and that single number constrains everything about it. Several results here work by showing what the denominator would have to be and then showing no such number can exist, which is why so much of the argument is about denominators rather than about the sum.","reader_rule":"A statement about a possible denominator is a statement about whether the value can be rational at all.","reader_deep":"The denominator is the handle rationality gives you. It is the reason assuming rationality is productive: it converts an infinite object into a finite one you can reason about."},{"object_id":"term:derivation","preferred_label":"derivation","reader_preview":"A derivation is an ordered sequence of valid transformations or inferences that obtains a result from stated starting material.","reader_card":"Derivations occur in mathematics, logic, data processing, and generated systems. Each step should follow an allowed rule so the result can be traced back to its premises or inputs.","reader_rule":"Check a derivation by examining its starting assumptions, transformation rules, intermediate steps, and final result.","reader_deep":"A derivation explains how a result follows; it does not by itself show that the starting assumptions accurately describe the intended real-world subject."},{"object_id":"term:derivation_certificate","preferred_label":"derivation certificate","reader_preview":"A derivation certificate is the finite checkable artifact that lets an independent checker recompute a claim from its named basis.","reader_card":"Doctrine AX-1 asks for a named basis, a derivation certificate, and a small checker. The certificate is what the checker reads. Without it, a fluent claim has nothing to recompute. On the maths side, a Lean proof is one such artifact; on the software side it may be a receipt or a fixture output.","reader_rule":"Ask what artifact a checker would recompute. If none is named, the claim is not yet certified.","reader_deep":"Producer and certificate are split on purpose. The person or model who wrote the certificate does not get to accept it. The named basis is the input; the certificate is the trace; the checker is the verdict."},{"object_id":"term:diebold_mariano_test","preferred_label":"Diebold-Mariano test","reader_preview":"The Diebold-Mariano test compares the forecast errors of two competing methods and asks whether the difference between them is larger than chance would produce.","reader_card":"It is the standard first check on a claim that one forecasting method beats another. It compares two named methods on the same data, so it does not license a claim about methods it did not see.","reader_rule":"A significant result compares the two methods tested, on the sample tested. It is not a general ranking.","reader_deep":"Forecast-skill claims in this corpus are put through Diebold-Mariano, Hansen's superior predictive ability test, and a stationary bootstrap, over synthetic market fixtures. Synthetic fixtures mean the check establishes that the statistical machinery runs and refuses as specified, not that any live market was predicted."},{"object_id":"term:digest","preferred_label":"digest","reader_preview":"A digest is a short fingerprint computed from a file's contents, used to check that two copies are byte-for-byte identical.","reader_card":"Digests are how this site checks that a published copy really matches its source: the deploy compares the digest of every paper against the public repository before it will publish, and refuses if any differ. A digest proves sameness cheaply and exactly, which is worth more than a promise that nothing changed.","reader_rule":"A matching digest proves the bytes are identical. It proves nothing about whether they are right.","reader_deep":"This is the precise limit of digest-based evidence, and the reason components carry source bodies rather than only hashes: identical bytes can still be the wrong bytes."},{"object_id":"term:digestion","preferred_label":"digestion","reader_preview":"Digestion, in Terence Tao's account of mathematics in the age of AI, is the stage after a result has been generated, verified, and clearly written: other mathematicians take it in and start using it in their own work.","reader_card":"Tao's pipeline is generation, verification, exposition, publication, digestion, then canonicalisation. Those are different jobs, in that order. This project names digestion first, ahead of formal verification. Verification asks whether a proof is correct; exposition asks whether it can be communicated; publication and acceptance ask whether the community will take it; digestion asks whether mathematicians actually absorb it and use it. He argues that authors can help digestion by recording the insights, the false starts, and the stories from the working period. Polishing every step until it looks equally easy is not the help he describes. A Lean-checked proof that nobody can restate, situate, or continue from has not been digested. Keeping failed routes, exact remaining obligations, and one fixed public version is how this project tries to assist that process. It cannot complete it. Acceptance and digestion are slow, human, and external; a green check here is neither.","reader_rule":"Read digestion as community uptake. A clear explanation can assist it. It does not prove that digestion has happened.","reader_deep":"Tao's expert-talk test is the practical bar: if the people responsible for a result cannot give a clear, correctly attributed, expert-level account of it, the result is incomplete even when formally verified. Canonicalisation, restating the result in its natural form, with its right proof rather than its first proof, and absorbing it into the standard toolkit, is later still, and is never something this repository can award itself."},{"object_id":"term:discrete_mathematics","preferred_label":"discrete mathematics","reader_preview":"Discrete mathematics studies structures made of distinct, separately countable objects rather than continuously varying quantities.","reader_card":"Its subjects include combinatorics, graph theory, logic, algorithms, and many parts of number theory. Discrete does not mean small: an infinite set can still have discrete structure.","reader_rule":"Look for individually distinguishable objects and exact relations rather than continuous change.","reader_deep":"Computer science and formal proof frequently use discrete mathematics because symbolic states and program steps can be represented as countable structures."},{"object_id":"term:divergence","preferred_label":"divergence","reader_preview":"Divergence means that a sequence, series, or limiting process does not approach a finite limit in the stated sense.","reader_card":"Divergence is relative to a specified mode of convergence: values may grow without bound, oscillate, or fail to settle for another reason. The reason matters to later arguments.","reader_rule":"Identify the object and definition of convergence before reading a divergence claim.","reader_deep":"A divergent series can still have meaningful partial sums, transformed expressions, or alternative summation methods, but those are not ordinary convergence."},{"object_id":"term:divisor","preferred_label":"divisor","reader_preview":"An integer divisor of an integer n is an integer d for which n equals d times another integer.","reader_card":"The notation d \u2223 n means that d divides n with no remainder. Divisibility is an exact integer relation, not an approximate numerical one.","reader_rule":"Check the domain and whether zero or negative divisors are included by the convention in use.","reader_deep":"Divisors organize factorisation, primality, congruences, and multiplicative functions such as Euler's totient. Distinguishing positive, negative, proper, and unit divisors is therefore a theorem-level convention, not cosmetic notation."},{"object_id":"term:doctrine","preferred_label":"doctrine","reader_preview":"Doctrine is the curated, searchable layer of principles, concepts, mechanisms, routes, and interpretations that explains why the system is shaped as it is, distinct from the code that implements those ideas.","reader_card":"Doctrine is not one document but a network of governed artifacts: principles, anti-patterns, concepts, mechanisms, standards, and recorded lessons, each given a name and a place. It captures the operating theory of the system, the rules it holds itself to and the ideas it is built around, so that the reasoning behind a design is written down rather than left implicit. Each piece can be opened on its own and traced back to the source it came from.","reader_rule":"Treat a doctrine entry as a statement of intent and principle, not as proof that the running code obeys it. It tells you what the system means to do and why; the matching component or check is where that intent is actually carried out.","reader_deep":"Doctrine entries come in kinds, including axioms the system takes as given, principles it follows, anti-patterns it forbids, and concepts and mechanisms it names; together they form the meaning lattice the rest of the system is read against."},{"object_id":"term:doi","preferred_label":"DOI","reader_preview":"A DOI is a permanent identifier that a publisher or repository mints for a document, so citations to it keep resolving after the document moves.","reader_card":"The identifier resolves through a registration agency rather than through whatever web address a document currently sits at, which is the whole point: a publisher can reorganise its site, or a repository can move, and existing citations keep working. Getting one requires depositing the document somewhere that is a member of such an agency and undertakes to keep resolving it.","reader_rule":"A DOI records that a document was deposited and will keep resolving. It says nothing about whether the document was reviewed, whether its results are correct, or whether anyone read it.","reader_deep":"None of the papers here carries one, because none is deposited anywhere that mints them, and that is stated rather than left for a reader to notice. A citation pins a manuscript by its source commit instead, which is a stronger identity in one respect, since the commit fixes the exact bytes cited, and weaker in another, since it depends on the repository remaining reachable."},{"object_id":"term:domain_expert","preferred_label":"domain expert","reader_preview":"A domain expert here is someone with specialised field judgement the public work still needs, listed beside mathematicians and lay people on the landing.","reader_card":"The hero groups domain experts with mathematicians and lay people as people who can collaborate and keep credit. The phrase covers specialised knowledge the Erd\u0151s-problem or software work may need without requiring a university title.","reader_rule":"Read domain expert as an invited contributor role, then look at the named problem or component for what judgement is being asked.","reader_deep":"Expert judgement does not replace Lean checks or recorded evidence. It can name the interesting statement, catch a mismatch between prose and formalisation, or point at a closed route the author missed."},{"object_id":"term:drift","preferred_label":"drift","reader_preview":"Drift is the gap that opens when a generated surface stops matching the source it was generated from, so a page, count, or copy keeps reporting something that is no longer true.","reader_card":"Drift is quiet by construction. Nothing errors; a stale page simply carries an old number with the same confidence as a current one, and a reader has no way to tell. This is why so much of the site is a projection with a build that fails when its output no longer matches its source. Drift caught by a build is a broken build; drift caught by a reader is a broken claim.","reader_rule":"If two surfaces disagree, suspect drift and find the source authority before believing either.","reader_deep":"The dangerous case is a hand-edited copy that is more current than its source, because regenerating destroys the correction and looks like a fix. Keeping authorship in one place is what makes drift recoverable."},{"object_id":"term:drilldown","preferred_label":"drilldown","reader_preview":"A drilldown is a route from a summary or aggregate view into more specific underlying detail.","reader_card":"Plectis keeps the first view deliberately small, then lets the selected object open through progressively richer bands: a glossary definition can open its contextual card and scope, an option row can open its source-backed card, and a paper-module summary can open the module itself. A valid drilldown preserves the object\u2019s stable identity and moves closer to its evidence or owner; it does not merely send the reader to another page mentioning the same words.","reader_rule":"Use a drilldown when the summary no longer answers the question, and verify that the deeper surface refers to the same identified object.","reader_deep":"Drilldown is a depth transition, not an authority promotion. The summary remains a projection, while the destination contributes whatever authority its own contract grants. Stable ids and source references are therefore part of the operation: without them, a deeper-looking page may silently change subjects."},{"object_id":"term:dyadic","preferred_label":"dyadic","reader_preview":"Dyadic means built from powers of two.","reader_card":"Several of these series divide their terms by successive powers of two, which is the same as writing the value in binary. That connection is what makes the binary digits of the sum the object of study: a question about whether the value is a fraction becomes a question about whether its digits eventually repeat.","reader_rule":"Dyadic questions are usually questions about binary digits.","reader_deep":"Powers of two are the smallest interesting base, which makes these the easiest cases to state and often the only ones anyone can prove anything about."},{"object_id":"term:endogenous","preferred_label":"endogenous","reader_preview":"Endogenous means arising from inside the system or process under discussion, rather than from an independent outside source.","reader_card":"An endogenous judgement can be careful and still not settle what the work is. If the same people and tools generate the output, check it, and then agree with it, that agreement is still from inside the process. A machine refusing a bad formal step is useful; it is not an independent outside source.","reader_rule":"When a claim is endogenous, name the loop that produced it before treating it as independent confirmation.","reader_deep":"Endogenous is ordinary English here, not a claim about economics or biology. A year of solo work with AI is an endogenous setting even when every Lean check is green. Outside inspection is what would make a judgement exogenous."},{"object_id":"term:engine_room","preferred_label":"engine room","reader_preview":"Engine room is the label for the components that run the system itself rather than serve a reader: the routers, gates, drift checks and queues the machinery uses on its own operation.","reader_card":"Most components here do something for a user. Engine-room components do something for the system, which is why they read as unusually inward-facing. They are published for the same reason as the rest, that the claim is checkable, but their subject is the system's own behaviour and their fixtures are drawn from its own runs.","reader_rule":"Read an engine-room component as a statement about the system's internal handling, not about anything it produces for a reader.","reader_deep":"The engine room is where self-referential failure lives. A gate that checks the system's own output can pass because it stopped checking, and nothing downstream would notice. That is why these components carry negative cases as a rule rather than as an option."},{"object_id":"term:entry","preferred_label":"entry","reader_preview":"An entry is a supported starting route or one selectable item in a governed collection.","reader_card":"Plectis uses entry in two related but distinct ways. A collection entry is one stable selectable row, such as a glossary term or component. A system entry is a supported first-contact surface that accepts a task or question and returns a bounded next route, such as the kernel\u2019s task-conditioned entry packet. Both uses promise identity and direction: the reader should know what object or route was entered and where its deeper source lives.","reader_rule":"First identify whether entry means a collection row or a first-contact route. In either case, rely on it only when it names the object or task and provides a supported path to deeper source-backed material.","reader_deep":"The local distinction is support. Many files can be opened, but only an entry is deliberately designed to orient a cold reader or process without prior knowledge. A good entry spends little context, preserves the selected identity, and hands off to a drilldown rather than trying to contain the whole system."},{"object_id":"term:epistemic_control_plane","preferred_label":"epistemic control plane","reader_preview":"An epistemic control plane is a proposed runtime form of the Workingness Instrument that connects the system's current beliefs and acknowledged unknowns to actions that are allowed, blocked, or routed for review.","reader_card":"It works as a chain: evidence sets the status of a claim, the status of that claim sets which actions are permitted, and any unresolved uncertainty stops a change from being made. It would draw together the recorded checks, judgments, appeals, and projection comparisons into claim rows and matching action boundaries, so that the system only acts where its own beliefs are backed and holds back where they are not. Today it exists as a stated target and an alias; the written-up artifact behind it is the Workingness Instrument paper module and a candidate standard.","reader_rule":"Treat it as a planned bridge from self-observation to safe action, not a shipped runtime: where you meet it, expect a description of how belief is meant to gate action, with the live writer still to be built.","reader_deep":"The core rule it encodes is that changing evidence changes a claim's status, changing status changes the allowed actions, and an open question blocks any mutation until it is settled."},{"object_id":"term:equality","preferred_label":"equality","reader_preview":"An equality is a statement that two expressions denote the same value or object under the stated assumptions.","reader_card":"Equality is exact and symmetric: if a = b, either side can replace the other where the expression is defined. Numerical approximations use a different relation.","reader_rule":"Check the domain, assumptions, and whether the symbol denotes exact equality or an approximation.","reader_deep":"Proofs of equality may proceed by calculation, algebraic rewriting, extensionality, or showing both sides satisfy a unique characterization."},{"object_id":"term:equation","preferred_label":"equation","reader_preview":"An equation is an equality containing one or more unknowns whose allowed values are to be found or constrained.","reader_card":"In the Plectis papers, equations are exact mathematical constraints, not formatted approximations. They may define a quantity, express a recurrence, or state the equality from which a proof derives a denominator or integrality condition; the permitted values depend on the declared number system.","reader_rule":"Read the variables, domain, hypotheses, and equivalence of each transformation before treating a displayed equation as solved.","reader_deep":"A Lean theorem can machine-check an equation and the transformations applied to it, but the surrounding prose still has to identify why that formal equality represents the intended Erd\u0151s problem."},{"object_id":"term:equivalence","preferred_label":"equivalence","reader_preview":"An equivalence is a proved two-way relationship in which each of two statements implies the other under the same stated hypotheses.","reader_card":"Equivalent statements may look very different while carrying exactly the same truth conditions. Proving only one direction gives an implication or reduction, not an equivalence.","reader_rule":"For an equivalence, locate both directions and verify that their hypotheses and domains match.","reader_deep":"Equivalences are valuable because they let a problem move into a representation with better tools without changing what would count as a solution."},{"object_id":"term:erdos","preferred_label":"Paul Erd\u0151s","reader_preview":"Paul Erd\u0151s is the mathematician whose questions this site treats as numbered problems, via the independently maintained Erd\u0151s Problems database.","reader_card":"The numbered Erd\u0151s problems discussed here are questions associated with Erd\u0151s and indexed by the independently maintained Erd\u0151s Problems database. A label such as \u201cErd\u0151s #249\u201d identifies that database entry; it is not a claim that Erd\u0151s assigned the number himself.","reader_rule":"Read \u201cErd\u0151s #n\u201d as a database identifier for a mathematical problem associated with Paul Erd\u0151s.","reader_deep":"Erd\u0151s worked extensively through collaboration and left a large body of questions that continue to organize research; the site covers only the explicitly listed problems and makes no claim about the rest."},{"object_id":"term:erdos_problem","preferred_label":"Erd\u0151s problem","reader_preview":"An Erd\u0151s problem is a question Paul Erd\u0151s posed or collected, indexed by number in the Erd\u0151s Problems database; Erd\u0151s #249 is that database's entry 249.","reader_card":"The eight problems on this site are all recorded there as open. The database supplies the numbering and the current reported status; the exact statement comes from Erd\u0151s's own papers, which each problem note cites. Several of the eight ask whether one particular infinite sum is an irrational number.","reader_rule":"Use the number to find the problem; use the problem note for the exact statement, its sources and what has been proved.","reader_deep":"Open status is the database maintainer's present assessment and may omit relevant literature, so each problem note also records the prior work it knows of."},{"object_id":"term:evaluator","preferred_label":"evaluator","reader_preview":"An evaluator is a read-only component that measures something and reports, without changing what it measured.","reader_card":"Separating measurement from repair matters because a tool that both judges and fixes can quietly redefine the standard it is judging against. An evaluator emits a report and stops, leaving the decision about what to do with it outside itself.","reader_rule":"An evaluator reports. If a tool also repairs what it measures, ask what its measurement means.","reader_deep":"The independence is what makes an evaluator's number worth reading. A measurement produced by the thing being measured is not a measurement."},{"object_id":"term:evidence","preferred_label":"evidence","reader_preview":"Evidence is a source, observation, check result, or record that supports a specific claim within a stated scope and can be inspected independently of that claim.","reader_card":"Evidence is the backing under a statement, not the statement itself. Its strength depends on what produced it, whether the record is current, and whether its scope actually matches the claim being made.","reader_rule":"Ask which exact claim the evidence supports, how it was produced, and where its coverage stops.","reader_deep":"The same artifact can be strong evidence for one narrow assertion and irrelevant to a broader one; a passing fixture run may establish that a validator handles those fixtures while saying nothing about real-world performance."},{"object_id":"term:evidence_band","preferred_label":"evidence band","reader_preview":"An evidence band is the tag on a statement in the long working records saying what kind of support it has: Lean, Cert, Math, Cited or Open.","reader_card":"The bands are never blurred: a statement with one band is not described in the language of another. A second tag gives the scale at which a parameter-indexed statement holds: at listed values, below an explicit bound, for arbitrarily large parameters, or for all of them.","reader_rule":"Read the band before the statement. It tells you what would have to be re-checked and by what means.","reader_deep":"Sorting by band and scale keeps the unresolved endpoint visible: the open problems each depend on a statement at a scale the checked results have not reached. The five bands and four scales are the vocabulary the whole record is written in, so a reader who learns them once can read every problem page the same way."},{"object_id":"term:evidence_class","preferred_label":"evidence class","reader_preview":"An evidence class names the kind of support behind a result, without treating that support as proof of every claim drawn from it.","reader_card":"On a component card the Evidence chips name the class; the evidence page sorts results by the same vocabulary. The classes distinguish a link to a source row, a fixture that passed, a tool that ran locally, and a recorded review. Two results can both be supported while resting on different kinds of evidence, and each class carries its own ceiling on what may be claimed from it.","reader_rule":"Check which evidence class a result carries. Read it as the kind of support, then read the neighbouring scope limit for what that support does not establish.","reader_deep":"The governing list of classes is kept in the source slice and the site builder's claim-ceiling projection. A class is a fixed vocabulary entry rather than a free-form label."},{"object_id":"term:exact_rational_arithmetic","preferred_label":"exact rational arithmetic","reader_preview":"Exact rational arithmetic works with fractions as pairs of whole numbers rather than as decimal approximations, so rounding error cannot enter.","reader_card":"It is slower than floating-point arithmetic and answers a different question. A floating-point computation shows a value is close to something; an exact rational computation shows it is that something, for the case computed.","reader_rule":"Exactness removes rounding error from a finite computation. It does not extend that computation to cases it did not run.","reader_deep":"One Plectis component recomputes an Erd\u0151s certificate in exact rational arithmetic and compares the result against the checked-in value. A pass establishes that the recorded certificate is the one the arithmetic produces, for that certificate."},{"object_id":"term:executable_cross_section","preferred_label":"executable cross-section","reader_preview":"An executable cross-section is a bounded public slice of a larger system whose included components, checks, source, and result records can be run and inspected independently.","reader_card":"An executable cross-section is the public middle ground between a description and a complete system release. Plectis selects mechanisms whose code, fixtures, validation commands, source links, and result records can stand together and be rerun by a cold reader. That makes the selected behavior inspectable rather than merely narrated. The selection remains a boundary: omitted mechanisms, private inputs, live orchestration, and the behavior of the larger working system do not inherit evidence from the runnable slice.","reader_rule":"Judge the executable cross-section by the components and records it actually publishes; do not infer that it represents or proves the unpublished remainder.","reader_deep":"The important distinction is not size but evidential closure. A cross-section is executable only when the included source, inputs, commands, expected negatives, and durable records are sufficient to inspect the selected mechanism without private context. It is still a projection because the act of selecting that closed slice does not establish equivalence with everything outside it."},{"object_id":"term:exogenous","preferred_label":"exogenous","reader_preview":"Exogenous means arising from outside the system or process under discussion, rather than from inside it.","reader_card":"Outside is not automatically correct. It names independence: a judgement whose source is not the process being judged. Automatic filters run by the same author or tools are not exogenous, even when they refuse a bad step. A named person inspecting the public record, including to say 'this is not that', would be.","reader_rule":"Treat a judgement as exogenous only when its source sits outside the process being judged. Automatic filters run by the author are not exogenous.","reader_deep":"Exogenous keeps its ordinary meaning. No technical claim about economics or biology is intended. The compound 'exogenous truth' is a specific ask for an independent judgement; this row defines the adjective that ask uses."},{"object_id":"term:exogenous_truth","preferred_label":"exogenous truth","reader_preview":"Exogenous truth is a judgement that comes from outside the process being judged, rather than from the people or tools that produced it.","reader_card":"The word names the source of a judgement, not a guarantee that the judgement is correct. A process that generates work, checks it, and then agrees with itself can be technically careful and still closed. An automatic filter run by the author is not exogenous truth. A named outsider inspecting the public record, including to say the work is not what it thinks it is, would be.","reader_rule":"Treat assessments produced inside the same process, including author-run checks, as author-side. Treat a named outsider's inspection as exogenous.","reader_deep":"Passing an automatic filter can conserve attention. It is not community acceptance, and it is not exogenous truth about whether the work is what it thinks it is."},{"object_id":"term:exponent","preferred_label":"exponent","reader_preview":"An exponent states how many times a base is multiplied by itself, so `b^n` uses base `b` and exponent `n`.","reader_card":"Exponents index the terms in several series on this site. Choosing which exponents are included changes the support, while their least common multiple can control repeating arithmetic patterns.","reader_rule":"Distinguish the exponent that selects a power from the value of the power itself.","reader_deep":"The arithmetic relationship among exponents (divisibility, gaps, and least common multiples) often matters more than their individual size."},{"object_id":"term:exposition","preferred_label":"exposition","reader_preview":"Exposition, in Terence Tao's account of mathematics in the age of AI, is the stage of communicating a result clearly after it has been generated and verified, and before it is published and digested.","reader_card":"Tao's pipeline is generation, verification, exposition, publication, digestion, then canonicalisation. Exposition asks whether a qualified reader can be shown what was proved, why the argument works, who did which part, and what remains open. A formally verified proof can still fail this stage if nobody, including the people who prompted it, can give that account. The papers and problem pages here are attempts at exposition. They are not a claim that exposition is finished, and they are not digestion.","reader_rule":"Read exposition as communication of a checked result, not as the check itself and not as community uptake.","reader_deep":"Tao's expert-talk test sits here: if the people responsible cannot give a clear, correctly attributed, expert-level talk, the result is incomplete even when a proof checker accepted it. Recording false starts and remaining obligations helps later digestion; sanding them away until every step looks equally easy does not."},{"object_id":"term:factor","preferred_label":"factor","reader_preview":"A factor is one quantity or expression multiplied with others to form a product.","reader_card":"For integers, saying d is a factor of n usually means d divides n. In algebra, factors can be expressions whose product expands to the original expression.","reader_rule":"Identify whether factor refers to integer divisibility, algebraic factorisation, or a multiplicative term.","reader_deep":"Factorisation exposes multiplicative structure and can make divisibility, roots, cancellation, and proof obligations easier to see."},{"object_id":"term:factorial","preferred_label":"factorial","reader_preview":"The factorial of a whole number n, written n!, is the product of every whole number from 1 through n, with the convention that 0!","reader_card":"Factorials grow very quickly: 10! is already 3,628,800. Erd\u0151s #68 asks whether the infinite sum of 1/(n! \u2212 1), starting at n = 2, is irrational. The factorial in the denominator is what makes the terms shrink so fast. That speed is why the sum is easy to compute to many digits and why a proof still has to control those denominators exactly rather than numerically.","reader_rule":"Read n! as a product. Read n! \u2212 1 as one less than that product, which is a different integer with its own factorisation.","reader_deep":"Because n! is a multiple of every smaller factorial, these denominators have a nested divisibility structure that ordinary polynomials do not. The Lean work on #68 proves exclusions about those denominators. It does not claim to have settled the original irrationality question."},{"object_id":"term:factorisation","preferred_label":"factorisation","reader_preview":"Factorisation is the expression of an integer or algebraic object as a product of simpler factors, especially the unique prime factors of a positive integer.","reader_card":"Prime factorisation exposes divisibility structure that addition or decimal notation hides. Arithmetic functions such as the totient depend directly on which primes divide a number and with what multiplicities.","reader_rule":"When a result uses factorisation, check which object is being factored and whether the factors are required to be prime or merely nontrivial.","reader_deep":"Some certificate algorithms deliberately avoid factoring large integers because repeated modular checks can verify the needed property more cheaply."},{"object_id":"term:fail_closed","preferred_label":"fail-closed","reader_preview":"Fail-closed means a composite is only as trusted as its weakest required part, and missing evidence blocks rather than silently passes.","reader_card":"Doctrine AX-5 names a fail-closed monotone lattice. If a required witness is missing, the composite does not inherit a pass from its neighbours. Public scope limits use the same posture: a local fixture can succeed while the wider claim stays blocked.","reader_rule":"When evidence is missing, expect a block or a typed refusal, not an implicit pass.","reader_deep":"Monotone here means adding evidence can raise trust and removing it cannot leave the old trust in place. That is why generated pages are told to stay below source authority: a summary must not outrun the weakest cited record."},{"object_id":"term:failure_class_propagation","preferred_label":"failure class propagation","reader_preview":"Failure-class propagation tests whether one local break recurs on sibling surfaces, then repairs only the proven class boundary.","reader_card":"When something fails in one place, that failure is taken as evidence about one location and as a question about others: does the same cause sit in sibling files, standards, routes, tests, or generated records. Failure class propagation answers that question by scanning those siblings, stating exactly where the pattern holds and where it stops, and then making a fix that covers the proven extent and no more. A deliberate decision not to widen counts as a valid outcome when it records what was checked and why widening would currently cause harm.","reader_rule":"When you see a fix described as failure class propagation, read what siblings were scanned and where the boundary was drawn: it claims to have repaired a bounded class, not every instance everywhere and not just the one case that was first noticed.","reader_deep":"It carries a fixed set of parts: the concrete local case, the sibling scan, the class boundary, the counter-force against overgeneralising, the plane that owns the repair, and the validation. A no-op or deferred repair is sound only when it names what was examined and why holding back is the right call for now."},{"object_id":"term:failure_modes","preferred_label":"failure modes","reader_preview":"Failure modes are the distinct ways a method, check, proof, or project can fail, not merely the fact that something did not work.","reader_card":"A failure mode is a typed break: a plausible step that does not follow, a statement that is not the intended one, a check that never ran, a proof nobody can explain. Naming the mode is what makes a later fix possible. Trying is how those modes get encountered; time spent with them is how they get repaired. A pile of undifferentiated failures is only a mood.","reader_rule":"When something fails, name the mode: what was attempted, what was expected, what was observed, and which class of break that is.","reader_deep":"Different modes have different repairs. A Lean rejection is not the same as an uninterpretable success, and neither is the same as nobody looking. Recording failed routes on the problem pages is how this project tries to keep modes visible instead of sanding them away."},{"object_id":"term:farey_sequence","preferred_label":"Farey sequence","reader_preview":"A Farey sequence lists, in increasing order, every fraction in lowest terms whose denominator is at most a chosen bound.","reader_card":"Two neighbours in a Farey sequence cannot be arbitrarily close: their separation is fixed by their denominators. So if a target number is shown to lie strictly between two neighbours, it cannot equal any fraction whose denominator is inside the bound.","reader_rule":"A Farey exclusion rules out denominators up to its bound and says nothing whatever about denominators above it.","reader_deep":"The Erd\u0151s #249 work uses a 240-bit Farey window to exclude every reduced denominator through 79,639,646,646,701,375,323,355,774,875,831,053, roughly 7.96 times ten to the thirty-fourth. The problem remains open because no argument covers the denominators beyond that bound."},{"object_id":"term:feedback_loop","preferred_label":"feedback loop","reader_preview":"A feedback loop is a process whose output is fed back as input, so the process starts to sustain or amplify itself.","reader_card":"A loop can produce real, checked results and still be closed as a social fact: the same process drafts, checks, and agrees. Treating that agreement as independent confirmation is the mistake. Breaking the loop requires a source that is not another turn of the same process.","reader_rule":"Name what is being fed back, and from where, before treating a loop's output as independent evidence.","reader_deep":"Feedback is ordinary systems language here. Positive feedback amplifies; negative feedback can stabilize. The worry, when the word is used about this project, is self-confirmation, not a claim that every loop is vicious. Automatic checks are useful inside the loop. They are not the outsider who would stop it."},{"object_id":"term:finite","preferred_label":"finite","reader_preview":"Finite means containing or requiring only a limited number of elements, steps, or operations.","reader_card":"A finite object can in principle be exhausted by counting or enumeration, even when it is too large to inspect practically. Finiteness is a structural property, not a claim of small size.","reader_rule":"Do not confuse finite with computationally easy or already enumerated.","reader_deep":"Many proofs separate finite verification from infinite generalization because checking every member is possible only after a finite bound is justified."},{"object_id":"term:finite_computation","preferred_label":"finite computation","reader_preview":"A finite computation is a terminating calculation over a bounded set of inputs that produces an exact or explicitly approximate result.","reader_card":"Finite computations can settle individual cases, verify certificates, and discover patterns. They do not settle an infinite family unless a proof shows that the bounded cases exhaust every possibility.","reader_rule":"Identify the computed range and the theorem, if any, that connects it to the general claim.","reader_deep":"Formal projects often turn large computations into compact certificates so the trusted checker verifies the result without trusting the generator's implementation."},{"object_id":"term:fixture","preferred_label":"fixture","reader_preview":"A fixture is the fixed input a check runs against: a small, committed file standing in for the real data, chosen so the same run produces the same result on any machine.","reader_card":"Every component here ships with its inputs rather than reaching for live data. That is what makes a check repeatable on a stranger's laptop: the fixture is in the repository, so the run does not depend on the private system, a network call, or the state of anyone's machine. It also fixes what the check can establish. A component passing against a fixture has been shown to work on that input, which is a smaller claim than working on production data, and the scope limit says so.","reader_rule":"When a check passes, look at the fixture it ran against. The result holds for that input; it is not a statement about data the fixture does not contain.","reader_deep":"Fixtures are why the public repository can be checked at all without the private system. They are deliberately synthetic: shipping real data would leak the substrate this system does not publish. The trade is explicit rather than hidden - a synthetic input buys reproducibility and costs realism, and the component's scope limit records which of the two you are being sold."},{"object_id":"term:formal","preferred_label":"formal","reader_preview":"Formal describes something expressed through explicit rules and symbols precise enough for rule-governed checking.","reader_card":"A formal statement removes selected ambiguities by giving its objects and allowed operations exact meaning. Formality can support machine checking, but it does not by itself guarantee that the formal statement captures the intended real-world question.","reader_rule":"When something is called formal, identify the language, rules, and checker that give the description its precision.","reader_deep":"Formality concerns how a claim is represented and checked; truth, relevance, and correspondence to the intended subject remain separate questions."},{"object_id":"term:formal_mathematics","preferred_label":"formal mathematics","reader_preview":"Formal mathematics is mathematics written so a machine can check it: every definition and step expressed in a language a proof assistant verifies, rather than in the usual prose of a paper.","reader_card":"An ordinary paper is checked by referees reading it. Formal mathematics is checked by software, which is unforgiving about steps a human reader would grant. The translation is substantial work and often reveals that a published argument covers slightly less than it appeared to, which is the main reason to bother.","reader_rule":"When a result is described as formal, look for the source. The formal statement is the claim; the prose is commentary.","reader_deep":"The gap between a formal statement and its description is where overstatement survives machine checking untouched, which is why the wording is registered and checked separately."},{"object_id":"term:formal_proof","preferred_label":"formal proof","reader_preview":"A formal proof is a mathematical argument encoded in a precise language so a proof checker can verify every inference from the stated definitions, axioms, and hypotheses.","reader_card":"Formalization removes hidden appeals to intuition by requiring each step to type-check against explicit rules. It guarantees that the encoded statement follows inside the formal system, not that the statement was translated from informal mathematics exactly as intended.","reader_rule":"Read the formal statement and hypotheses as carefully as the fact that its proof checker accepted it.","reader_deep":"Trust is concentrated in the definitions, formal statement, axioms, elaborator boundary, and small proof-checking kernel rather than spread across a long human argument."},{"object_id":"term:formal_verification","preferred_label":"formal verification","reader_preview":"Formal verification is checking a proof in a precise language against a small trusted kernel, so that correctness no longer depends on the author's reputation or diligence.","reader_card":"On this site the language is Lean. If Lean accepts a proof, it has accepted the statement exactly as written in the source, under the declared axioms. That removes a specific failure: the plausible step that does not actually follow. Tao's point, which this project treats as a limit, is that a verified proof can still be one that nobody understands, including the people who prompted the tool. He notes that the Erd\u0151s problems database already contains AI-generated submissions that are likely correct and still have no human willing to vouch for them. This project treats formal verification as necessary. It is not sufficient. It settles the formal statement. It does not settle whether that statement is the interesting one, whether the prose matches it, whether the result is new, or whether anyone can explain it.","reader_rule":"Trust the formal statement Lean checked, not the summary of it. A verified result still needs a human account of what it means and where it stops.","reader_deep":"The remaining risk after a Lean acceptance is at the proof boundary: does the formal statement capture the intended mathematics, and can a person reconstruct the idea? Those questions belong to exposition and digestion, not to the Lean kernel."},{"object_id":"term:formalisation","preferred_label":"formalisation","reader_preview":"Formalisation is the work of restating a mathematical definition or proof in a proof assistant's language so the software can check every step.","reader_card":"On this site, formalised means a Lean proof exists and was checked. Formalising a result often exposes a gap or a hidden hypothesis in the informal argument, and a formal statement can differ from the informal one, which is why the papers say what each Lean statement covers.","reader_rule":"Formalised tells you the stated proposition was checked. Read the proposition to see what was proved.","reader_deep":"The project treats the Lean statement, its authored meaning and the public claim about it as three separate things; a dense formal proof cannot make the claim stronger than the statement."},{"object_id":"term:fraction","preferred_label":"fraction","reader_preview":"A fraction is a number written as one integer numerator divided by a nonzero integer denominator.","reader_card":"The numerator is the top integer and the denominator is the bottom integer. A fraction is reduced when those integers share no common factor greater than one, making the denominator canonical up to sign.","reader_rule":"When denominator properties matter, check that the fraction has first been reduced.","reader_deep":"Exact fraction arithmetic preserves numerator and denominator structure that floating-point approximations discard, which is essential in irrationality and multiplicative-order arguments."},{"object_id":"term:freshness","preferred_label":"freshness","reader_preview":"Freshness is whether a dated reading of live state is still current under the basis and refresh rule it declared when it was cached.","reader_card":"A live value written into durable prose without an as-of time, a named basis, and a way to recompute it becomes a bare assertion as soon as the world moves. Freshness is the contract that keeps a cached read honest: the value, the time it was taken, the basis it used, and the rule for when it must be taken again.","reader_rule":"For any figure about current state, ask when it was read, from which basis, and how it is refreshed. If those fields are missing, treat the figure as stale.","reader_deep":"AX-10 treats a claim about live state as a cached read. AP-9 names the shortcut of dropping those freshness fields because the number was true at the instant it was written."},{"object_id":"term:front_end","preferred_label":"frontend","reader_preview":"In the Why section here, frontend means the unpublished operator interface of the larger system, shown in the videos, not this public website.","reader_card":"That operator frontend is not published. The videos show what the author sees; the components and their checks, which are the part that can be verified, are the part released. This public site is a separate, published reading surface. The split is stated rather than implied.","reader_rule":"Video of that frontend shows that it exists and how it behaved when recorded. It is not a check anyone else can run, and it is not this website.","reader_deep":"Demonstration and evidence are different currencies. A recording is persuasive and unfalsifiable; a check is dull and repeatable."},{"object_id":"term:frontier","preferred_label":"frontier","reader_preview":"A frontier here is the exact line between what has been proved about a problem and what remains open.","reader_card":"On a problem page, Results and remaining work is that line: the strongest checked statements, the routes that closed, and the precise remaining obligation. It never scores difficulty, and it never claims a problem is solved. One question this project asks is whether that line moves as new AI models are released. A new Lean acceptance can move one problem's frontier; community digestion of that move is a later, external stage.","reader_rule":"Read a frontier as a boundary, not a score: it tells you where the proved part ends and what a contribution would have to prove.","reader_deep":"The Lean repository's frontier relay lets a person hand one problem's frontier to their own coding agent as a bounded task, starting from one exact public revision."},{"object_id":"term:function","preferred_label":"function","reader_preview":"A mathematical function assigns exactly one output to each allowed input in its domain.","reader_card":"A function is specified by its domain, codomain, and assignment rule, not only by a formula. Different formulas can define the same function on a stated domain.","reader_rule":"Read the domain and output type before applying or comparing functions.","reader_deep":"Functions can take numbers, sets, sequences, or other functions as inputs and can be studied through properties such as injectivity, monotonicity, continuity, or multiplicativity."},{"object_id":"term:gate","preferred_label":"gate","reader_preview":"A gate is a rule-controlled checkpoint that permits or blocks a later action according to declared evidence or conditions.","reader_card":"Gates turn requirements into enforceable boundaries: a release gate may require tests, source checks, and safety scans before publication. A gate should explain both its decision and repair route.","reader_rule":"Read which evidence the gate consumes and which action its result controls.","reader_deep":"A passing gate licenses only the downstream action named by its contract, not a broader claim that the whole artifact is correct."},{"object_id":"term:generalise_up_propagate","preferred_label":"generalise-up-propagate","reader_preview":"Generalise-up-propagate is the move of taking a lesson learned in one local piece of work and carrying it upward into a durable, system-wide rule, while stating exactly how far that lesson applies and where it does not.","reader_card":"When work on one part of the system reveals something worth keeping, generalise-up-propagation does two things at once: it preserves the original case as evidence, and it names the broader invariant that case revealed. The general rule it produces carries an applicability boundary and a not-when boundary, so a single instance is never written up as if it held everywhere. It also records which layer of the system owns the new rule, so the lesson lands in a definite home rather than floating loose.","reader_rule":"When you meet a rule that arrived this way, check the boundary it states: it was promoted from one concrete case, and it holds only within the limits recorded alongside it.","reader_deep":"It answers three questions about a local finding: what invariant the case actually revealed, which layer of the system should own that invariant, and how broadly it can be stated without claiming more than the single case supports."},{"object_id":"term:git","preferred_label":"Git","reader_preview":"Git is the version-control system most software projects use to record every change to their files as a numbered commit.","reader_card":"Because every commit has an identifier derived from its contents, a claim can be pinned to an exact state of a repository rather than to whatever the project looks like today.","reader_rule":"A commit identifier names one snapshot. A branch name does not, because the branch moves.","reader_deep":"Several checks here run real Git commands against a sample repository rather than reading a description of what Git would have said. The receipts record how many calls were made."},{"object_id":"term:github","preferred_label":"GitHub","reader_preview":"GitHub is an online service for hosting Git repositories and presenting their code, history, issues, releases, and web-published artifacts.","reader_card":"This site links GitHub repositories so readers can inspect the public source and its revision history. GitHub hosts the material but does not author, verify, or endorse its mathematical or software claims.","reader_rule":"Use GitHub to locate and inspect the repository, then rely on the repository's own evidence and licenses.","reader_deep":"A GitHub URL can identify a moving branch, an exact commit, a file view, a release, or a hosted page, and those routes provide different levels of version precision."},{"object_id":"term:govern","preferred_label":"govern","reader_preview":"To govern something is to set or enforce the rules, authority, and decision process that constrain how it may operate or change.","reader_card":"Governance identifies who can decide, which standards apply, how exceptions are handled, and where outcomes are recorded. It differs from implementation because it controls the conditions under which implementation is accepted.","reader_rule":"When material is governed, locate the owning rule, authority surface, and required validation or approval.","reader_deep":"Governance can be encoded in tools and schemas as well as prose, but it remains ineffective if the operating path bypasses those controls."},{"object_id":"term:grounding","preferred_label":"grounding","reader_preview":"Grounding is the connection of a claim, term, or decision to the source, evidence, observation, or rules that give it support and meaning.","reader_card":"Grounding prevents a statement from floating free of the material it summarizes. A grounded answer makes it possible to trace important claims back to evidence or authority appropriate to the question.","reader_rule":"When something is described as grounded, follow the cited route and check that it directly supports the stated claim.","reader_deep":"A source can ground what it actually contains without grounding broader inferences that the reader adds afterward."},{"object_id":"term:guinea_pig","preferred_label":"guinea pig","reader_preview":"A guinea pig is the experimental subject: the person or case a method is tried on, especially when the risks and confusion of the trial fall on them.","reader_card":"A guinea pig is the named experimental subject, not a branding gesture. The experiment is public and the failures are supposed to stay visible. Being the guinea pig does not make the theorems true, and a weak background does not make the subject a statistically valid sample of experts. It only names who the trial is running on.","reader_rule":"Read guinea pig as who the trial runs on, not as proof that the trial worked or that the subject is an expert.","reader_deep":"In ordinary use the phrase can be affectionate or grim. Here it is literal enough: one person putting incomplete work in public so others can see how AI-assisted mathematics actually behaves, including the uninterpretable stretches. The larger private system is mentioned and then declined as a first load."},{"object_id":"term:harness","preferred_label":"harness","reader_preview":"A harness is the scaffolding that runs a check: it supplies the inputs, executes the thing under test, and captures what came out.","reader_card":"The harness is not the thing being tested; it is what makes testing repeatable. Keeping it separate means the same scenario can be re-run identically, and that a failure can be attributed to the component rather than to how it happened to be invoked.","reader_rule":"Distinguish a harness failure from a component failure. They look alike and mean different things.","reader_deep":"Scaffolding is worth naming because it is where flakiness usually lives. A check that fails intermittently is more often a harness problem than a real defect."},{"object_id":"term:hologram","preferred_label":"hologram","reader_preview":"A hologram here is a compressed copy of the whole public record that still lets you inspect any part: one file that keeps enough structure to zoom, rather than a pile of disconnected pages.","reader_card":"Publicly, a hologram names the JSON packets: one download that carries the site's content and map, so an AI assistant or a person without a clone can query any region without reconstructing the website by hand. Internally, a navigation hologram is the same idea: each row is supposed to hold enough identity, purpose, relationships and next step that you can choose a drilldown without loading everything. In both uses the point is compression that keeps the shape of the whole, not a decorative summary that throws the joins away.","reader_rule":"A hologram is a reading interface derived from source, not a second source of truth. Follow the named files and checks when a decision needs proof.","reader_deep":"Tao's worry about over-polished exposition is the failure mode to avoid: a hologram that sands away the hard steps, the false starts, and the remaining obligations is easy to scan and hard to digest. The packets are useful only if they still carry those joints."},{"object_id":"term:html","preferred_label":"HTML","reader_preview":"HTML is the markup language browsers use to structure the text, links, controls, metadata, and other content of a web page.","reader_card":"HTML describes document structure and semantics; CSS controls presentation and JavaScript can add behavior. A generated HTML page is still a projection of its source data and templates.","reader_rule":"Inspect HTML when exact page structure, links, anchors, or accessible semantics matter.","reader_deep":"Browsers build a document object model from HTML, which scripts and assistive technologies then traverse rather than reading the raw file as plain text."},{"object_id":"term:hypothesis","preferred_label":"hypothesis","reader_preview":"A hypothesis is a condition a result assumes rather than proves, so the conclusion holds only where the condition does.","reader_card":"Hypotheses are where formal work is most often overread. A theorem proved under two assumed analytic inputs is a real theorem, and it is not a statement about the unrestricted problem. The registry separates results by whether their hypotheses are proved, cited from the literature, or simply assumed.","reader_rule":"Read the hypotheses before the conclusion. They decide what the result covers.","reader_deep":"An assumed hypothesis is not a defect; leaving it unstated is. The distinction between derived and assumed is the whole difference between a proof and a reduction."},{"object_id":"term:ide","preferred_label":"IDE","reader_preview":"On this site an IDE is an application such as Visual Studio Code, Cursor, or a similar coding app, used if you clone the repository and run the stated checks, not something required to read these pages.","reader_card":"You do not need an IDE to read this site or to upload a JSON packet to an AI website. You do need some way to run commands if you want to clone a repository and execute the stated checks yourself. An IDE is one convenient way to do that; a terminal without an IDE is another. A chat window that only quotes the website is neither an IDE nor a coding agent.","reader_rule":"Need an IDE only if you intend to open and run the source. Reading a packet is a different, weaker act.","reader_deep":"JSON packets are a practical way to brief an AI website, more convenient than cloning a repository. That is a statement about access, not about mathematical strength: the stronger verification is still to clone the repository and run the check."},{"object_id":"term:implication","preferred_label":"implication","reader_preview":"An implication is a one-way statement that whenever its antecedent holds, its conclusion must also hold.","reader_card":"An implication transfers truth from a stated condition to a consequence but does not prove that the condition itself holds. Reversing the direction requires a separate proof.","reader_rule":"Identify the antecedent, conclusion, hypotheses, and direction before treating an implication as progress.","reader_deep":"Conditional reductions are implications whose antecedent is still open, so they relocate a problem's difficulty without settling the conclusion unconditionally."},{"object_id":"term:inequality","preferred_label":"inequality","reader_preview":"An inequality is a statement that compares two expressions using an order relation such as less than, greater than, or at most.","reader_card":"In the Plectis mathematics, inequalities usually do one of three jobs: bound the tail of an infinite series, constrain a possible denominator or counterexample, or trap a quantity tightly enough to contradict integrality. An exact inequality can therefore advance a problem without computing the underlying value.","reader_rule":"Check the compared quantities, direction, strictness, hypotheses, and whether the bound is uniform over the claimed cases.","reader_deep":"Machine checking verifies the encoded chain of inequalities, but scope still matters: a bound proved only up to a finite cutoff, under a hypothesis, or for one base cannot be read as a universal estimate."},{"object_id":"term:infinite","preferred_label":"infinite","reader_preview":"Infinite means not finite: no finite count exhausts the elements, steps, or extent under discussion.","reader_card":"An infinite set can still be described precisely, and different infinite sets can have different structures or cardinalities. Finite computation samples or bounds an infinite object but cannot enumerate it completely.","reader_rule":"Distinguish a statement about all infinitely many cases from evidence covering only a finite range.","reader_deep":"Proofs about infinite objects use general arguments, induction, limits, compactness, or structural properties rather than checking each case one by one."},{"object_id":"term:inspection","preferred_label":"inspection","reader_preview":"Inspection here is looking at the work closely enough to judge it: reading the Lean, the papers, and the checks rather than taking a summary on trust.","reader_card":"Inspection is looking closely enough to judge, after building has outrun understanding. The unpublished front end is described as the inspection layer that moves a person from maps to files to traces to evidence. A green badge on a page is not that work; opening the named source or result is.","reader_rule":"When a sentence offers inspection, follow the linked source, Lean statement, or check result. Do not treat the surrounding English as the inspection.","reader_deep":"Agents can report a fix that never landed. Inspection is how a human, or an agent through kernel routes, makes a claim auditable after the system outgrows one context window. It does not itself produce a Lean acceptance."},{"object_id":"term:integer","preferred_label":"integer","reader_preview":"An integer is a whole number, positive, negative or zero, with no fractional part.","reader_card":"Integers do most of the work in these problems. The usual route to proving something is not a fraction runs through showing that some quantity would have to be a whole number and cannot be, so the arguments turn on integrality far more than on size or approximation.","reader_rule":"When a proof turns on whether something is an integer, that is usually the whole argument rather than a detail.","reader_deep":"Integrality is a rigid condition, which is what makes it useful: there is no room between two consecutive integers, so a quantity trapped strictly between them cannot exist."},{"object_id":"term:intelligent","preferred_label":"intelligent","reader_preview":"Intelligent, in ordinary use, means able to understand, reason, and adapt; it is not a score this site assigns to people, models, or actor types.","reader_card":"Intelligent here is the everyday capacity to understand, reason, and adapt. A hope about AI is that such capacity can turn uninterpretable mathematics back into something a person can interpret; that is a hope about exposition and digestion, not a ranking. Type A/B is a different axis: who can change the live repositories versus who can only read a packet. Mixing those axes is the usual misread.","reader_rule":"Do not read Type A, Type B, or a green Lean check as a claim that someone or some model is intelligent. Those labels answer different questions.","reader_deep":"Intelligence here is the everyday word in the author's sentence. The site does not define a test for it, and does not treat formal verification as a proxy for it. A verified proof can still be one that nobody, including the people who prompted it, can explain. That limit is why the next term is uninterpretable."},{"object_id":"term:irrational","preferred_label":"irrational","reader_preview":"A number is irrational when it cannot be written as one whole number divided by another.","reader_card":"Most of the problems here ask whether a particular infinite sum is irrational. The question is usually much harder than it sounds: a sum can be computed to any number of digits without that revealing which kind of number it is, because a rational number with an enormous denominator looks exactly like an irrational one for as far as anyone can compute.","reader_rule":"Irrationality is not settled by computing digits. It needs a proof.","reader_deep":"This is why the work is formal rather than numerical. No amount of computation distinguishes the two cases, so the only route is an argument, and the only way to be sure of the argument at this scale is to have it checked."},{"object_id":"term:kernel","preferred_label":"kernel","reader_preview":"On the mathematics pages the Lean kernel is the small checker that accepts or refuses a completed proof; on the software pages the kernel is kernel.py, the local command-line program that answers system questions with small structured packets.","reader_card":"If the surrounding sentence is about Lean, proofs, or kernel acceptance, it means the proof assistant's checker: a deliberately small trusted core. If the surrounding sentence is about commands, navigation, phases, or packets, it means this repository's control program kernel.py. The long walkthrough on the landing names that software kernel as the agents' route, reached by commands instead of screens. The two are unrelated except by the ordinary English word. Trusting Lean means trusting its kernel; asking kernel.py a question means asking for a map over this system's files.","reader_rule":"Read 'kernel acceptance' as Lean. Read 'kernel.py' or a navigation or phase command as the software control layer.","reader_deep":"This glossary keeps both cards so a collision is visible rather than silently resolved. The software kernel's reply is a view over source, not the source. The Lean kernel's acceptance is a check of a formal proof, not a judgement that the English beside it is correct, that the result matters, or that the community has digested it."},{"object_id":"term:laboratory","preferred_label":"laboratory","reader_preview":"A laboratory is a bounded, fixture-backed lane for exercising a candidate standard, check, route, validator, or runtime contract before live use, producing evidence and a possible promotion candidate but never authority by itself.","reader_card":"A laboratory runs a proposed mechanism against a deliberately small, self-contained fixture so the system can see how that mechanism behaves without putting the real files and records at risk. Each run collects records of what happened, names the gaps it found, and can hand back a candidate worth promoting. Its results stand as evidence for that one experiment; they do not by themselves change a standard, a rule, or a release decision.","reader_rule":"Treat what a laboratory produces as the findings of one bounded trial: useful evidence and a possible candidate for promotion, not a settled standard or a guarantee about the live system.","reader_deep":"Because a laboratory works on an isolated fixture rather than the live body of files and records, anything it shows is provisional until it passes the system's standards and release gates. Some older parts of the system call this lane a microcosm probe, which points to the same thing."},{"object_id":"term:lambert_series","preferred_label":"Lambert series","reader_preview":"A Lambert series is an infinite sum of the form \u2211 a\u2099 x\u207f / (1 \u2212 x\u207f), which at x = 1/b is the same as summing terms divided by one less than a power of the base b.","reader_card":"Two of the problems here are about this family: Erd\u0151s #249's binary totient series \u2211 \u03c6(n)/2\u207f sits at x = 1/2, and Erd\u0151s #1049 asks the same shape at rational bases. The form matters because the small adjustment of subtracting one from each power (or equivalently using 1 \u2212 x\u207f in the denominator) changes the arithmetic completely and is what makes the questions hard rather than routine.","reader_rule":"Note the base a Lambert-series result is proved at. Results at base two rarely extend to other bases.","reader_deep":"The base is where most of the open ground is. Several results settle base two and leave every other base untouched, which is a much narrower statement than the phrasing usually suggests."},{"object_id":"term:latex","preferred_label":"LaTeX","reader_preview":"LaTeX is a document-preparation system in which authors write structured source text that a typesetting engine turns into formatted documents such as PDFs.","reader_card":"Mathematical papers use LaTeX because it represents equations, references, and document structure precisely in source form. Compilation checks syntax and cross-references, not the truth of the paper.","reader_rule":"Use the LaTeX source to inspect the authored text and the generated PDF to inspect its published rendering.","reader_deep":"LaTeX separates semantic document commands from final layout, allowing the same source to be rebuilt consistently with a pinned toolchain."},{"object_id":"term:lattice","preferred_label":"lattice","reader_preview":"A lattice is the network connecting the system's artifacts, routes, standards, skills, principles, runtime surfaces, and records, with every edge labelled by a relationship such as authority, implementation, or navigation.","reader_card":"The system is not one flat list of rules but a graph of related parts spread across different kinds: a standard, a skill, a paper module, a vocabulary entry, a navigation route, and a runtime surface can all be joined by an edge that states how they relate. The lattice is that whole-system relationship graph. Because the edges are typed, a lesson learned in one place can move to the part of the system that can safely reuse it while its origin and authority stay intact.","reader_rule":"When something is described as living in the lattice, read it as a position in a connected map rather than an isolated note: it has edges to the standards, routes, and units it relates to, and those edges tell you where else to look.","reader_deep":"The typed edges are what stop the system collapsing into flat doctrine: they keep authority, implementation, navigation, compression, and runtime as distinct but linked layers, so a claim carries its provenance as it travels rather than being copied loose."},{"object_id":"term:lattice_transposition","preferred_label":"lattice transposition","reader_preview":"Lattice transposition is the move of extracting the reusable rule from a local lesson and placing it in the governing skill, standard, paper module, vocabulary, axiom, or principle so other artifact kinds can inherit it.","reader_card":"When a fix or insight surfaces in a single file or task, it often encodes a rule that should not stay tied to that one spot. Lattice transposition is the disciplined version of moving it: it keeps the original case on record, names the portable rule, states where the rule applies and where it does not, and deposits it in the narrowest mature plane that can carry it. A friction met while promoting one principle can refine the standard for writing principles; a routing failure can refine a skill and its documentation. The move is only valid if it preserves the source case and respects the authority of the plane it lands in.","reader_rule":"When you see a rule presented as transposed, check that it cites the concrete case it came from and states its applicability and limits. It is a generalisation that is meant to stay accountable to its source, not a free-floating maxim.","reader_deep":"It is the cross-plane form of up-propagation: up-propagation says a lesson moves upward into durable material, while transposition adds that the lesson changes coordinates, from one artifact kind to the underlying invariant that governs several kinds. It is vocabulary the system's agents author, and it remains tied to cited source surfaces and to the promotion rules of the plane it enters."},{"object_id":"term:launch_scope_decision","preferred_label":"launch-scope decision","reader_preview":"A launch-scope decision is a judgment about whether something is ready to ship, host, or share publicly, an authority that components on this site explicitly do not possess.","reader_card":"A component can establish that a check passed. It cannot establish that the result is sufficient to launch, and the distinction is enforced rather than left to taste: a component whose wording claims launch readiness, hosting authority, or public-sharing authority is rejected by the release gate with a named finding code. Readiness stays a human decision made with evidence in hand.","reader_rule":"Treat component evidence as input to a separate operator or release-owner decision. No passing component grants readiness to host, ship, publish, or share.","reader_deep":"The gate exists because the overclaim is so natural. A run that passes reads as permission, and prose drifts toward it without anyone intending to lie. Naming the refusal and giving it a finding code turns a matter of tone into something a build can fail on."},{"object_id":"term:lay_person","preferred_label":"lay person","reader_preview":"A lay person here is a non-specialist the project still invites to contribute, including with a coding agent, while credit stays with the contributor.","reader_card":"The hero names mathematicians, domain experts, and lay people together as the audience who can collaborate and keep credit. Formal verification is treated as necessary but not sufficient for a lay person wanting to use AI to do mathematics. The coding-agent path is how that invitation is meant to be used.","reader_rule":"Read lay person as an intended role on this site, not as a judgement that the reader cannot follow the maths pages.","reader_deep":"Credit for a contribution does not require a professional title. A lay person working with a coding agent can still file a concrete correction. The mathematics still has to be checked in Lean or against the named record."},{"object_id":"term:lean","preferred_label":"Lean","reader_preview":"Lean is a proof assistant: software that checks a mathematical proof step by step and refuses it if any step does not follow.","reader_card":"A proof written in Lean is checked by a small trusted kernel rather than by a reader's judgement. That is the sense Tao uses when he says a formally verified proof is one whose correctness no longer depends on the author's reputation or diligence. It removes a specific class of error, the plausible step that does not follow, and it does not remove the rest: Lean confirms the proof establishes the statement written in the source. Whether that statement is the intended one, and whether anyone can explain it, are later stages.","reader_rule":"For a Lean result, read the formal statement rather than the summary. Lean checked the statement, not the description of it.","reader_deep":"Machine checking moves the risk rather than deleting it. What remains is whether the formal statement means what the prose says it means, which is the proof boundary. Other assistants exist (Rocq, HOL); this project uses Lean."},{"object_id":"term:lean_kernel","preferred_label":"Lean kernel","reader_preview":"The Lean kernel is the small fixed core of the Lean proof assistant that decides whether a completed proof is accepted.","reader_card":"Everything else in Lean, including tactics, automation, and the Mathlib library, only produces a candidate proof. The kernel is what checks it, and it is deliberately small so that trusting Lean means trusting that one component.","reader_rule":"Kernel acceptance means the written formal statement was proved. It does not mean the English sentence printed beside that statement says the same thing.","reader_deep":"The alignment between a formal statement and its public wording is an authored judgement, checked separately. Beyond the kernel, this corpus also runs Comparator, an independent checker, over nineteen propositions declared apart from their proofs."},{"object_id":"term:lean_standard_axioms","preferred_label":"Lean's standard axioms","reader_preview":"propext, Quot.sound and Classical.choice are the three axioms Lean assumes beyond its core logic: propositional extensionality, the soundness of quotient types, and the axiom of choice.","reader_card":"Essentially all of Mathlib rests on these three, so a proof that uses only them is standing on ordinary mathematical foundations rather than on anything the author added.","reader_rule":"A count of added axioms means axioms beyond these three. A non-zero count means the proof assumed something extra, which has to be read and judged.","reader_deep":"The corpus reports 0 added axioms and 1 admitted proof step, the latter in ExternalVerification/Challenge.lean. An admitted step is a different thing from an added axiom: it is a gap left open inside a proof rather than an assumption introduced for it."},{"object_id":"term:least_common_multiple","preferred_label":"least common multiple","reader_preview":"The least common multiple of a set of whole numbers is the smallest number all of them divide into exactly.","reader_card":"It appears here as the quantity governing when a repeating pattern comes back into alignment. Several results turn on the least common multiple of a set of exponents, because that number determines the period at which the structure of a sum repeats.","reader_rule":"Where a least common multiple appears, it is usually setting the period of something.","reader_deep":"Least common multiples grow quickly, which is what makes the periods involved large enough to be hard to search directly."},{"object_id":"term:lebesgue_measure","preferred_label":"Lebesgue measure","reader_preview":"Lebesgue measure is the standard way of assigning a length to a set of numbers, extended to sets far more broken up than an interval.","reader_card":"Measure and topology disagree on what counts as small. A set can contain no interval at all and still have positive measure, and a dense set can have measure zero. The two answer different questions about the same set.","reader_rule":"Measure says how much of the line a set fills. It does not say whether the set contains any particular number.","reader_deep":"The set of subsums of the sum of one over two to the n minus one is compact, nowhere dense, and of Lebesgue measure exactly one, which is Lean-checked. Being nowhere dense and of positive measure at once is the substance of that statement. It leaves open whether one half is one of those subsums over an infinite support, which is a separate question about membership."},{"object_id":"term:lemma","preferred_label":"lemma","reader_preview":"A lemma is a proved supporting statement introduced mainly for use in proving a later theorem or organizing an argument.","reader_card":"Lemma describes the statement's role, not its difficulty or truth status. In a formal library a lemma is checked in exactly the same way as a theorem and may be mathematically substantial.","reader_rule":"Read a lemma's exact statement and dependencies rather than assuming it is minor because of the label.","reader_deep":"Breaking a proof into lemmas exposes reusable structure, gives the proof checker smaller obligations, and makes later results' dependency chains inspectable."},{"object_id":"term:lemniscate","preferred_label":"lemniscate","reader_preview":"A polynomial lemniscate is the set of points in the plane where a polynomial has one fixed size, so it is a level curve of the polynomial's absolute value.","reader_card":"For a small threshold the curve is a set of separate loops around the roots; as the threshold grows the loops merge. Where and how they merge is what Erd\u0151s #1041 asks about.","reader_rule":"A lemniscate is fixed by the polynomial and the chosen level, so a statement about one level is not a statement about the family.","reader_deep":"Erd\u0151s #1041 asks about short connections inside polynomial lemniscates. The Lean work formalises a no-connection criterion, the exact translation ray-collision locus, finite-line avoidance, and a root-retention bound. The global topology and length gluing remain open."},{"object_id":"term:limit","preferred_label":"limit","reader_preview":"A limit is a boundary or restriction; in Plectis, the surrounding phrase distinguishes a scope limit, mathematical limit, or service rate limit.","reader_card":"Unqualified limit is a routing word, not one local technical object. In evidence pages it marks where a claim stops; in mathematics it is a value approached by a sequence or function; in runtime prose a rate limit caps requests over time. Plectis uses scope limit and mathematical limit when precision matters.","reader_rule":"Read the noun phrase around limit before following the definition; never transfer conclusions between scope, analytic, and rate limits.","reader_deep":"The senses share the ordinary idea of a boundary but have different authorities and checks: scope contracts govern claims, proofs govern mathematical limits, and provider or service policies govern rate limits."},{"object_id":"term:lineage","preferred_label":"lineage","reader_preview":"Lineage is the recorded chain of origins and transformations through which an artifact or claim came to exist.","reader_card":"Lineage connects a current result to its source material, intermediate versions, builders, and prior names. It lets a reader distinguish inherited material from newly authored or transformed material.","reader_rule":"Follow lineage when you need to know where something came from and which transformations may have changed it.","reader_deep":"Lineage establishes ancestry, not correctness; a faithfully traced claim can still be wrong at its source."},{"object_id":"term:living_system_posture","preferred_label":"living system posture","reader_preview":"Living system posture is the stance that every page, result, doctrine row, raw quote, or runtime claim is dated evidence from one system state rather than a timeless fact, so present truth must be checked against the live source and its freshness.","reader_card":"The system changes continuously, so a claim that held when it was written can later be out of date while the record of it stays on file. Living system posture keeps both halves separate: the past claim is preserved as a record of what was true then, and current authority comes from the live files and the surface that tracks how fresh each thing is. Reading something under this posture means asking what was true when it was written, what is true now, what changed, and which check proves it.","reader_rule":"Treat any record on this site as carrying a date and a state of the system, not as a permanent truth: it tells you what held when it was made, and a current claim is confirmed against the live source and its freshness check.","reader_deep":"Different layers go stale in different ways: a generated summary can lag its source, a recorded result can describe wiring that has since moved, and an early piece of doctrine can predate the rule that now governs it. The posture makes the gap between then and now something you can locate and check rather than something hidden."},{"object_id":"term:locus","preferred_label":"locus","reader_preview":"A locus is a specific place where a feature, rule, effect, or piece of evidence resides or becomes observable.","reader_card":"In software and documentation, a locus may be a file, function, field, route, or generated section. Naming it makes responsibility and inspection more precise than referring to the whole repository.","reader_rule":"Use a locus to point to the smallest stable place that owns or demonstrates the relevant behaviour.","reader_deep":"An effect can have several loci, such as one source definition, one builder transformation, and one public projection."},{"object_id":"term:machine_checked","preferred_label":"machine-checked","reader_preview":"Machine-checked means a result has been verified by software rather than accepted on a reader's judgement.","reader_card":"It is the strongest evidence class on this site, and also the narrowest. A machine-checked theorem is settled for exactly the statement checked, which is often a smaller claim than the surrounding description suggests. Reading the statement rather than the headline is the whole discipline.","reader_rule":"Machine-checked applies to the formal statement. Check what that statement actually says.","reader_deep":"The label is worth distrusting when it appears near prose rather than near a source reference, because that is where the range quietly widens."},{"object_id":"term:manifest","preferred_label":"manifest","reader_preview":"A manifest is a structured inventory that lists the artifacts in a release or build together with the metadata needed to identify and check them.","reader_card":"A manifest answers what was included and often records paths, hashes, roles, sizes, or source revisions. It is an inventory, not the content or proof of correctness of every listed item.","reader_rule":"Use the manifest to establish membership and identity, then inspect the listed artifact for substance.","reader_deep":"Manifests make omissions and unexpected additions mechanically detectable because the declared collection can be compared with disk or build output."},{"object_id":"term:map","preferred_label":"map","reader_preview":"A map here is a generated view over a body of material, built from that material rather than written alongside it, showing what is in it and how the parts connect.","reader_card":"Several things on this site are called maps: the architecture map over the components, the semantic map over the proof corpus, the site map for machine readers. They share a form rather than a subject. Each is regenerated from what it describes, so it cannot quietly disagree with it, and each is meant to be entered at any point rather than read from the start.","reader_rule":"A map is regenerated from its subject. If it disagrees with the subject, the map is stale.","reader_deep":"The distinction from documentation is that documentation is written and maps are derived. Written documentation ages silently; a derived map either regenerates or shows as stale, which is why the maps carry the coverage and orphan counts that let you check them."},{"object_id":"term:mathematical_limit","preferred_label":"mathematical limit","reader_preview":"A mathematical limit is the value a sequence or function gets arbitrarily close to as its index or input approaches a stated point or infinity.","reader_card":"In the Plectis mathematics, limits turn infinite processes into exact statements. Saying a_n tends to L means that every requested tolerance is eventually met; it does not require any term to equal L, and it is separate from calling a claim boundary a limit.","reader_rule":"Identify the varying object, target value, direction of approach, and quantified notion of closeness.","reader_deep":"For the infinite series discussed in the papers, convergence establishes that partial sums have a limit, while irrationality asks what kind of number that limit is. A long numerical prefix can suggest the value but cannot replace the quantified proof."},{"object_id":"term:mathematical_priority","preferred_label":"mathematical priority","reader_preview":"In mathematics, priority is recognition of who found a result first, and asking for zero priority means the author does not claim that recognition for a result someone else solves.","reader_card":"Scientific credit is not the same as a git commit, a Lean declaration, or a name on a website. If someone solves one of these problems using this system, the stated policy is that the solver keeps the result: the operator asks for none of the mathematical priority. Contributor credit on this site is the durable named record of accepted help, including downstream results. Priority is the further, external question of who the mathematical community treats as having found the theorem.","reader_rule":"Read 'zero priority' as a claim about mathematical recognition, not as 'this work has no importance' and not as a scheduling queue.","reader_deep":"Priority disputes in mathematics are settled by the community, timestamps, and publication, not by a repository policy. The local promise can be kept as a refusal to contest credit. It cannot award the solver a theorem in the literature. That still depends on writing, checking, and others accepting the result."},{"object_id":"term:mathematician","preferred_label":"mathematician","reader_preview":"A mathematician here is an invited reader and collaborator who can supply insight and direction on the public maths work, with credit kept for that contribution.","reader_card":"The hero names mathematicians first among the people the work is published for. The credit section says mathematicians provide insight and direction, after which others can follow that direction with coding agents. The site does not claim that opening a page makes the reader a mathematician.","reader_rule":"Treat the word as a role in the public collaboration, then follow the problem page or paper for the actual mathematics.","reader_deep":"Digestion, in Tao's sense, still belongs to mathematicians taking a result into their own work. This repository cannot complete that stage by labelling its audience."},{"object_id":"term:mathematics","preferred_label":"mathematics","reader_preview":"Here, mathematics means the published Erd\u0151s-problem work: definitions, proofs, computations, and papers on this site and in the Lean repository.","reader_card":"The published work here is eight open Erd\u0151s problems. Each problem page gives the original question, the results checked in Lean, the approaches that failed, and what remains unproved. Informal exposition, computational evidence, and machine-checked formal proof are kept distinct rather than collapsed into one claim of certainty.","reader_rule":"Match each mathematical claim to the kind of argument or evidence that supports it on that problem page, not to the surrounding English.","reader_deep":"A numerical pattern in examples is not a proof. Lean acceptance settles the formal statement as written. Whether that statement is the intended question, and whether anyone can explain it, belong to exposition and digestion."},{"object_id":"term:mathlib","preferred_label":"Mathlib","reader_preview":"Mathlib is the community-maintained mathematical library for Lean, providing reusable definitions, theorems, tactics, and notation.","reader_card":"The formal mathematics in this project can depend on Mathlib instead of rebuilding standard foundations. Exact proof reproduction therefore pins a compatible Lean and Mathlib revision.","reader_rule":"Treat imported Mathlib theorems as checked dependencies and record the revision used by the build.","reader_deep":"Mathlib's breadth makes formalization faster, but an imported result still has an exact statement whose hypotheses and types must match the local proof."},{"object_id":"term:mechanism","preferred_label":"mechanism","reader_preview":"A mechanism is an account of how something in the system happens: how an object moves, transforms, routes, validates, or changes state, stated as the actual flow of inputs, steps, and outputs rather than as a label.","reader_card":"A mechanism names the working path behind a piece of doctrine or behaviour: what goes in, what is done to it, what comes out, what authority governs it, and how it can fail. It ties an idea to running code, a standard, or a runtime surface, so a claim about how the system behaves can be traced to the steps that actually carry it out.","reader_rule":"When a mechanism is described, follow it down to the inputs, steps, and outputs it names, and check that those steps are grounded in real code or standards rather than asserted in the abstract.","reader_deep":"A mechanism is meant to bind meaning to flow: inputs, transformations, outputs, authority, validation, failure modes, and the relationships between the parts. A description that names a behaviour without those moving parts is a slogan, not a mechanism."},{"object_id":"term:mechanism_contract","preferred_label":"mechanism contract","reader_preview":"A mechanism contract is the public component card's declared shape: what the component is for, which evidence class backs it, the source path, and where the claim stops.","reader_card":"The tour says the public slice turns private machinery into a mechanism contract a stranger can inspect. That is why eighty-eight components can share one reading habit: purpose, evidence class, source, receipts, and a stated scope limit. A card missing one of those is an incomplete public object, not merely poorly written.","reader_rule":"Read the contract fields before trusting surrounding prose. The card is the claim; the command and the result record are how you check it.","reader_deep":"Repeating the same slots across seven areas is what makes a new component comparable to one you already opened. The architecture page can then talk about the whole set without asking you to memorise each name. An unbounded card is a missing edge, not a hint that the claim is unlimited."},{"object_id":"term:mersenne_number","preferred_label":"Mersenne number","reader_preview":"A Mersenne number is one less than a power of two: 1, 3, 7, 15, 31, and so on.","reader_card":"Erd\u0151s #257 asks about sums of reciprocals of these numbers, taken over a chosen set of exponents. At a general whole-number base b the same construction uses b to the n minus one instead.","reader_rule":"Base two is the critical case for these questions, not a representative one, so a result at base two does not automatically extend to other bases.","reader_deep":"The reduced denominator of a finite subsum over Mersenne numbers has multiplicative order exactly the least common multiple of the support, which is Lean-checked. That is a statement about finite supports and does not settle the infinite case."},{"object_id":"term:metabolism","preferred_label":"metabolism","reader_preview":"Metabolism is the governed upkeep loop that refreshes derived artifacts from their authoritative sources and records the checks.","reader_card":"Where ordinary navigation reads what is already there, metabolism is the layer that produces and refreshes it. It watches the authoritative source material, queues bounded pieces of work, populates or rebuilds derived surfaces such as generated summaries and indexes, checks the results, and records what it did. It is durable upkeep, not a one-off answer: enforcing standards, refreshing generated views, and repairing scaffolding all run here. It is bounded by design and halts at the point where a human or a higher authority must decide.","reader_rule":"When something on this site is described as metabolized, take it as a surface that the upkeep loop generated and last refreshed, not source material edited by hand.","reader_deep":"There are two kinds: deterministic metabolism handles fixed, well-defined refresh once the rules exist, while the judgement-bearing kind handles unsettled cases such as naming a new concept or resolving a contradiction before any automatic refresh can take over."},{"object_id":"term:metadata_only","preferred_label":"metadata-only","reader_preview":"Metadata-only describes a record that carries the shape and verdict of a run but none of the content it read: counts, classes, digests and outcomes, without the bodies behind them.","reader_card":"This is what lets a run over private inputs still produce something publishable. The record says how many rows were classified and how, or that a body matched its declared digest, without reproducing the row or the body. It is a deliberate reduction, and it is checkable in its own right: the build refuses a record that has let content leak into a field meant to hold only shape.","reader_rule":"Read a metadata-only record as evidence about a run, never as the data the run saw.","reader_deep":"The guarantee is enforced rather than promised. Result-record fields are checked for body leakage on every build, and a record carrying content where it should carry shape raises a specific failure code instead of passing quietly."},{"object_id":"term:microcosm","preferred_label":"Microcosm","reader_preview":"Microcosm is the retired public name and continuing technical compatibility identifier for the source slice now presented to readers as Plectis.","reader_card":"The name remains in repository paths, package and schema identifiers, generated filenames, `.microcosm/` state, historical records, and legacy URLs because changing those technical handles would break traceability and compatibility; current human-facing copy uses Plectis.","reader_rule":"Read Microcosm as lineage or a technical handle, never as a second current brand beside Plectis.","reader_deep":"Microcosm changed from a public product label to a compatibility name during the Plectis identity migration, so preserved occurrences are intentional evidence of history or stable machine interfaces rather than stale public naming."},{"object_id":"term:microcosm_instance","preferred_label":"a microcosm","reader_preview":"A microcosm is a small case that stands in for a larger class: you can see the wider pattern in the smaller instance.","reader_card":"This row is the ordinary representative-case sense: a small instance in which a wider pattern can be seen. It is not the old project name. The capital-M name Microcosm is a compatibility identifier for the same project now presented as Plectis. Two meanings, two cards; 'a microcosm of' a larger phenomenon is this one.","reader_rule":"If the word is 'a microcosm of' a larger phenomenon, read this card. If it is a path, package, or old product label, read Microcosm.","reader_deep":"Ordinary English borrowed the scientific word for a world in miniature. The guinea-pig sentence depends on that miniature: one non-expert's public attempt as a preview of a broader shift. It does not claim that one person is a statistically valid sample, and it does not revive the retired public brand."},{"object_id":"term:mining","preferred_label":"mining","reader_preview":"Mining, here, is the continuous search for useful structure in a large space of AI reasoning, proofs, and drafts, analogously to how cryptocurrency mining searches a huge space of hashes.","reader_card":"Mining here is ongoing extraction under a given direction: try routes, keep failures, return what checks. It is not a one-shot query. Mathematicians can provide that direction; people with coding agents can then search it. The similarity to cryptocurrency mining is the grind of GPU cycles, not the economics, and not a claim that proofs are coins.","reader_rule":"Read mining as continuous search under a given direction, not as ownership of the resulting theorem and not as a crypto product.","reader_deep":"Data mining in the statistical sense is a different ordinary phrase; the scare-quoted 'mine' on this site is closer to crypto mining's continuous computational search. GPUs are the shared substrate. What is extracted here is a checkable argument or a failed route, which still needs a human to stand behind it. Formal verification can accept a statement found this way; it cannot replace exposition or digestion of that statement."},{"object_id":"term:model","preferred_label":"model","reader_preview":"A model is a representation used to reason with: usually an AI system here, and with a qualifier a mathematical structure or a data schema.","reader_card":"The question is whether the frontier moves as new models release. Nearby, the same English word can still mean a mathematical representation or a data schema; the qualifier decides. An AI model can propose a proof or a patch. A validator or Lean kernel independently checks the resulting artifact.","reader_rule":"Identify what the model represents, how it was produced, and where its assumptions stop before treating its output as a fact about the problem.","reader_deep":"Model outputs are observations about the model's behaviour, not direct facts about the mathematics unless a check connects the two. The site does not score models; it records whether a named problem's frontier moved."},{"object_id":"term:module","preferred_label":"module","reader_preview":"A module is a bounded unit of code or exposition grouped around one responsibility and designed to be referenced as a whole.","reader_card":"In software, a module collects related definitions or behavior; in this site's paper-module layer, it collects a subsystem's readable explanation. The surrounding label tells which meaning applies.","reader_rule":"Identify the module kind before assuming that it is executable code or a paper module.","reader_deep":"Modularity creates boundaries for ownership, testing, reuse, and navigation, but those boundaries are organizational rather than proof that the unit is independent."},{"object_id":"term:monic","preferred_label":"monic","reader_preview":"A polynomial is monic when the coefficient of its highest power is 1, so it looks like z\u207f + lower terms rather than 3z\u207f + lower terms.","reader_card":"Scaling a polynomial changes its coefficients but not its roots. Restricting to monic polynomials is a way of fixing that scale so statements about lengths and level curves are comparable. Erd\u0151s #1041 is stated for a monic polynomial whose roots lie in the open unit disc: the leading 1 is part of the hypothesis, not decoration.","reader_rule":"If a statement assumes a monic polynomial, multiplying through by a constant takes you outside the hypothesis even when the roots stay the same.","reader_deep":"In the complex plane the lemniscate of a polynomial depends on the absolute value of p(z), which scales with the leading coefficient. The monic restriction stops that extra degree of freedom from moving the level sets. Results proved for monic p do not automatically apply to every polynomial with the same roots."},{"object_id":"term:morse_saddle","preferred_label":"Morse saddle","reader_preview":"A saddle is a point where a surface rises in one direction and falls in another, like the middle of a mountain pass.","reader_card":"Non-degeneracy is what pins the number of directions leaving the point. An argument that assumes the wrong number of local branches at a saddle can be locally invalid while looking right globally.","reader_rule":"Morse is a hypothesis about curvature at the point, not a description of the whole surface.","reader_deep":"The Erd\u0151s #1041 note reports that a recent manuscript's load-bearing Proposition 12 uses an invalid three-ended local block at an interior Morse saddle. This was diagnosed here independently and matches Tao's public account of the same defect. The proposition is not refuted and may admit a repair."},{"object_id":"term:multiple","preferred_label":"multiple","reader_preview":"Multiple can mean more than one item, or in arithmetic a number obtained by multiplying another number by an integer; Plectis uses both senses.","reader_card":"In system prose, multiple usually means several, as in multiple providers or files. In the mathematical papers, m is a multiple of d exactly when m = d \u00d7 k for some integer k, equivalently when d divides m. The mathematical sense must be supplied by a product or divisibility context.","reader_rule":"Resolve the sense from the noun phrase: several objects in ordinary prose, or an exact integer product in a divisibility statement.","reader_deep":"The mathematical sense coordinates common denominators, periods, and least-common-multiple constraints. It includes zero and can include negative values under the theorem's convention; none of that structure is implied by the ordinary adjective."},{"object_id":"term:multiplicative_order","preferred_label":"multiplicative order","reader_preview":"The multiplicative order of an integer `b` modulo `Q` is the smallest positive exponent `k` for which `b^k` leaves remainder one when divided by `Q`.","reader_card":"The order exists when `b` and `Q` are coprime and measures the period of repeated multiplication by `b` modulo `Q`. It can connect a reduced denominator to the least common multiple of a set of exponents.","reader_rule":"Check the modulus, base, coprimality condition, and minimality of the claimed exponent.","reader_deep":"Verifying `b^k \u2261 1 mod Q` only shows that the order divides `k`; proving exact order also requires excluding every smaller positive divisor candidate."},{"object_id":"term:named_basis","preferred_label":"named basis","reader_preview":"A named basis is the exact bytes, rows, or inputs a claim is derived from, identified so an independent checker can recompute the verdict.","reader_card":"A result counts only when it says which inputs it used. Naming the basis is what makes reuse and drift detectable: matching digests may be reused, and any change in those inputs forces a recompute. A missing basis yields no result, however confident the sentence sounds.","reader_rule":"Ask which exact bytes or rows underlie the derivation. If the basis is unnamed, the claim sits at the bottom of the evidence order.","reader_deep":"The operating loop treats named basis as a question every claim faces, distinct from the checker that accepts the verdict and from the linear-algebra sense of the same English word on the maths pages."},{"object_id":"term:natural_number","preferred_label":"natural number","reader_preview":"A natural number is a whole number used for counting, with the convention about whether zero is included stated by the surrounding formalization.","reader_card":"Different texts use \u2115 for either {0,1,2,\u2026} or {1,2,3,\u2026}; Lean includes zero in its natural-number type. Positive natural numbers exclude zero.","reader_rule":"Check the zero convention before transferring a statement between sources.","reader_deep":"Natural numbers support induction, recursion, divisibility, and finite counting, and formal systems encode them as a specific type rather than an informal collection."},{"object_id":"term:navigable","preferred_label":"navigable","reader_preview":"A navigable term, record, or page carries at least one concrete anchor, source reference, relationship link, command, or pointer that takes a reader from its summary to the underlying object or evidence.","reader_card":"The architecture map is the main public example: each area and component links through to source files, standards, and checks. Glossary rows and component cards are navigable when they carry an anchor, source reference, relationship, command, or pointer from the summary to the object.","reader_rule":"When something is described as navigable, expect a real link, anchor, or command behind it, and follow that route to the source if you want to confirm the claim yourself.","reader_deep":"A navigable concept can be selected, opened, related to others, and bounded by what it is allowed to claim. Anything that cannot route to a source, record, or owner is counted as a gap rather than a finished glossary row."},{"object_id":"term:navigation","preferred_label":"navigation","reader_preview":"Navigation is the system\u2019s browse-and-drilldown layer for moving from a question to the smallest source-backed answer surface.","reader_card":"Rather than opening the whole codebase to find one thing, navigation presents the system as a series of choices that can be opened in stages: a top-level map, then groups, then individual rows, then the source or evidence behind a row. Each step shows what exists and what it contains before the full text is loaded, so a path through the material can be chosen deliberately. It is the layer that makes the system inspectable in order, from overview down to a single record.","reader_rule":"Treat a navigation surface as a map of what is available, not the thing itself: follow it down to the underlying record before relying on what a row says.","reader_deep":"Navigation works by compression, turning a large body of files into visible rows that can be expanded at widening levels of detail, so the system can present its own structure instead of each reader rebuilding the map from scratch."},{"object_id":"term:necessary_and_sufficient","preferred_label":"necessary and sufficient","reader_preview":"A condition is necessary if the result cannot hold without it, and sufficient if it is enough for the result to hold.","reader_card":"Necessary: without the condition, the result cannot hold. Not sufficient: having the condition is not enough for the result to hold. Formal verification is a local example: without a checkable proof, a plausible argument can still be wrong; a kernel can still accept a statement nobody can explain, including the people who prompted it. Digestion and exposition are the remaining jobs. This row defines the logical words; the formal-verification card applies them.","reader_rule":"When someone says necessary but not sufficient, name both halves: what still has to be true, and what would still be missing if it were.","reader_deep":"In proofs, a necessary condition can hold while the theorem remains false. A sufficient condition can be stronger than the interesting statement. The pair is used here as a limit on method, not as a completed characterisation of intelligence, credit, or community acceptance."},{"object_id":"term:negative_case","preferred_label":"negative case","reader_preview":"A negative case is an input a check must reject, shipped beside passing inputs so a detector that stops discriminating fails visibly instead of quietly accepting everything.","reader_card":"A check that only ever sees valid input cannot tell you it is working. Break it and it still passes. The negative case closes that hole: it is a deliberately bad input with a recorded expectation that the check refuses it. If a component's negative case starts passing, the component is broken even though nothing has thrown an error. This is the difference between a check that runs and a check that discriminates.","reader_rule":"Read a component's negative cases to see what it can actually detect. A check with no negative case has not shown it can fail.","reader_deep":"Negative cases are the cheapest defence against the failure mode this system is most exposed to: machinery that reports success because it has stopped looking. They are why a passing result is treated as evidence rather than as a green light - the run has demonstrated both that it accepts what it should and refuses what it should not."},{"object_id":"term:newton_flow","preferred_label":"Newton flow","reader_preview":"The Newton flow is the continuous version of Newton's method: instead of jumping in steps toward a root of a polynomial, a point drifts smoothly in the direction Newton's method would push it.","reader_card":"Along that flow the value of the polynomial decays in a controlled way, which turns a question about curves in the plane into a question about how those trajectories can meet.","reader_rule":"A statement about the flow constrains the trajectories, not the polynomial's roots directly.","reader_deep":"For Erd\u0151s #1041, Lean checks that the polynomial value decays as exp of minus t along the flow and therefore stays on one oriented ray, and formalises the resulting no-connection criterion. The global topology and the length gluing remain open."},{"object_id":"term:normal_form","preferred_label":"normal form","reader_preview":"A normal form is a standard way of rewriting an object so that two objects are equal exactly when their rewritten versions match.","reader_card":"Putting a problem into a normal form does not solve it. It moves the difficulty into a shape where the remaining obligation can be stated exactly, which is often what makes a machine check possible at all.","reader_rule":"A normal form is a reformulation, so it carries the same difficulty as the statement it rewrites unless a separate result says otherwise.","reader_deep":"Erd\u0151s #68 has an exact factorial-successor normal form in Lean, and Erd\u0151s #257 an exact carry-pivot normal form. Both are exact reformulations, and both problems remain open."},{"object_id":"term:nowhere_dense","preferred_label":"nowhere dense","reader_preview":"A set is nowhere dense when it contains no interval, and neither does the set you get by adding all its limit points.","reader_card":"It is the topological sense of thin: however far you zoom in, you always find a gap. The Cantor set is the standard example.","reader_rule":"Nowhere dense is a statement about gaps, not about size in measure. A set can be nowhere dense and still fill most of an interval in Lebesgue measure.","reader_deep":"The achievement set studied for Erd\u0151s #257 is compact, nowhere dense, and of Lebesgue measure one at the same time. The pairing is the point: no interval sits inside it, and yet it is not negligible."},{"object_id":"term:number_theory","preferred_label":"number theory","reader_preview":"Number theory is the area of mathematics that studies integers and related structures, including divisibility, primes, congruences, and arithmetic functions.","reader_card":"The Erd\u0151s problems on this site are largely number-theoretic, often involving sequences, reciprocal sums, denominators, and multiplicative behavior. Number theory can use tools from analysis, algebra, combinatorics, and computation.","reader_rule":"Identify which integer or arithmetic structure the problem is asking about.","reader_deep":"Elementary statements in number theory can require deep proofs because simple integer operations encode strong global constraints."},{"object_id":"term:numerator","preferred_label":"numerator","reader_preview":"The numerator is the top part of a fraction: in P/Q, it is P, the quantity being divided by Q.","reader_card":"Plectis uses numerators when an exact finite sum is reduced to P/Q. P and Q must be tracked as a pair: cancelling a common factor changes both, and only the reduced denominator Q is later used for the order and divisibility certificates.","reader_rule":"When a paper or component writes P/Q, check whether the fraction is reduced and do not transfer a claim about Q to P.","reader_deep":"In the finite denominator-certificate work, exact rational summation produces P/Q before the denominator is reconstructed independently from least-common-multiple and factorisation data. The numerator records the accumulated finite sum; it is not the certificate target, and its value depends on the same cancellation that determines the final denominator."},{"object_id":"term:obs_studio","preferred_label":"OBS Studio","reader_preview":"OBS Studio is the free screen-recording and streaming program most people use to record a screencast.","reader_card":"It is named here only as a point of comparison: the recording console used for these videos does a similar job but sits inside the system being recorded, so it can index what was on screen rather than only capturing it.","reader_rule":"The comparison is to the recording function alone, not to a claim about features.","reader_deep":"The Demo Take Console records, transcribes, indexes frames against words, and compiles cuts. That indexing is what a general-purpose recorder does not do."},{"object_id":"term:observe","preferred_label":"observe","reader_preview":"An observe is a read-only pass that gathers structured evidence from files, probes, and responses into a bounded record before any decision or change is made.","reader_card":"Observe is the evidence-gathering half of the observe/apply pairing the system runs. An observe pass learns, maps, compares, or probes a target and writes down what it found: the questions it asked, the files it examined, the responses it got back, a synthesis, and any validation signals. It can run locally or across a bridge of helpers, but it never mutates code or doctrine. Its job is to keep exploration separate from action so a decision rests on collected facts rather than a first guess.","reader_rule":"Read an observe pass as a record of what was examined and found, not as a completed change. The work it describes has been looked into, not yet carried out.","reader_deep":"Keeping the observe step explicit means the evidence behind a later change is inspectable on its own, and the system does not treat its first plan as if the work were already done."},{"object_id":"term:omission_receipt","preferred_label":"omission receipt","reader_preview":"An omission receipt is the note on a compressed row that states what was left out, why that omission is allowed at the current depth, and which command reopens the full source.","reader_card":"Pages and browse rows on this site are compressed views of a much larger body of source material. An omission receipt is the part of such a row that keeps the compression honest: it names what was dropped, such as the underlying source bodies, deeper relationships, or fuller command output, gives the reason that omission is acceptable for a quick-scan row, and points to the drilldown that recovers the complete material. It lets a row be useful for choosing what to open without quietly pretending to be the whole story.","reader_rule":"Treat the omission receipt on a row as the row's own statement of its limits: it tells you what is not shown and how to reach the full source, so follow that pointer when you need the complete picture rather than assuming the short row is everything.","reader_deep":"Its function is to stop a compact view from claiming false completeness: the row stays cheap to read, yet the receipt records exactly what was excluded, why that exclusion is acceptable at this level of detail, and which command restores the full source behind it."},{"object_id":"term:open_obligation","preferred_label":"open obligation","reader_preview":"An open obligation is the exact statement that would still have to be proved for a result to reach its target.","reader_card":"Problem pages list open obligations after the checked results. Each is written so that a proof of it, combined with the results already checked, would settle the named question. The list specifies the remaining work; it does not rank or predict it.","reader_rule":"Treat an open obligation as a precise target. Proving one is a contribution; restating one is not.","reader_deep":"Obligations are kept separate from conjectures and from failed approaches, so a reader can tell what is needed from what was guessed and what was tried."},{"object_id":"term:open_problem","preferred_label":"open problem","reader_preview":"An open problem is a precisely stated question for which no proof or counterexample accepted by the relevant mathematical community is currently known.","reader_card":"A problem can remain open despite extensive computations, conditional reductions, solved special cases, and formalized supporting theory. Those results narrow or reorganize the question but do not settle its universal statement.","reader_rule":"When a problem is marked open, distinguish the original question from the partial and conditional results reported around it.","reader_deep":"Open status is time-sensitive and source-sensitive, so a responsible statement names the problem, date or source of the status, and any claimed resolution that would need independent review."},{"object_id":"term:open_world","preferred_label":"open-world","reader_preview":"Open-world epistemics means absence of proof is unknown, not false, unless the domain was declared finite and fully covered.","reader_card":"Doctrine AX-6 is the public statement: failing to prove something does not establish its negation. A Lean search that found no proof, or a page that does not mention a result, is not a disproof. Closed-world reasoning is allowed only where the domain was declared finite and the coverage is itself evidenced.","reader_rule":"Do not read silence, a missing page, or a failed search as a negative theorem.","reader_deep":"This is the same caution as not treating a green check as digestion. The system refuses to mint a fact from a gap. Typed refusal is the honest shape when the computation did not cover the case."},{"object_id":"term:operator_proxy","preferred_label":"operator proxy","reader_preview":"An operator proxy is an outside worker acting on the operator's behalf for one bounded task from a prepared context-and-instruction packet, carrying the operator's intent without acquiring the operator's authority.","reader_card":"When the system hands a task to a separate worker, such as a chat model or coding agent, it sends a packet that holds the purpose, the relevant doctrine, worked examples, the output shape, and the constraints. The worker runs as an operator proxy: it can do real work because the packet makes the intent executable, but its remit is fixed to that one task. Choosing what context to send, validating the result, and deciding whether the output enters the system stay with the owning side, not the proxy.","reader_rule":"Treat an operator proxy's output as work done to order, not as a decision made by the operator. It is bounded to the task it was given, and it becomes part of the system only after the owning side validates and admits it.","reader_deep":"The role separates how capable a worker is from how much authority it holds: a highly capable proxy can outperform a less capable owner on a well-prepared packet, yet it still cannot see context it was not given, change the system's rules, or speak as the operator."},{"object_id":"term:operator_voice","preferred_label":"operator voice","reader_preview":"Operator voice is the preserved first-person record of what the operator said directly, kept separate from every later agent-authored summary or interpretation as the original human input behind the system.","reader_card":"Operator voice is the unmediated expression of the person who runs the system: raw seed paragraphs, spoken notes, and direct instructions, recorded as they were given. It holds authority over every later artifact, so paper modules, standards, and agent-written summaries may compress or route it but can never replace or contradict it. The raw record is kept append-only for this reason: it is the layer everything else answers to.","reader_rule":"When something is marked as operator voice, treat it as the original human source, above any summary or agent rephrasing of it. If a later page conflicts with it, the operator voice is the one that holds.","reader_deep":"It is distinct from two nearby things: a Bridge proxy acting for the operator under a prepared packet, and an agent-authored projection that compresses operator voice into doctrine. Both of those are downstream; only operator voice is the original."},{"object_id":"term:option_surface","preferred_label":"option surface","reader_preview":"An option surface is a browse layer of stable, kind-grouped, selectable artifact rows used to choose and inspect a specific item before opening the authoritative source it points to.","reader_card":"An option surface sits between the top-level Kind Atlas and the underlying source material. For a chosen kind of artifact, such as components, paper modules, or doctrine cards, it enumerates the available rows cheaply, lets one be selected by its stable id, and shows a compact card for that row that states what is and is not included and how current it is. Only after a row is chosen does it hand off to the authoritative standard, registry, skill, or paper module behind it.","reader_rule":"Treat an option surface as a catalogue for finding and selecting one row, not as the final authority on that row. The card it shows is a summary with its own currentness and omission notes; the binding detail is in the source it links to.","reader_deep":"It works in bands: a cheap listing band shows many rows at a glance, and a card band expands a single selected id into more detail, including a record of any id that was asked for but not found. This keeps browsing inexpensive while keeping the path back to source explicit."},{"object_id":"term:orbit","preferred_label":"orbit","reader_preview":"An orbit is the sequence of values produced by applying the same step over and over from a starting point.","reader_card":"Orbits turn a question about a number into a question about long-run behaviour: does the sequence stay bounded, return, drift, or vanish. That is often easier to state exactly, and no easier to prove.","reader_rule":"A finite stretch of an orbit is a computation. Statements about the whole orbit need a separate argument.","reader_deep":"Erd\u0151s #243 becomes rigidity of a centred integer error orbit once denominators are cleared, and the remaining Erd\u0151s #257 targets reduce to explicit alternatives about infinite orbits. In both cases the reformulation is exact and the problem is open."},{"object_id":"term:organ","preferred_label":"organ (source identifier)","reader_preview":"An organ is the source-code name for the same bounded unit that public pages call a component.","reader_card":"You will not see organ as the public object name. It still appears in source paths such as organ_registry.json in the public clone. Each organ does one job, such as mapping a route, running a check, importing source material, or generating a page, and carries a record of what it is allowed to assert.","reader_rule":"When a source path or identifier names an organ, read it as one bounded unit doing a single job. The same unit appears on the public pages as a component.","reader_deep":"Organ is the source-level and runtime name, kept stable in file paths, identifiers, and compatibility records. Component is the same unit under its public label."},{"object_id":"term:orientation","preferred_label":"orientation","reader_preview":"Orientation is the material meant to be read first: a short account of what this is and how to navigate it, as distinct from the evidence itself.","reader_card":"Orientation is deliberately separated from evidence, because the two carry different weight. An orientation guide is the author describing the work, which is useful and is not proof of anything. Keeping the boundary visible stops a good explanation from being mistaken for a good result.","reader_rule":"Read orientation to find your way. Read records to check claims.","reader_deep":"The separation is easy to lose, because clear description is persuasive in the same register as evidence."},{"object_id":"term:orphan","preferred_label":"orphan","reader_preview":"An orphan is an item an index has failed to place: something present in the source but connected to nothing in the map built over it.","reader_card":"The orphan count is how you tell an index apart from a partial index. A map with orphans is one that quietly stops covering part of its subject, and any question asked of it will return an answer that looks complete. Publishing the count is what makes coverage checkable rather than assumed.","reader_rule":"Check the orphan count before trusting a map's coverage. Zero is the only reassuring number.","reader_deep":"The failure mode is silence: an orphaned declaration does not raise an error, it simply never appears in an answer, so the gap is invisible from the outside."},{"object_id":"term:package","preferred_label":"package","reader_preview":"A package is a collection prepared to travel as one unit.","reader_card":"A software package carries code, metadata, dependencies, and a version for package tooling. A submission package instead gathers the files and records needed for review or delivery. Neither meaning is automatically the same as an AI packet or evidence bundle, which have their own reader and provenance contracts.","reader_rule":"Use install, import, dependency, or version context for the software sense; use submit, review, or delivery context for the assembled-artifact sense.","reader_deep":"Plectis also preserves some package identifiers for compatibility and lineage. Such an identifier can keep an old route or dependency resolvable without making that package name a current public category or product identity."},{"object_id":"term:packet","preferred_label":"packet","reader_preview":"A packet is one JSON file that carries a structured copy of the public record, so it can be uploaded to an AI website in a single step instead of cloning a repository or scraping pages.","reader_card":"On an ordinary AI website, a packet is the practical object, and in that setting it is more usable than asking the same assistant to clone a repo. Cloning and running the named commands remains the stronger verification. A packet is trusted more than a chat paraphrase of the site, and less than a local replay of the stated checks. Reading a packet is orientation. A check counts as passed only when the named command was run and its evidence shown.","reader_rule":"Use a packet to find the claim, the source, and the limit. Do not treat having read the packet as having verified the work.","reader_deep":"The reviewer brief is the small packet, sized for one turn. The full AI review packet is the large one. Both are generated from the same public site, so they can drift if the site is rebuilt; the live pages remain the human reading surface."},{"object_id":"term:palomar","preferred_label":"Palomar","reader_preview":"Palomar is an external registry for machine-checked mathematical results, recording a repository at one fixed version, the formal statements claimed and the checks run.","reader_card":"The project prepares result families for Palomar and keeps its own ranking of them in the Lean repository's showcase file. Submission, a passed automated check and public registration are three different states, and the site names which one applies wherever it mentions a record. A Palomar record does not certify the rest of the corpus, establish novelty or replace peer review.","reader_rule":"Read a Palomar reference as a pointer to a pinned, checked formal statement, then check which state the record has reached.","reader_deep":"Palomar's automated checks and review have stated limits. The project's own claim records remain the authority for what each result claims, and Comparator remains the check that a stated proposition matches its Lean proof."},{"object_id":"term:paper","preferred_label":"paper","reader_preview":"A paper is a rendered PDF write-up that gives a human-readable account of a project, question, method, result, and evidence boundary, linked to its supporting source where available.","reader_card":"The Plectis paper shelf contains reader-facing rendered PDFs: short problem notes, longer working records, mathematical expositions, and systems write-ups. Calling one a paper identifies that presentation form, not peer review, journal publication, novelty, or proof authority. A paper module is different: it is an internal ai_workflow document that maps a subsystem, its owners, and its routes. For mathematical claims, the paper explains the argument while the Lean declarations and claim registry own what was checked and what remains open.","reader_rule":"Use a paper to understand the argument and its significance, then follow its claim and source references for verification.","reader_deep":"A paper integrates source records into a coherent human exposition and is versioned with the source used to render it. Its value is explanation and synthesis. Formal claims remain subordinate to Lean and the claim registry, measurements remain subordinate to their records, and the label paper alone grants no academic status."},{"object_id":"term:paper_module","preferred_label":"paper module","reader_preview":"A paper module is a stable explanation packet mapping one subsystem's purpose, structure, invariants, source locations, and current state without replacing the code or records it describes.","reader_card":"You meet them on docs/paper-modules.html and via the Read paper module button on a component card. They are not the PDF papers on the landing. Each packet records a subsystem's intent, shape, rules, source files, current state, remaining work, and refresh rule, and points down to source and up to the principles, components, and standards the area connects to.","reader_rule":"Treat a paper module as an orientation for one area, then check the source it points to for any detail you will rely on. It describes a subsystem at a point in time and can lag the code if it has not been refreshed.","reader_deep":"A paper module is one ontology row per subsystem: it names the area and links it to source loci below and to principles, concepts, mechanisms, skills, standards, and navigation surfaces above."},{"object_id":"term:partial_sum","preferred_label":"partial sum","reader_preview":"A partial sum is the finite sum of the first selected terms of an infinite series.","reader_card":"Partial sums are the finite objects used to define and approximate a series. Convergence means that these sums approach one fixed value as the cutoff moves farther out.","reader_rule":"Do not confuse a computed partial sum with the exact value of the infinite series without a bound on the remaining tail.","reader_deep":"A rigorous approximation pairs the partial sum with an error estimate controlling every term not yet included."},{"object_id":"term:passion","preferred_label":"passion","reader_preview":"Passion is the judgment trait that keeps the operator's live aim and stake present while the work remains bounded by standards, evidence, and source authority, preserving why the work matters as well as what it must complete.","reader_card":"Passion is one of the named traits the system uses to describe disciplined human judgment, alongside care and common sense. It is the trait that resists flattening a request into sterile task completion: it carries the person's theory, urgency, and meaning into the action so the action stays intelligible. It is bounded, not free rein. Whatever it motivates must still be backed by a source and constrained by the standards, so the live purpose is preserved without becoming unchecked enthusiasm.","reader_rule":"When you see passion named, read it as the stake and intent behind a piece of work, held alongside the evidence rather than in place of it. It signals why something matters; it does not on its own license a claim.","reader_deep":"In this system passion sits inside a wider surface of judgment traits: passion supplies the live purpose, while care and common sense keep that purpose grounded in artifacts that can be located, checked, and maintained. Passion without standards is noise, and standards without passion miss the point the work exists to serve."},{"object_id":"term:path_handle","preferred_label":"path handle","reader_preview":"A path handle is a stable short identifier that resolves to a current file path, its role, and its verification command.","reader_card":"A path handle separates the identity of a reference from the place a thing is stored. Instead of repeating a long or unstable filesystem path everywhere, the system keeps one governed entry that records where the target lives now, what job it plays, how to display it, how fresh it is, which older references still point to it, and the command that expands it back to the full path. Because the handle stays stable while the underlying location can move, it lets the system keep packets compact and references intact without renaming files on disk.","reader_rule":"When you meet a path handle, treat it as a stable pointer rather than the location itself: it names where the target lives now and carries the command to expand it, while old references it lists still resolve to the same target.","reader_deep":"A path handle carries the target path, a role, a display label, a source fingerprint or freshness posture, compatibility redirects, an expansion command, and a migration state, so a reference can stay constant even as the storage container behind it changes."},{"object_id":"term:pdf","preferred_label":"PDF","reader_preview":"PDF is a document file format designed to preserve a page's visual layout across devices and software.","reader_card":"The site publishes papers as PDFs for stable human reading and links their source separately. A PDF preserves presentation but is less suitable than source or structured data for exact reuse and machine inspection.","reader_rule":"Use the PDF for the published reading copy and follow its source links for editable text and proof authority.","reader_deep":"A PDF can embed text, fonts, images, links, and metadata, but its appearance does not reveal whether the underlying claims were checked."},{"object_id":"term:peer","preferred_label":"peer","reader_preview":"A peer is another capable coding-agent instance working on the system that can inherit durable lessons recorded by earlier agents, without implying shared memory or authority beyond those records.","reader_card":"The system is built to be operated by capable AI coding agents, and a peer is one such agent considered in relation to the others. When an earlier agent records a piece of judgment it learned, a later peer can pick that lesson up and use it. The transfer is agent to agent: it carries practical know-how between siblings of comparable capability, and it sits below the human operator's own words and below the system's settled doctrine until it has been reviewed and adopted.","reader_rule":"When something is marked as coming from a peer, treat it as one capable agent's preserved judgment offered to another, not as the operator's instruction or as confirmed doctrine.","reader_deep":"A peer is an authority boundary as much as a kind of actor: it marks the level at which one agent may help a future agent by passing on judgment, a level that remains beneath the human voice and beneath active doctrine until that material is curated and promoted."},{"object_id":"term:peer_propagate","preferred_label":"peer-propagate","reader_preview":"A peer-propagate is a source-linked, reusable lesson in operating judgment that one capable agent records for future peer agents to inherit.","reader_card":"When an agent working in this system learns something genuinely useful but not yet settled enough to become a formal rule, it writes that lesson down as a peer-propagate record so a later agent can find and apply it. Each record cites where the lesson came from, where it applies, where it does not, and how confident it is. It is held as candidate knowledge passed between agents, kept distinct from the operator's own words, from private memory, and from the system's active rules.","reader_rule":"Treat a peer-propagate record as one agent's tested advice to the next, not as a settled rule: check what it says it applies to and where it says it does not before leaning on it.","reader_deep":"It sits deliberately below the formal lanes, a skill, standard, paper module, or established principle, and stays marked as a candidate so that useful working knowledge survives between agents without being mistaken for the operator's voice or for confirmed doctrine."},{"object_id":"term:perfect_set","preferred_label":"perfect set","reader_preview":"A set is perfect when it is closed and has no isolated points, so every point in it is approached arbitrarily closely by other points of the set.","reader_card":"Perfectness rules out the picture in which the set is a scatter of separate points. It is one of the exact structural facts established about the achievement sets in Erd\u0151s #257, alongside their coding, topology, and measure.","reader_rule":"Perfect is a topological property of the set. It does not tell you which numbers belong to it.","reader_deep":"The restricted achievement sets for Erd\u0151s #257 receive exact coding, topology, perfectness, and measure statements, each with its finite or infinite hypothesis attached. The hypotheses matter: results proved for finite support do not carry to infinite support."},{"object_id":"term:periodic","preferred_label":"periodic","reader_preview":"A sequence is periodic when a fixed block repeats forever, and eventually periodic when such repetition begins after a finite initial segment.","reader_card":"The period is the smallest positive shift that preserves the repeating tail. Positional digit expansions of rational numbers are eventually periodic, which makes forbidden repetition a route to proving irrationality.","reader_rule":"Check whether periodicity begins immediately or only eventually and whether the claimed period is minimal.","reader_deep":"Finite data can be consistent with a long period or a repetition beginning beyond the observed range, so no finite prefix alone rules out eventual periodicity."},{"object_id":"term:phase","preferred_label":"phase","reader_preview":"A phase is a scoped work packet inside a family of related work: a bounded unit of execution that holds one objective, the active wave of work, the references it draws on, and the state needed to pause and resume it later.","reader_card":"A phase is how the system breaks a broad area of work into a single resumable unit. Each phase narrows a wide family objective into a concrete sequence of waves, revisions, ledgers, and a closeout record, and it preserves the current objective, the execution mode, the active wave, and the legal next steps so the work can be picked up exactly where it was left.","reader_rule":"When you meet a phase, read it as the live container for one stretch of work: it tells you what is currently being done, how far it has got, and what may happen next, rather than describing the system as a whole.","reader_deep":"A phase is temporary by design. It exists to carry a piece of work from its starting objective through its waves and revisions to a recorded closeout, after which it can be archived."},{"object_id":"term:pip","preferred_label":"pip","reader_preview":"pip is Python's package installer, the command that fetches a project's dependencies and puts them where Python can find them.","reader_card":"Square brackets after a package name select an optional group of extra dependencies, so pip install with a test extra pulls in the packages the test suite needs and a plain install does not.","reader_rule":"An optional extra is not installed by default, so a lane that needs one will silently have nothing to run without it.","reader_deep":"The quickstart route no longer asks for an install on first contact. The August 2026 rerun receipt is dated for that reason: two of its four commands needed an install the repository no longer requires."},{"object_id":"term:planted_error","preferred_label":"planted error","reader_preview":"A planted error is a fault introduced on purpose to check that a detector notices it.","reader_card":"A check that has never caught anything has not been shown to work. Planting an error and confirming it is rejected is the difference between a gate that runs and a gate that discriminates. This site records one such case: a deliberately false claim was introduced after a real failure had been repaired, and the repaired gate refused it.","reader_rule":"Ask what a detector has actually caught. An untested detector is an assumption.","reader_deep":"The honest version of this reports both halves: the real failure that got through first, and the planted case that the repair then caught. Reporting only the second would make a repair look like foresight."},{"object_id":"term:planted_forgery","preferred_label":"planted forgery","reader_preview":"A planted forgery is a deliberately false claim placed in a test input so that a check can be observed refusing it.","reader_card":"Watching a check pass on correct input shows only that it does not object. Watching it reject a known-false input by name shows it can tell the difference, which is the harder half.","reader_rule":"A run that plants a forgery and does not report a refusal has failed, even when it reports a pass.","reader_deep":"The completion-faithfulness audit runs against notes carrying three planted forgeries: a fake commit claim, a fake test claim, and a fake work-record claim. The external verification job uses the same idea, running a second fixture with one statement altered and counting as passed only when the checker rejects it by name."},{"object_id":"term:plectis","preferred_label":"Plectis","reader_preview":"Plectis is the public name of this project: an open-source mathematics effort that publishes papers, Lean proofs, and software, with digestion and formal verification as the stated priorities.","reader_card":"Two public repositories sit under this name. The Lean repository holds the mathematics: formal proofs, short notes, longer working records, and open questions. A separate software repository holds reusable research components. This site is the reading surface that connects both. Older paths, package names, and URLs still say Microcosm; those are this same project under the name it used before.","reader_rule":"Read Plectis as the public project name. If you see Microcosm in a path, package, or old link, treat it as the same project, not a second one.","reader_deep":"The name comes from Latin plectere, to weave. It names the public cut shown on this site and on GitHub. What the system actually is lives in the source files, standards, and validators; renaming the public identity did not rename every package id or old URL."},{"object_id":"term:positive_integer","preferred_label":"positive integer","reader_preview":"A positive integer is an integer greater than zero.","reader_card":"The positive integers are 1, 2, 3, and so on, excluding zero and all negative integers. Formal statements often use positivity to permit division, ordering, or finite counting arguments.","reader_rule":"Check whether the domain is positive integers, nonnegative integers, or all integers.","reader_deep":"A positivity hypothesis can be essential even when it looks routine, because removing it may introduce zero denominators or sign changes."},{"object_id":"term:prime","preferred_label":"prime","reader_preview":"A prime is a whole number greater than one divisible only by itself and one.","reader_card":"Primes appear in several of these problems as the rule generating the terms of a series, either directly or through the gaps between consecutive primes. That makes the series inherit every difficulty of the primes themselves, which is a large part of why the questions are hard.","reader_rule":"A series built from primes is at least as hard as what is known about primes.","reader_deep":"Results about such series are often conditional on facts about prime distribution that are themselves deep, which is why several claims here rest on stated analytic hypotheses rather than standing alone."},{"object_id":"term:prime_gap","preferred_label":"prime gap","reader_preview":"A prime gap is the difference between two consecutive prime numbers.","reader_card":"Prime gaps vary irregularly: most are small compared with the primes themselves, but arbitrarily large gaps occur. A series built from them inherits hard questions about how primes are distributed.","reader_rule":"Check whether a statement concerns every gap, average behaviour, infinitely many gaps, or a bounded finite range.","reader_deep":"Writing consecutive primes as a starting prime plus accumulated gaps can transform a prime-generated series into an equivalent gap-generated series with different proof handles."},{"object_id":"term:principle","preferred_label":"principle","reader_preview":"A principle is a durable operating law that constrains how agents and the system behave, stored as a numbered rule with a written rationale.","reader_card":"A principle states a rule that holds across many cases rather than a fix for one situation, such as \"status fails closed\" or \"derive before you assert\". Each principle carries not just the rule but the design instinct behind it: the underlying tradeoff and the reasoning a later decision should reuse when a new case differs from the one that first prompted it. Principles are more stable than a single observation and more general than a mechanism, and each is grounded in recorded evidence and a curation history.","reader_rule":"Read a principle as a standing commitment the system holds itself to, and look at the rule it states and the reason given for it, not just its label.","reader_deep":"A principle is one layer in a doctrine hierarchy: above a local note or a one-off observation, below nothing it cannot still be traced back to, and always anchored to the evidence and curation that promoted it."},{"object_id":"term:problem_note","preferred_label":"problem note","reader_preview":"A problem note is a short standalone paper on a single open mathematical problem, stating what has been formally proved, what remains open, and where the boundary between them lies.","reader_card":"The notes exist because a formal proof and a readable account of it are different artefacts, and the gap between them is where overstatement lives. Each note is written for a mathematician reading cold, records the exact range proved rather than the impression it creates, and names the adjacent stronger statement that is still open.","reader_rule":"Read a problem note for the boundary, not the headline. The interesting content is what it declines to claim.","reader_deep":"Keeping the note honest is itself checked: the relationship between a note's public wording and the formal statement behind it is registered, so a later edit that erases a distinction fails rather than passing quietly."},{"object_id":"term:producer","preferred_label":"producer","reader_preview":"A producer is the untrusted generator of a claim or certificate; acceptance must come from a separate small checker, not from the producer itself.","reader_card":"The doctrine lede repeats that a result is independent of the producer. An AI model, a human author, or a build script can emit a certificate. The checker that recomputes from a named basis is a different actor. Trusting the emitter because the sentence is fluent is the failure the rule is written against.","reader_rule":"When a page names a producer, look for the checker and the named basis before treating the emission as accepted.","reader_deep":"AX-2 is the public form: independently of the producer. A typed refusal is what the checker returns when it cannot honestly accept. The producer may still be correct; the point is that correctness is not self-certified."},{"object_id":"term:product","preferred_label":"product","reader_preview":"In Plectis, product usually means a user-facing software offering or capability; in mathematical passages, a product is the result of multiplication.","reader_card":"The site actively uses both senses. Product performance, product quality, and product authority concern a software offering and what may be claimed about it. Prime products, running-LCM products, and product formulae concern factors multiplied together. One sense supplies no evidence about the other.","reader_rule":"Read the surrounding noun: performance, launch, quality, or authority indicates the software sense; factors, primes, LCM, or a formula indicates multiplication.","reader_deep":"This is a real vocabulary collision in Plectis, not a local redefinition of either ordinary meaning. The popup therefore keeps both senses visible so mathematical language cannot silently overwrite product-governance prose."},{"object_id":"term:profile_gap","preferred_label":"profile gap","reader_preview":"A profile gap is a recorded admission that an artifact kind exists but lacks the supported summary and card rows needed for honest row-by-row browsing, so navigation shows the gap instead of pretending the option surface is complete.","reader_card":"The system arranges its artifacts into kinds and lets each kind be enumerated as stable rows that can be selected and drilled into. A profile gap marks a kind that has a registry, source files, or generated index entries but no fully supported rows yet, so it is visible at the top level without being claimed as fully navigable. It is a deliberate marker of navigation debt: a way of saying the support is incomplete rather than papering over the difference between data that exists and a surface that can honestly list it.","reader_rule":"Treat a profile gap as an honest \"not yet browsable here\" sign, not a defect: the kind is real and visible, but its row-level listing has not been finished, so do not expect to enumerate or open every member of it.","reader_deep":"It exists to stop a kind's mere presence (a registry entry, a legacy command, a generated index, or raw data bands) from being mistaken for full navigability until the navigation layer can actually enumerate and open its members by stable identifier."},{"object_id":"term:program","preferred_label":"program","reader_preview":"Program can mean executable computer instructions or an organized initiative such as a bug-bounty program; Plectis uses both senses.","reader_card":"Most component pages use program for code that a machine runs. Policy and disclosure pages can instead use program for an organized activity with participants, rules, and a purpose. The initiative does not execute merely because it shares the word.","reader_rule":"Commands, inputs, source code, and runtime indicate the executable sense; participation, eligibility, disclosure, or administration indicate the initiative sense.","reader_deep":"Plectis needs the distinction because a component may be a computer program while the vulnerability-disclosure page explicitly says the project is not a bug-bounty program. Conflating them turns a scope disclaimer into a claim about executable software."},{"object_id":"term:projection","preferred_label":"projection","reader_preview":"A projection is a derived view generated from one or more source artifacts to make selected facts easier to read, query, or navigate without becoming their authority.","reader_card":"A projection selects, reorganizes, or compresses source material into a useful surface such as a page, index, card, or report. Because it can become stale, a trustworthy projection records its inputs, builder, source fingerprint, and refresh path.","reader_rule":"Use a projection for access and orientation, then follow its source pointer when the underlying fact or authority matters.","reader_deep":"Projection quality has two independent dimensions: fidelity to the source and usefulness to the reader; a perfectly accurate dump may be unusable, while a beautiful summary may be dangerously incomplete."},{"object_id":"term:projection_drift","preferred_label":"projection drift","reader_preview":"Projection drift is what happens when a copy stops matching the source it was made from.","reader_card":"Every page on this site is generated from the repositories behind it, so a stale copy would say something the source no longer supports without anything visibly breaking. Drift is dangerous precisely because it is quiet.","reader_rule":"When a copy and its source disagree, the source is the authority. A copy that agrees with its source proves nothing about whether the source is right.","reader_deep":"One group of components exists to recompute a copied projection against its source and fail when the two differ, which is also the rule the videos state: if the map and the source disagree, the source wins."},{"object_id":"term:proof","preferred_label":"proof","reader_preview":"A proof is a logically valid argument showing that a mathematical statement follows from stated definitions, assumptions, and previously established results.","reader_card":"A human proof communicates the reasoning to qualified readers; a formal proof encodes enough detail for a proof checker. Both establish only their exact conclusion under their exact assumptions.","reader_rule":"Read the theorem statement and dependencies before treating a proof as settling a broader claim.","reader_deep":"Proof validity is different from exposition quality, novelty, significance, or whether the theorem solves the motivating open problem."},{"object_id":"term:proof_assistant","preferred_label":"proof assistant","reader_preview":"A proof assistant is a program that verifies mathematical proofs mechanically, requiring every step to be justified in a form it can check.","reader_card":"Writing mathematics for a proof assistant is slower than writing it for a reader, because nothing can be left to the reader. That cost buys a specific guarantee: if it compiles, the argument holds. The tool is honest about scope in a way prose rarely is, since a proof that only covers a finite range simply will not check for the general one.","reader_rule":"Treat a machine-checked result as settled for what it states, and no further.","reader_deep":"Proof assistants make gaps visible rather than making authors careful. An argument with a hole does not compile, so the hole is found at writing time instead of at review time."},{"object_id":"term:proof_boundary","preferred_label":"proof boundary","reader_preview":"A proof boundary is the line between what a formal proof establishes and what the prose around it claims: the point where machine-checked certainty stops and ordinary description begins.","reader_card":"A proof assistant checks that a proof establishes the formal statement written in the source. It does not check that a README or a paper describes that statement faithfully. The proof boundary is where those two diverge, and it is the gap where a correct proof can sit underneath an overstated summary without anything failing.","reader_rule":"For any formal result, find the formal statement and read it. The prose is a description of the proof, not part of it.","reader_deep":"This site records one real instance: an edit that erased a finite-versus-open distinction passed, because the relationship between wording and formal statement had not been registered. Registering it made a later deliberately false version fail. That is one failure and one repair, not complete claim discovery."},{"object_id":"term:proof_checker","preferred_label":"proof checker","reader_preview":"A proof checker is the small trusted program that accepts a formal proof only when every encoded inference follows from the system's rules and stated assumptions.","reader_card":"The checker does not invent the proof or judge whether the formal statement captures the intended mathematics; it verifies the derivation it receives. Keeping this trusted core small makes the result less dependent on large generators, tactics, or user interfaces.","reader_rule":"Separate what the checker verified from what translators, generators, and theorem authors supplied to it.","reader_deep":"In Lean, elaboration and automation may be complex, but accepted declarations are replayed through a compact kernel whose rules define the final formal trust boundary."},{"object_id":"term:proposition","preferred_label":"proposition","reader_preview":"A proposition is a declarative mathematical statement that has a definite truth value once its terms and assumptions are fixed.","reader_card":"A proposition can be a theorem, lemma, conjecture, or intermediate claim depending on whether and how it has been proved. Its wording, not its label, determines what must be established.","reader_rule":"Separate the proposition's exact statement from its proof status.","reader_deep":"Formal systems represent propositions as types whose inhabitants are proofs, making hypotheses and logical structure explicit."},{"object_id":"term:provenance","preferred_label":"provenance","reader_preview":"Provenance is the record of where something came from: which source a copied file was taken from, and whether it still matches.","reader_card":"Copies drift from their sources silently. Provenance is what makes that detectable rather than discovered later, by recording the origin alongside the copy and checking the two still agree. It is the reason a published body can be trusted to be the real implementation rather than an old snapshot of it.","reader_rule":"For any copied artefact, ask where it came from and whether that link is still checked.","reader_deep":"Provenance is more durable than correctness. A wrong thing with recorded provenance can be traced and repaired; a right thing without it cannot be maintained."},{"object_id":"term:pytest","preferred_label":"pytest","reader_preview":"pytest is the standard tool for running automated tests in Python projects.","reader_card":"It reports an exit status: zero when every test passed, non-zero otherwise. A check that runs pytest and then ignores that status has learned nothing, which is a failure mode this system explicitly looks for.","reader_rule":"Running a test suite is not the same as checking its result. Look for the exit status.","reader_deep":"The completion-faithfulness audit runs one real pytest process and checks its exit status explicitly. That lane needs the test extra installed; a plain install leaves it nothing to run."},{"object_id":"term:queryable","preferred_label":"queryable","reader_preview":"Queryable means a reader or assistant can find the thing by asking or searching, without already knowing the path to it.","reader_card":"The AI-reader section describes a packet as a hologram you can zoom with a queryable microscope, and mathematical statements as queryable rows. The glossary search box is the same idea at smaller scale: type exogenous, mining, guinea pig, or CS and land on the card. If a word on the public site cannot be found that way, it is not yet queryable here, however well it is defined in someone's head.","reader_rule":"A surface is queryable when a name, alias, or search handle actually retrieves it. A definition that cannot be found is not available to a cold reader.","reader_deep":"Queryable is not the same as verified, and not the same as understood. It is the prior condition that a question can be aimed. Packets, the glossary, and site search exist so an assistant or a person can retrieve a row rather than reread the whole site. The act of looking still has to happen."},{"object_id":"term:quickstart","preferred_label":"quickstart","reader_preview":"The quickstart is the shortest path from nothing to a first result: clone, install, run one check, read the file it wrote.","reader_card":"It exists so that the first thing a reader does is produce evidence rather than read more prose. Everything else on the site is easier to evaluate once you have run something and seen what it writes.","reader_rule":"Run the quickstart before evaluating any claim here. It takes minutes and changes what you are able to check.","reader_deep":"The shape is deliberate: the reader ends holding a file they produced, which is a different epistemic position from having read a description of one."},{"object_id":"term:radix","preferred_label":"radix","reader_preview":"A radix is the base of a positional numeral system, so a radix-two expansion is binary and a radix-ten expansion is ordinary decimal.","reader_card":"Choosing a radix fixes which digits are available and therefore what carrying looks like. Several arguments in this corpus turn on exactly which digit values can occur.","reader_rule":"A digit-level result is stated relative to one radix and does not transfer to another without proof.","reader_deep":"The Erd\u0151s #269 work gives an exact running-lcm product, logarithmic cells, shell bounds, and a four-value radix at the primes 2, 3 and 5, together with a rank-two nonseparability witness. The remaining criterion is conditional and the problem stays open from three primes onward."},{"object_id":"term:rational","preferred_label":"rational","reader_preview":"A number is rational when it can be written as one whole number divided by another.","reader_card":"Rationality is the property most of these problems are trying to rule out. It is a strong constraint: if an infinite sum is rational, its value has an exact denominator, and that denominator forces detectable structure. Much of the formal work here consists of showing that the required structure cannot occur.","reader_rule":"Assuming rationality and deriving a contradiction is the standard route to proving irrationality.","reader_deep":"The reason rationality is attacked rather than irrationality proved directly is that rationality gives you something finite to work with, and irrationality gives you nothing to hold."},{"object_id":"term:raw_seed","preferred_label":"raw seed","reader_preview":"A raw seed is the append-only, unedited record of the operator's own words and thinking, serving as the source layer from which later summaries, doctrine, and routes are derived.","reader_card":"The raw seed holds the operator's voice in its first form: the intent, the recurring phrases, the changes of mind, and the provenance of an idea, written down before any tidying. Indexes, shards, principles, and paper modules elsewhere in the system are summaries drawn from it; the raw seed is the thing they summarise, and it is never overwritten or compressed away. When a higher layer needs to be checked against what was actually meant, the raw seed is the bottom authority it answers to.","reader_rule":"Treat the raw seed as the original wording and the summaries above it as maps of that wording. When the two seem to disagree, the raw seed is the record of what was actually meant.","reader_deep":"Because it is append-only, the raw seed preserves the full history of an idea, including reversals and recurring motifs, so a later summary can be traced back to the exact source it came from."},{"object_id":"term:reader","preferred_label":"reader","reader_preview":"A reader is the person or software consumer trying to understand, navigate, or use a published artifact.","reader_card":"Reader needs depend on prior context: a cold reader starts without the repository's house vocabulary, while an expert may need exact source and proof routes. Public pages try to serve both through layered definitions and drilldowns.","reader_rule":"Choose the shallowest explanation that makes the next step clear, while preserving a route to deeper evidence.","reader_deep":"A software reader can consume structured packets and pointers that would be awkward for a person, but it still needs definitions, authority boundaries, and unambiguous identifiers."},{"object_id":"term:reader_packet","preferred_label":"reader packet","reader_preview":"A reader packet is a generated, portable guide that gathers routes, short summaries, source references, and scope limits so a person or AI assistant can inspect a public source slice without mistaking the guide for that source.","reader_card":"A reader packet is the compact starting document handed to someone, or to an automated assistant, who is opening the public slice for the first time. It carries the routes worth following, brief descriptions of components and glossary rows, links back to the underlying source files, and an explicit note of what it does and does not cover. It is reproducible and built from the source, so its job is to point at where to inspect rather than to stand in for the records it summarises.","reader_rule":"Follow a reader packet through to the source records it links rather than treating its summaries as the final word. It tells you where to look and where its own boundaries are.","reader_deep":"A reader packet is built to cut cold-start confusion without turning its own summary text into authority: it is compact and source-linked precisely so that its claims can be checked against the records it routes to."},{"object_id":"term:real_number","preferred_label":"real number","reader_preview":"A real number is a point on the continuous number line, including every rational and irrational number.","reader_card":"Plectis first treats the value of a convergent series as a real number and then asks the narrower arithmetic question: is that real value rational or irrational? A decimal approximation locates the value but does not settle that exact classification.","reader_rule":"Separate existence as a real limit from the stronger claim that the limit is rational, irrational, algebraic, or transcendental.","reader_deep":"Finite certificate computations may live in exact rational arithmetic while the completed infinite series lives in the reals. Moving between those levels requires an explicit limit argument and, in formal work, explicit type coercions; a rational partial sum does not make the real limit rational."},{"object_id":"term:receipt","preferred_label":"receipt","reader_preview":"A receipt is the durable output that proves an action happened: the file or record a step writes, kept so the claim can be checked later rather than taken on trust.","reader_card":"Actions here are expected to leave something behind. A commit reports a hash, a deploy reports the bytes it verified live, a check writes its result record. The pattern is deliberate, because the failure it guards against is the confident report of work that did not occur. A receipt turns that from a matter of trust into something you can look up.","reader_rule":"When a step is reported as done, ask what it wrote. No receipt, no completion.","reader_deep":"The discipline extends to failure. A refused action produces a receipt too, naming what stopped it, which is what stops a blocked step from being quietly reported as finished."},{"object_id":"term:reciprocal","preferred_label":"reciprocal","reader_preview":"The reciprocal of a number is one divided by it.","reader_card":"Most of the series here are sums of reciprocals, which is why the terms shrink and the sums settle on a value at all. Taking reciprocals also inverts the difficulty: a fast-growing sequence gives a fast-shrinking sum, and the arithmetic of the sum depends delicately on how the original sequence grew.","reader_rule":"In a reciprocal sum, how fast the underlying sequence grows controls almost everything.","reader_deep":"The delicacy is the point: sequences that grow at similar rates can give sums of entirely different character, so growth conditions have to be stated exactly."},{"object_id":"term:record","preferred_label":"record","reader_preview":"A record is a durable file or row you can reopen later to see what was checked, produced, or routed.","reader_card":"JSON packets, result files, and receipts are inspectable records: each names what produced it and where its authority stops, and sits below its owning source rather than replacing it. Reading those objects is not the same as running the checks that produced them.","reader_rule":"When a page points you to a record, open it and read what it actually preserves and where its scope ends. A record holds a checkable object; it does not make a claim true merely by existing.","reader_deep":"In this system a record may be a source row, a generated projection, a receipt, a ledger entry, or a public packet. Each is durable and reopenable, and each ranks below the source that produced it."},{"object_id":"term:recurrence","preferred_label":"recurrence","reader_preview":"A recurrence is a rule defining each term of a sequence from the ones before it, rather than by a direct formula.","reader_card":"Recurrences appear here on both sides of the work. Some problems ask whether a sequence must eventually satisfy a particular recurrence; elsewhere, assuming a sum is rational forces a recurrence in its long division, which then gives something concrete to contradict.","reader_rule":"A forced recurrence is usually a proof strategy rather than a result: it is the structure the contradiction will come from.","reader_deep":"The reason recurrences are useful in irrationality arguments is that they propagate: establish one at a single index and it constrains every later one."},{"object_id":"term:reduction","preferred_label":"reduction","reader_preview":"A reduction turns one problem into another, so that solving the second would solve the first.","reader_card":"A reduction is not progress on its own. If the new statement is no easier than the original, the problem has been restated rather than advanced. Several results in this corpus are exact reformulations and are labelled as such rather than presented as progress.","reader_rule":"Count results, not restatements. Statements each proved equivalent to a problem are one result about that problem, not many.","reader_deep":"The claim registry records 39 of its 103 claims as conditional reductions, and separately records that neither Erd\u0151s #249 nor Erd\u0151s #257 is settled. The Lean repository also lifts the hypotheses of its conditional theorems into standalone propositions and proves 17 of the 23 substantial ones equal in strength to the open problem they were meant to approach."},{"object_id":"term:reference","preferred_label":"reference","reader_preview":"A reference is a pointer that identifies another source, record, definition, or location relevant to the current statement.","reader_card":"References make claims traceable by naming where supporting or explanatory material can be found. A reference may provide context without itself being evidence for the claim.","reader_rule":"Follow the reference and inspect the role assigned to its destination.","reader_deep":"Exact references include stable identifiers, paths, anchors, versions, or citations so later readers can resolve the same object rather than a nearby substitute."},{"object_id":"term:reformulation","preferred_label":"reformulation","reader_preview":"A reformulation restates a problem or theorem in a different representation while preserving a proved relationship to the original statement.","reader_card":"A reformulation can expose new structure (digits instead of sums, recurrences instead of rationality, or gaps instead of primes) without solving anything by itself. Its value depends on whether the new form is equivalent, merely implied, or only heuristically related.","reader_rule":"Check the exact relation between the original and reformulated statements before treating them as interchangeable.","reader_deep":"Productive reformulations preserve the target while moving the difficulty into objects with stronger tools, clearer barriers, or finite certificates."},{"object_id":"term:registry","preferred_label":"registry","reader_preview":"A registry is a governed collection of uniquely identified records for one kind of object.","reader_card":"A registry defines membership, identifiers, and required fields for a class of artifacts. Generated indexes and pages should project from it rather than maintain competing lists.","reader_rule":"Use the registry to answer which records exist and which identifier names each one.","reader_deep":"Registries make additions, removals, and duplicates mechanically visible, but the truth of each record's substantive claims still depends on its own evidence."},{"object_id":"term:rename_migration_receipt","preferred_label":"rename migration receipt","reader_preview":"A rename migration receipt is the durable proof that a storage-path rename passed its gate, recording the evidence, obligations, rollback plan, or, when blocked, the unmet precondition that prevented the change.","reader_card":"When a file or directory in the source is renamed, the receipt is the artifact that makes that rename auditable and reversible. It records the scope of the change, the evidence that the rename gate was satisfied, the post-rename tasks, and closure notes. Receipts move through fixed states, planned, blocked, in progress, completed, and reverted, so the history of a rename is legible from a single object rather than reconstructed from the file system. A completed receipt is evidence the migration happened safely; a blocked receipt is the honest record that it is not yet safe to proceed.","reader_rule":"Check a rename migration receipt's state and its named gate evidence before treating a rename as settled. A blocked receipt means the change was deliberately not made, and it names the precondition that is still missing.","reader_deep":"Each receipt forces the reference-impact map, stable-identifier plan, alias and redirect plan, projection-rebuild plan, verification plan, and rollback plan to be written down before the file system is touched, and the actual outcome to be recorded afterwards."},{"object_id":"term:replay","preferred_label":"replay","reader_preview":"A replay is a check that re-runs a recorded scenario end to end and asserts the outcome, rather than testing a function in isolation.","reader_card":"Unit-level checks tell you a part behaves; a replay tells you a situation resolves the way it is supposed to. The scenario is committed as fixtures, including the cases that must fail, so the whole path can be re-run by anyone and compared against a recorded expectation. It is the closest this repository gets to showing a behaviour rather than asserting one.","reader_rule":"Read what scenario a replay covers. It establishes that recorded path, not the behaviour of the system around it.","reader_deep":"Replays are where negative cases earn their place: a scenario that only ever replays its happy path proves very little, because a component that has stopped discriminating still completes it. The recorded refusals are what make the pass informative."},{"object_id":"term:repository","preferred_label":"repository","reader_preview":"A repository is a project's files together with their complete change history, hosted so anyone can copy the whole thing.","reader_card":"Two public GitHub repositories sit under this project: plectis for the runnable software, and plectis-erdos for the Lean mathematics. Everything claimed on this site should be traceable to a file in one of them. The history matters as much as the files, because it shows when something was written rather than only that it exists now.","reader_rule":"If a claim cannot be traced to a repository file, treat it as description.","reader_deep":"History is what makes a claim of priority checkable. A file can be written at any time; a commit is dated."},{"object_id":"term:reproducible","preferred_label":"reproducible","reader_preview":"Reproducible means another reader can use the declared source, inputs, environment, and command to obtain the same materially relevant result.","reader_card":"Reproducibility turns a reported outcome into something independently checkable. It requires enough information to rebuild or rerun the work, plus a criterion for what must match: exact bytes, the same verdict, or values within a stated tolerance.","reader_rule":"A reproducibility claim should name the inputs, versions, command, expected output, and acceptable comparison rule.","reader_deep":"Exact byte reproduction is stronger than semantic reproduction, but not every valid workflow can be byte-identical across platforms; the claim must state which level it promises."},{"object_id":"term:research_shift","preferred_label":"research shift","reader_preview":"A research shift is a bounded task a person gives their coding agent: start from one exact public revision, attempt a continuation on one problem, and return a checkable result.","reader_card":"The Lean repository's frontier relay guide defines the shift: inspect the pinned revision, choose a frontier, work, and prepare a return the maintainer can check locally, with contributor credit attached. It needs no account and no access to the private system that produced the corpus.","reader_rule":"Judge a shift by the checkable return it produces, not by how long the agent ran.","reader_deep":"A return can be a theorem, a narrower reduction, a closed route, a source correction or a better next question; each is a contribution if it survives review."},{"object_id":"term:result_record","preferred_label":"result record","reader_preview":"A result record is the durable file a check writes with what it tested, what it found, and the boundary of that finding, as distinct from an ephemeral message shown while the run executes.","reader_card":"Running a component does not just print something and exit - it writes a result record you can open, keep, and compare against a later run. This is what makes a claim on this site checkable rather than quotable: the record is the evidence, and prose describing it is not. A result record carries its own scope limit, so it states the boundary of what it establishes instead of leaving that to the reader.","reader_rule":"Prefer the result record to any summary of it. If a claim has no record behind it, treat it as description, not evidence.","reader_deep":"Result records are metadata-only by design: they carry the shape and verdict of a run, not the private bodies it read. That is what lets a run over non-public inputs still produce a publishable artefact. It is also why a record can be honest about a failure - an exit-1 record is as publishable as a passing one, and the repository ships both."},{"object_id":"term:reviewer_brief","preferred_label":"reviewer brief","reader_preview":"The reviewer brief is the small generated JSON packet a reader can hand the public site to an assistant in one turn.","reader_card":"Downloading the reviewer brief writes plectis-reviewer-brief.json. That small packet is distinct from the full AI review packet. Both are holograms of the public site at different depths. If they disagree with a live page, the live page and the repository win.","reader_rule":"Use the reviewer brief for a first pass. Switch to the full packet or the pages when the brief is too thin for the question.","reader_deep":"The brief is generated, so it can lag a rebuild. It is a compression of the public records, not a second authority. Asking an assistant to review Plectis from the brief still requires that assistant to stay inside those records and to stop where the records stop."},{"object_id":"term:rigidity","preferred_label":"rigidity","reader_preview":"A rigidity result says that an object satisfying a few loose conditions is forced to be one specific thing.","reader_card":"Rigidity is what makes a classification possible: instead of describing many candidates you show there was only ever one. The work is usually in ruling out the near-misses.","reader_rule":"A rigidity theorem is only as strong as its hypotheses, so read which conditions are assumed before reading what is forced.","reader_deep":"Erd\u0151s #243 is stated here as rigidity of a centred integer error orbit. Lean excludes eventually negative constant magnitude and positive-drift periodic magnitude, and proves that normalised vanishing plus an eventually bounded negative part forces the error to vanish. The negative-part bound remains the missing hypothesis."},{"object_id":"term:routing","preferred_label":"routing","reader_preview":"Routing is the path-selection step that maps a query, task, file path, or state to the smallest useful answer surface or owning work lane before source is opened.","reader_card":"The system holds far more surfaces, such as documents, components, paper modules, standards, and source records, than anyone can open directly. Routing turns a situation into a ranked, source-linked set of options, each with a reason and a cost, so the right starting point can be chosen first. It selects the path; it does not make the chosen summary authoritative, and the underlying source still has to be opened to confirm what it claims.","reader_rule":"When a page or tool routes you somewhere, treat that as a suggested starting point with its stated reason, not a final answer; the surface it points to is where the real content lives.","reader_deep":"Routing emphasises selection, choosing which option surface to enter, whereas navigation emphasises traversing the surface once it has been selected."},{"object_id":"term:row_job","preferred_label":"row job","reader_preview":"A row job is one predeclared, bounded change against a named row, finding, or artifact slice, recording the target, operation, allowed scope, expected delta, required check, and final receipt before any worker runs it.","reader_card":"Rather than handing a broad instruction like \"repair every finding\" or \"use the providers\" straight to an automated worker, the system first breaks that pressure into row jobs. Each row job pins down exactly one target and one operation, states the authority ceiling it must stay within, the validation it has to satisfy, and the gate it must clear before its result is promoted. It is a description of one bounded task, settled in advance, not the act of running it.","reader_rule":"Read a row job as the full specification of one small, scoped change: what it touches, what it is permitted to do, and the check it must pass. It governs that one task and grants no wider authority over the system.","reader_deep":"Row jobs are what let the work scale safely across cheap automated workers and external providers: because each job already names its target, limits, and validation, a worker receives a bounded task instead of an open-ended question about the whole body of files."},{"object_id":"term:rowability","preferred_label":"rowability","reader_preview":"Rowability means an artifact kind can be browsed as stable, source-linked rows instead of found by guessed keywords.","reader_card":"Rowability is the test of whether a category of objects in the system, such as its components or its paper modules, is ready to be navigated as a list. A rowable kind has a place in the system's index of kinds, gives every item a stable id, exposes short summary and detail views without expensive work, marks each item's source and freshness, and states honestly anything it has left out, so the items can be reached by browsing and selecting rather than by guessing search terms. A registry or a source file simply existing is not enough: the kind becomes rowable only once it can be listed, selected, and traced back to source in this way.","reader_rule":"When a kind on this site is described as rowable, you can browse and select its rows directly rather than searching for them; follow any row back to its named source before treating the short summary as the full truth.","reader_deep":"Rowability separates the cheap, listable summary of an item from the source it stands for: a compressed row may omit detail, but it must say so and point back to authority, so a row is never mistaken for the complete object."},{"object_id":"term:run","preferred_label":"run","reader_preview":"A run is one execution of a program, command, test, experiment, or governed process.","reader_card":"A run is one execution you can point at: a command, a test, a Lean check, an experiment. Reading a page is not a verification run; a check counts as passed only when the stated command was run and its evidence shown. Ran is the past tense of the same fact. Repeated runs may differ when time, environment, or inputs differ.","reader_rule":"Read a run result with its exact command or procedure, inputs, environment, and exit status.","reader_deep":"A result from one run is historical evidence; reproducibility asks whether the same conditions can produce an equivalent result again. Search should find this card from run, ran, or running. The everyday verb is not auto-linked through every sentence on the site, because that would turn a definition into underline texture; the card remains queryable."},{"object_id":"term:runnable","preferred_label":"runnable","reader_preview":"Runnable means a component ships everything needed to execute it locally: the code, its inputs, and a command, so a reader can produce the result rather than read about it.","reader_card":"It is the difference between a repository you can inspect and one you can exercise. Runnable requires that the fixtures are committed, the dependencies are declared, and the command is written down, which is a stricter condition than the code being public. A cold clone is how the claim gets tested.","reader_rule":"Check whether something is runnable before treating its result as reproducible. Published is not the same as runnable.","reader_deep":"The constraint shapes what can be published at all: a component whose real inputs are private is only runnable against synthetic fixtures, and its scope limit has to say so rather than implying the wider case."},{"object_id":"term:runtime","preferred_label":"runtime","reader_preview":"A runtime is the code and execution context active while a program or workflow is actually running, as distinct from its source files and static descriptions.","reader_card":"Runtime behaviour includes the processes, state transitions, tool calls, permissions, and external interactions that occur during execution. A page or source inspection can describe that behaviour but cannot prove it happened without a run record.","reader_rule":"Treat runtime claims as claims about observed execution and look for a trace, result record, or live probe.","reader_deep":"The same source can exhibit different runtime behaviour under different inputs, environments, permissions, or concurrent activity, which is why runtime evidence is tied to one recorded execution."},{"object_id":"term:schema","preferred_label":"schema","reader_preview":"A schema is a machine-readable contract describing which fields a structured record may or must contain, their types, and the constraints between them.","reader_card":"Schemas make malformed or incomplete data fail explicitly instead of being interpreted differently by each consumer. They validate shape, not meaning: a perfectly schema-valid claim can still be false or unsupported.","reader_rule":"Use a schema to check record structure and a separate validator or evidence rule to check the record's content.","reader_deep":"A schema can encode required fields, enumerated statuses, identifier formats, nested objects, and cross-field constraints, giving producers and consumers a shared contract that can evolve by version."},{"object_id":"term:scope","preferred_label":"scope","reader_preview":"Scope is the declared set of objects, cases, operations, time, and conditions to which a statement, check, permission, or piece of work applies.","reader_card":"Scope says what is inside a result before its limit says where the result stops. A precise scope names the relevant inputs, source version, environment, operations, and exclusions so a local finding cannot be mistaken for a universal one.","reader_rule":"Before using a result, compare its declared scope with the case you want to apply it to.","reader_deep":"Scope is multidimensional: two results may mention the same component while differing in tested inputs, runtime, date, authority, or permitted action, and those differences decide whether the evidence transfers."},{"object_id":"term:scope_limit","preferred_label":"scope limit","reader_preview":"A scope limit is the explicit boundary between what a unit's evidence establishes and what that unit is not allowed to assert.","reader_card":"Every component card labels a Scope limit. Doctrine cards and the landing specimen do the same. It marks the edge of what the supporting evidence covers, so a real result stays a claim about exactly what was checked. A local fixture can pass while the scope limit still rules out launch readiness, whole-system correctness, hosted-service behaviour, professional advice, or private runtime state.","reader_rule":"When a result is shown, read the Scope limit field beside it. The result holds only within that boundary.","reader_deep":"The component digest, the claim-ceiling index, the question routes, and the evidence pages all check this boundary before an answer is permitted to make a broader claim. Source identifiers may still say claim ceiling or authority ceiling for the same line."},{"object_id":"term:semantic_naming","preferred_label":"semantic naming","reader_preview":"Semantic naming makes an identifier reveal an artifact\u2019s role, authority, lifecycle state, and route to detail.","reader_card":"Under semantic naming, a name is a compact record rather than an arbitrary tag. From the name alone you can infer the artifact's kind, its job, the authority plane it acts on, and how far you can trust it, before opening the source. Each name compresses these facts in a fixed order, so the smallest reading gives the role and a longer reading gives the detail. A name has several layers, including a stable identifier, a storage path, a display label, and aliases, and these can change independently.","reader_rule":"Read a name as a claim about what the artifact is and what it may do, and trust the stable identifier over the display label or alias when they differ.","reader_deep":"Renaming the underlying storage path is treated as a governed migration because other artifacts point at it, whereas a display label or alias can be adjusted more freely; the layers are deliberately separate so a name can be corrected without breaking the things that reference it."},{"object_id":"term:sequence","preferred_label":"sequence","reader_preview":"A sequence is an ordered list of terms indexed by positions, usually the positive integers, so each position has a specified value.","reader_card":"Order is part of a sequence: the same values arranged differently form a different sequence. A sequence may be defined by a direct formula, a recurrence, a selection rule, or observed data.","reader_rule":"Identify the indexing set and rule that generates the terms before using a property of the sequence.","reader_deep":"Infinite sequences are known through finite definitions and proofs about all indices, not by listing every term, which is why recurrences and quantified bounds matter."},{"object_id":"term:series","preferred_label":"series","reader_preview":"In Plectis, series can mean an infinite mathematical sum or an ordered stream of data values; the two uses are distinct.","reader_card":"The Erd\u0151s papers use series for infinite sums defined through limits of partial sums. Software and forecasting components use series for ordered observations such as yields, prices, or losses. A data series need not be summed or convergent, and an infinite mathematical series is not merely a dataset.","reader_rule":"Terms, partial sums, convergence, or irrationality indicate the mathematical sense; timestamps, labels, deltas, forecasts, or observations indicate the data sense.","reader_deep":"For mathematical series, the central question is what limit the partial sums approach and what arithmetic type that limit has. For data series, the central questions are ordering, missing observations, alignment, and change across records. Plectis uses both, so context must select the definition before any inference follows."},{"object_id":"term:set","preferred_label":"set","reader_preview":"A set is a collection determined by which distinct elements belong to it, without treating order or repetition as part of its identity.","reader_card":"In Plectis mathematics, sets usually select indices, exponents, factors, or premises. This matters because several results quantify over every finite or infinite choice of support, not over one listed sequence. The verb \u201cset,\u201d meaning assign a value, is a separate ordinary use.","reader_rule":"For the mathematical noun, identify the membership condition and whether the set is finite or infinite; do not read the assignment verb as this glossary concept.","reader_deep":"Finite denominator certificates use a finite exponent set F so an exact rational sum can be computed and checked. The open support questions quantify over infinite sets, where proving one convenient choice says nothing about all choices. Order and duplicate entries remain irrelevant unless the source defines a sequence instead."},{"object_id":"term:shared_path","preferred_label":"shared path","reader_preview":"The shared path is the sequence most components on this site follow: state a claim, run a check against fixtures, write a result record, and record the scope limit of what that establishes.","reader_card":"Components differ in subject but not in shape. Knowing the shared path means a reader who has understood one component has largely understood how to read the next: find the claim, find the check, open the record, read the limit. The uniformity is the point, because it makes the parts comparable rather than each one a fresh puzzle.","reader_rule":"Once you have followed the shared path through one component, use the same four steps on any other.","reader_deep":"The shape is also what makes the components auditable in bulk. A component that departs from it is visible as a departure rather than as a variation in style."},{"object_id":"term:skill","preferred_label":"skill","reader_preview":"A skill is a discoverable, reusable procedure that packages its invocation triggers, execution steps, constraints, and metadata so an agent does not have to reconstruct the workflow each time.","reader_card":"Where a component is a unit of running machinery, a skill is a unit of action: a written-down operating procedure that tells an agent which task shape it matches and the steps to follow. Each skill carries a trigger that says when it applies, an ordered workflow, the limits it must stay inside, and discovery metadata so it can be located in the system's registry. The registry entry routes an agent to the right skill; the skill file then carries the actual procedure, with links out to the standards and modules that grant it authority.","reader_rule":"Treat a skill as a procedure with a stated scope: it applies to the task shape named in its trigger and runs within the constraints it declares, not as general permission to act.","reader_deep":"A skill is progressive disclosure for action: a short registry row surfaces it during navigation, and the full procedure is opened only once an agent has matched the task to it, keeping the working step list out of the way until it is needed."},{"object_id":"term:slice","preferred_label":"slice","reader_preview":"A slice is a bounded cut through a larger system: the specific files, paths, or records a piece of work covers, named so that what is inside and outside the cut is explicit.","reader_card":"This system is published in slices rather than all at once. A slice names its own edges, which is what lets a component be genuinely runnable while the system it came from stays private. The word carries the same meaning in the working sense: an agent claims a slice of paths, commits that slice, and leaves the rest alone. In both uses the point is the same, that the boundary is stated rather than assumed.","reader_rule":"When something is described as a slice, look for what it excludes. The exclusion is the informative half.","reader_deep":"A slice is the unit that makes partial publication honest. Publishing a whole system is a decision nobody can review; publishing a named cut, with its edges recorded, is one a reader can check by looking at what was left out."},{"object_id":"term:smoke_check","preferred_label":"smoke check","reader_preview":"A smoke check is a fast, shallow check that the basics work at all, run before anything more detailed is attempted.","reader_card":"It answers one question cheaply: is this thing standing up. A smoke check that fails makes deeper testing pointless, and one that passes establishes very little beyond that the obvious is not broken. Both facts are useful in the right order.","reader_rule":"A passing smoke check means nothing is obviously broken. It is not evidence the thing works.","reader_deep":"The value is entirely in sequence. Run late it is redundant; run first it saves a long investigation of a system that was never up."},{"object_id":"term:smooth_number","preferred_label":"smooth number","reader_preview":"A whole number is smooth relative to a set of primes when every prime that divides it lies in that set: equivalently, a y-smooth number has no prime factor larger than y.","reader_card":"Everyday 'smooth' is the wrong picture. In number theory the word names factorisation, not a curve. Erd\u0151s #269 asks about running least common multiples of the numbers whose prime factors all come from a fixed finite set of primes: those are the smooth numbers for that set. The library treats the three-prime case.","reader_rule":"Ask: smooth relative to which primes? A 2-smooth number is a power of two; a {2,3,5}-smooth number may be 2^a 3^b 5^c.","reader_deep":"The running least common multiple of successive smooth numbers grows according to how new prime-power factors appear. That growth controls the reciprocal sum in #269. Proving the three-prime case is not a proof for every finite set of primes."},{"object_id":"term:software","preferred_label":"software","reader_preview":"Here, software is the public Plectis repository of runnable research components, separate from the Lean mathematics repository.","reader_card":"The software repository is the public collection of runnable research components, with a Try the software path through the quickstart. Public source may expose only a bounded slice: dependencies, builds, and runtime still affect what a clone actually does. A screenshot or product description is not the software.","reader_rule":"State which artifact, version, and runtime behaviour a software claim covers before treating a page as evidence that the program ran.","reader_deep":"A software system can be inspected at source, build, test, and runtime layers. Evidence at one layer does not automatically settle the others. Local fixture success is not hosted-service behaviour."},{"object_id":"term:source","preferred_label":"source","reader_preview":"On this site, source is the committed file or path a public claim names as the thing to open, as opposed to the generated page that displays it.","reader_card":"Every component card is supposed to name a source path. The landing specimen does that next to evidence class and scope limit. If the site and the repository disagree, the repository wins because it is the source of record.","reader_rule":"Follow the named source path. Do not treat the generated HTML as the owning artifact.","reader_deep":"A page can be generated from several sources at once: a registry for membership, a paper for exposition, a result record for current evidence. The source label on the card should say which of those the claim is using. A hover glossary link is not a source path."},{"object_id":"term:source_authority","preferred_label":"source authority","reader_preview":"Source authority is the artifact, standard, tool, or ledger that owns a fact more strongly than anything generated from it: the source of record beneath any page, summary, or AI answer that merely reports it.","reader_card":"Doctrine includes the rule keep projections below source authority. Generated pages, digests, and AI summaries on this site are projections of something lower down: a source file, a standard, a validator, a ledger, or a generated sidecar with an ownership contract. When prose, HTML, or a summary may have drifted, that lower artifact is where the claim is settled.","reader_rule":"When a page makes a claim, treat the artifact it cites, not the page, as the owner of the fact.","reader_deep":"Generated public surfaces route, compress, and display. They stay accurate by naming the lower artifact or tool that owns each claim and refusing to assert anything beyond it."},{"object_id":"term:source_body","preferred_label":"source body","reader_preview":"A source body is the actual code of a function or module carried into a public bundle, as opposed to a description, signature, or hash of it.","reader_card":"A file hash proves bytes match; it says nothing about whether the logic is right. Carrying the source body means the published artefact contains the real implementation, so a reader can run it and assert its output rather than trusting a digest. Where the underlying data is private, the body is carried but the records around it stay metadata-only.","reader_rule":"Check whether the public bundle contains the executable implementation or only a description or digest. A source body makes the logic inspectable and runnable; only a named execution receipt shows how it behaved on particular inputs.","reader_deep":"Digest equality proves identity with a declared source, not semantic correctness. Carrying the body closes the inspectability gap because a reader can examine and execute the implementation, while fixtures and result records separately establish observed behavior. Source availability, identity, and execution evidence are three distinct claims."},{"object_id":"term:source_file","preferred_label":"source file","reader_preview":"A source file is the human-written file a program is actually built from, as opposed to anything generated, compiled or copied from it.","reader_card":"Pointing at a source file is more specific than pointing at a project. It names the exact place a behaviour is implemented, so a reader can go and check whether the description matches the code rather than taking the description on trust. Each component here names its source file directly.","reader_rule":"Follow the source-file link. It is the shortest route from a claim to the thing that implements it.","reader_deep":"The reason to name the file rather than the repository is that the repository is searchable but not answerable. A specific path either supports the claim or visibly does not."},{"object_id":"term:source_slice","preferred_label":"source slice","reader_preview":"A source slice is the deliberately selected, bounded set of source files published to back the hosted pages and reader packets, excluding the remainder of the working tree.","reader_card":"The published repository does not contain the whole working tree. The source slice is the portion that has been exported into the public repository, so the source-linked components, packets, and routes on this site can be opened and checked against real code. The cut is deliberate and stated, so what is available to inspect is clear and nothing unpublished is implied to be present.","reader_rule":"Treat the source slice as the part you can actually open and verify. It backs the public objects on the site, but it is not the whole codebase, so unpublished routes, ledgers, and operator context will not be found within it.","reader_deep":"The boundary of the slice is explicit by design: publishing source-linked material does not claim that the public repository is the entire workspace, only that this defined subset is open to inspection."},{"object_id":"term:span","preferred_label":"linear span","reader_preview":"The span of a collection is everything you can build from it by scaling its members and adding them together.","reader_card":"Saying that a family spans a space of a given dimension fixes exactly how much independent information the family carries, which is a sharper statement than saying the family is large.","reader_rule":"A span is determined by the collection, so adding a member that was already reachable does not change it.","reader_deep":"The dyadic sections of Euler's totient span a space of dimension exactly two to the e plus one at every level e at least one. Because that holds at every level, the full span is infinite-dimensional."},{"object_id":"term:sparse_checkout","preferred_label":"sparse checkout","reader_preview":"A sparse checkout downloads only the parts of a repository you name, so a large repository can be used without fetching everything.","reader_card":"The Lean repository offers three: the reader files (papers and guides), a quick proof build, and the full Lean source. The README gives the exact commands, and the reproducibility guide says what each one lets you check.","reader_rule":"Pick the checkout that matches what you want to do; you can widen it later.","reader_deep":"A sparse checkout is a snapshot at one revision. It does not update itself and it does not contribute anything by itself."},{"object_id":"term:specification","preferred_label":"specification","reader_preview":"A specification is an explicit description of required behaviour, structure, interface, or constraints against which an implementation can be judged.","reader_card":"A specification states what must hold without necessarily prescribing every internal method used to make it hold. Good specifications define inputs, outputs, invariants, edge cases, and permitted variation at the needed level.","reader_rule":"Use the specification as the comparison surface for implementation and validation, while checking that it reflects the intended purpose.","reader_deep":"Completeness and correctness of a specification are separate from conformance to it; an implementation can satisfy a flawed specification."},{"object_id":"term:squarefree","preferred_label":"squarefree","reader_preview":"A whole number is squarefree when no prime divides it more than once, so it has no repeated prime factor.","reader_card":"Ten equals two times five and is squarefree. Twelve equals two times two times three and is not, because two appears twice.","reader_rule":"A squarefree condition restricts which indices may appear in a support, so a theorem proved for squarefree support does not cover arbitrary support.","reader_deep":"For Erd\u0151s #257, irrationality at squarefree support over power-of-two bases is a cited prior result rather than a contribution of this corpus, and the papers say so."},{"object_id":"term:standard","preferred_label":"standard","reader_preview":"A standard is a machine-readable contract fixing an artifact kind's required fields, authority, validation, and maintenance rules in a form that both people and agents can inspect and check.","reader_card":"Each kind of governed artifact in the system, such as a component record, a paper module, or a doctrine card, has a standard that states its grammar: the fields that matter, the authority posture that applies, and the checks a change must pass. Because a standard is itself a structured file rather than prose advice, it can be validated automatically, and an artifact can be confirmed to conform to it. It encodes not just form but intent, lifecycle, and how the artifact is found, so the shape of a thing does not have to be inferred by reading examples.","reader_rule":"When something is governed by a standard, the standard is the authority on its shape and rules: it states which fields and checks the artifact is held to, rather than leaving them to be inferred from a single instance.","reader_deep":"A standard works as executable grammar for one plane of the system: it carries the artifact kind's form, authority, validation, and maintenance rules together, so the contract and the things it governs stay aligned over time."},{"object_id":"term:statement_node","preferred_label":"statement node","reader_preview":"A statement node is one mathematically distinct statement in the map over a proof corpus, which several declarations may share if they establish the same thing in different form.","reader_card":"This is the unit that makes a large corpus comprehensible. Raw declarations count restatements, generated instances and technical variants separately; statement nodes collapse them to the distinct things actually being asserted. The gap between the two numbers is the honest measure of how much of a corpus is machinery.","reader_rule":"Read the statement-node count, not the declaration count, when asking how much a corpus says.","reader_deep":"Collapsing is an authored judgement, not an automatic one: deciding two declarations say the same thing in different dress is exactly the step a machine cannot take unaided."},{"object_id":"term:stationary_bootstrap","preferred_label":"stationary bootstrap","reader_preview":"The stationary bootstrap resamples a time series in randomly sized blocks, so the resampled series keeps the short-range dependence the original had.","reader_card":"Resampling one observation at a time destroys the ordering, and financial series are not independent from one observation to the next. Blocks preserve enough of the local structure for the resampled series to be a fair stand-in.","reader_rule":"The method preserves short-range dependence. It does not preserve long-range structure or regime changes.","reader_deep":"It is used here to give the forecast-comparison tests a distribution to work against, over synthetic market fixtures rather than live data."},{"object_id":"term:stub","preferred_label":"stub","reader_preview":"A stub is a placeholder with the shape of the real thing but not its contents.","reader_card":"A stub lets a public release keep the interface of a private component without shipping what the component actually holds. It is honest only when it is labelled, because an unlabelled stub reads as working code.","reader_rule":"A stub is not evidence that the thing it stands in for works. It shows only the shape.","reader_deep":"The public release contains source-linked components, public-safe stubs, and synthetic fixtures. The stubs mark where the private data stops."},{"object_id":"term:substrate","preferred_label":"substrate","reader_preview":"A substrate is the underlying body of files, routes, recorded state, and tools that the system operates on, distinct from the pages and summaries projected over that source material.","reader_card":"You meet this word on the glossary. The substrate is everything the public pages are generated from: the source files, the standards, the recorded state, the validators, and the tools that act on them. The Source and license page is the public entry into that layer.","reader_rule":"When something is called the substrate, treat it as the lower source layer that a page is reporting on, and look to it rather than the page when you need the actual file or record.","reader_deep":"A generated page can describe or point into the substrate, but it does not own the source records, package identifiers, or state transitions that live there. The page makes a map, digest, or evidence view; the substrate remains the authority underneath."},{"object_id":"term:subsum","preferred_label":"subsum","reader_preview":"A subsum of a series is the total you get by keeping some of its terms and discarding the rest.","reader_card":"Collecting every subsum of a fixed series gives one set of numbers, called its achievement set. Its shape decides which values the series can reach, so questions about whether a target such as one half is reachable become questions about that set.","reader_rule":"A subsum over a finite selection and a subsum over an infinite selection are different objects, and a result about one does not transfer to the other.","reader_deep":"For Erd\u0151s #257 the targets one half and one twenty-first have no finite-support representation, and both reduce to explicit alternatives about infinite orbits. Neither has been decided."},{"object_id":"term:sum","preferred_label":"sum","reader_preview":"A sum is a quantity formed by addition; Plectis distinguishes finite exact sums from infinite sums defined by limits.","reader_card":"Finite sums in Plectis can be calculated exactly and reduced to rational form for certificate checks. Infinite sums are series: computation reaches only a partial sum, while the paper\u2019s question concerns the limiting value and its arithmetic type.","reader_rule":"Identify the index set and whether the sum is finite, truncated, or infinite before transferring any computed or proved property.","reader_deep":"The finite denominator-certificate components deliberately turn selected reciprocal terms into an exact rational P/Q. The Erd\u0151s papers ask about completed infinite sums, where convergence, tail control, and justified rearrangement become additional obligations. A certificate for the finite sum is evidence about that bounded object, not automatically the infinite limit."},{"object_id":"term:superior_predictive_ability","preferred_label":"superior predictive ability","reader_preview":"Hansen's superior predictive ability test asks whether any method in a whole set genuinely beats a benchmark, correcting for the fact that searching many methods will throw up a winner by luck alone.","reader_card":"It exists because comparing one favourite method against a benchmark, after quietly trying fifty, is how spurious forecasting results are produced. The correction is for the size of the search, not for the quality of any one method.","reader_rule":"The test controls for multiple comparisons. It does not establish that the winning method will keep winning.","reader_deep":"Forecast-skill claims here run this test alongside Diebold-Mariano and a stationary bootstrap, over synthetic fixtures. Passing on a fixture shows the check runs and refuses as specified for that fixture."},{"object_id":"term:support","preferred_label":"support","reader_preview":"On Plectis, support can mean evidence backing a claim or, in the Erd\u0151s mathematics, the set of indices whose terms are included in a sum.","reader_card":"Evidential support is a scoped relation between a claim and inspectable sources, checks, or records. Mathematical support is a membership set selecting nonzero or included terms. The site uses both heavily, and neither sense should be inferred from the other.","reader_rule":"Claims, evidence, sources, or authority indicate evidential support; indices, exponents, terms, finite sets, or infinite sets indicate mathematical support.","reader_deep":"Evidential support answers why a reader may rely on a bounded claim and must preserve provenance and scope. Series support answers which reciprocal terms participate and can itself be the object quantified over by an open problem. Sharing the noun does not turn evidence relations into mathematical membership or vice versa."},{"object_id":"term:sylvester_sequence","preferred_label":"Sylvester's sequence","reader_preview":"Sylvester's sequence is 2, 3, 7, 43, 1807, and onward, where each term is the product of all earlier terms plus one.","reader_card":"Its reciprocals sum to exactly one, and it is the greedy way of writing one as a sum of distinct unit fractions. That makes it the extreme case against which nearby sequences are measured.","reader_rule":"The recurrence is a condition a sequence may eventually satisfy; saying it begins at some index is weaker than saying it holds from the start.","reader_deep":"Erd\u0151s #243 asks about the rigidity of sequences near this one. The Lean work proves that normalised vanishing together with an eventually bounded negative part forces the error to vanish and the recurrence to begin. The negative-part bound itself is not proved, and that is where the problem stays open."},{"object_id":"term:synth_seed","preferred_label":"synth seed","reader_preview":"A synth seed is the current phase brief: its goal, success conditions, relevant files, and next execution wave distilled from durable intent.","reader_card":"A synth seed sits between the system's broad, lasting statement of intent and the moment-to-moment execution of one phase. It carries the goal, the reason the work is happening now, the success criteria, the relevant files, the wave, and the supporting context needed to act without rereading everything upstream. It is deliberately disposable: it is the authoritative model for the phase it describes and is meant to be replaced as the work moves on, not preserved as the system's permanent voice.","reader_rule":"Treat a synth seed as the live brief for the phase it names, accurate as of that phase and no wider. It states what one slice of work is trying to do, not the system's lasting intent.","reader_deep":"It is produced by distilling the lasting statement of intent down to a task-shaped form, so it is concrete and bounded by design rather than comprehensive."},{"object_id":"term:synthetic","preferred_label":"synthetic","reader_preview":"Synthetic means data made up for the purpose of a check, rather than taken from real use.","reader_card":"Everything runnable here runs on synthetic inputs. That is what allows the components to be published at all, and it is a real limit rather than a formality: a component shown to work on constructed data has not been shown to work on the messier real thing. The scope limit on each result says so rather than leaving it implied.","reader_rule":"A passing check on synthetic data establishes the constructed case, not the real one.","reader_deep":"The trade is reproducibility for realism, taken deliberately and in one direction. It is stated because the alternative is a reader assuming the stronger claim for free."},{"object_id":"term:terminal","preferred_label":"terminal","reader_preview":"The terminal is the text interface where you type commands directly to your computer rather than clicking through a screen.","reader_card":"Every route here that produces evidence runs in a terminal. The commands are written out in full so they can be copied rather than reconstructed, and they are ordinary commands with no wrapper of this project's own.","reader_rule":"If a step cannot be expressed as a command you can copy, it has not been made reproducible.","reader_deep":"Writing the exact command down is a small discipline with a large effect: it converts a described procedure into one someone else can execute identically."},{"object_id":"term:test","preferred_label":"test","reader_preview":"On this site, a test is the procedure a component ships (usually pytest, sometimes Lean) that compares what happened with what the claim said should happen.","reader_card":"Public components name the test module and the command. Review receipts show which pytest lanes reproduced, including planted-forgery lanes. Passing the public tests does not certify the private system.","reader_rule":"Read which cases the test actually runs before treating a green result as a wide claim.","reader_deep":"A test that cannot fail is theatre. Negative cases, planted forgeries, and fixture-bound replays exist so a pass means something narrower and checkable. The evidence page groups components by what their tests establish, not by how many there are."},{"object_id":"term:theorem","preferred_label":"theorem","reader_preview":"A theorem is a mathematical statement together with a proof of it.","reader_card":"In a formal corpus each theorem is a named item the proof checker has verified. The count is a measure of size rather than of significance: theorems range from a substantial result to a mechanical step generated in bulk, and most of a large corpus is the latter.","reader_rule":"A theorem is settled for exactly what it states. Read the statement, not the name.","reader_deep":"The gap between what a theorem states and what its title suggests is where formal work is most often overread, which is why the statements rather than the labels are the published object."},{"object_id":"term:toolchain","preferred_label":"toolchain","reader_preview":"A toolchain is the ordered set of compilers, interpreters, package managers, builders, and checkers used to turn source files into a runnable or verified result.","reader_card":"A toolchain is part of a result's provenance because changing one tool or version can change whether the source builds or what output it produces. Reproducible work records the exact versions and the command path through them.","reader_rule":"When a build or proof result matters, check which toolchain and versions produced it.","reader_deep":"Toolchains form a dependency chain: source is parsed, dependencies are resolved, code is compiled or elaborated, and a final checker accepts or rejects the output, so each stage has a different role in trust."},{"object_id":"term:totient","preferred_label":"totient","reader_preview":"The totient of a whole number is how many smaller positive numbers share no factor with it.","reader_card":"It is one of the oldest functions in number theory and behaves irregularly: it jumps around according to the factorisation of each number rather than following any smooth rule. That irregularity is what makes a series built from it hard, because no simple approximation captures the terms.","reader_rule":"A totient series inherits the irregularity of factorisation.","reader_deep":"The approach taken here is to find exact structure in the irregularity rather than to approximate it, which is what the rational basis for the dyadic sections provides."},{"object_id":"term:transcendental","preferred_label":"transcendental","reader_preview":"A number is transcendental when no polynomial with whole-number coefficients has it as a root.","reader_card":"Transcendence is strictly stronger than irrationality. Every transcendental number is irrational, but the reverse fails: the square root of two is irrational and satisfies x squared minus two, so it is not transcendental. Very few tools reach an individual constant, which is why transcendence results for specific series are rare.","reader_rule":"A transcendence claim is stronger than the matching irrationality claim, so check which one a result actually delivers.","reader_deep":"In the Erd\u0151s #269 work the reciprocal sums of running least common multiples over two distinct primes are argued transcendental by a paper argument using a result of Bugeaud and Laurent. That deduction is not Lean-checked, and Steve Fan published the same two-prime reduction first. Three primes onward remains open."},{"object_id":"term:transcript","preferred_label":"transcript","reader_preview":"A transcript is the recorded text of a session, kept so what was said or done can be reread rather than recalled.","reader_card":"Transcripts here are evidence in the same sense as result records: the account written afterwards is a summary, and the transcript is the thing it summarises. Keeping both is what makes it possible to check one against the other.","reader_rule":"Where a summary and a transcript disagree, the transcript is the record.","reader_deep":"Summaries are not usually dishonest; they are lossy in a direction that flatters. Keeping the source makes the loss recoverable."},{"object_id":"term:truth_maintenance","preferred_label":"truth maintenance","reader_preview":"Truth maintenance is the bookkeeping that ties each belief to its evidence, dependencies, invalidation conditions, and consequences so failed assumptions or contradictions are routed to repair instead of silently corrupting settled state.","reader_card":"Instead of holding facts as one flat, undifferentiated pile, the system records for each claim its evidence, its dependencies, its current status, its appeal state, the conditions that would invalidate it, and the actions it does or does not license. A receipt from a check, a model's output, a generated page, and a doctrine commitment therefore do not share one indiscriminate authority. The standing question is always what is believed now, why, what would overturn it, and what stays blocked while uncertainty remains.","reader_rule":"When a belief on this site is presented as held, treat it as held for stated reasons and only until those reasons are undercut: look for what it depends on and what would invalidate it, rather than reading it as fixed.","reader_deep":"It is a diagnostic discipline here, adapted locally by the Workingness Instrument: a claim that fails or is contradicted lowers in status and gates the actions that depended on it, instead of vanishing or quietly persisting as if still sound."},{"object_id":"term:trying","preferred_label":"trying","reader_preview":"Trying is attempting the work so that failure modes can be encountered; it is not a claim that the attempt succeeded.","reader_card":"Trying is the method: put incomplete work in public, keep the failed routes, spend time with the breaks, and attempt repairs. It is the opposite of waiting until the story can be told as an unbroken success. Uncertainty about whether the campaign is a good use of time is compatible with this: trying does not require already knowing what you are doing.","reader_rule":"Read trying as the attempt, with its failures included. Do not upgrade it to a result, a proof, or a claim that the project has arrived.","reader_deep":"Trying is how a guinea pig generates evidence. Mining is one continuous form of it under a given direction. Formal verification can accept or refuse a particular attempt; it cannot tell you whether the attempt was the interesting one. The public record is supposed to show the trying, not only the parts that later looked inevitable."},{"object_id":"term:type_a","preferred_label":"Type A","reader_preview":"A Type A is an actor with gated access to the live substrate, able to inspect current files and records, run tools, and perform mutations such as editing, validation, testing, committing, or routing work.","reader_card":"Type A describes a shape of actor, not a quality ranking. It is the lane with live-substrate authority: it can read the working files on disk, run commands, make edits under the system's gates, check its own work, and feed what it learned back into the shared records. Its freedom is bounded by the latest instruction it was given, the limits of what it is allowed to change, the standards, and the requirement to validate, roll back, and leave a record. Controller sessions are Type A; a tool-using helper agent with live access to the repository is a delegated Type A.","reader_rule":"When something is marked Type A, take it as an actor operating on the live system with real power to change it, kept inside defined gates, rather than a measure of how capable or senior that actor is.","reader_deep":"Type A sits on one axis only, substrate authority; how capable the actor is, which surface it runs on, its delegation role, its context mode, and its thinking budget are separate, independent classifications. The contrasting shape, Type B, reads the same world but works from prepared material rather than live private state and does not hold mutation authority."},{"object_id":"term:type_a_judgment","preferred_label":"Type A judgment","reader_preview":"Type A judgment is a capable substrate actor's governed discernment for noticing what is missing, stale, or misplaced and inferring the right bounded destination from the system's own rules without waiting for a bespoke instruction.","reader_card":"When an agent reads this system as a body of rules, standards, and recorded state, Type A judgment is the part of its work that is not mechanical: spotting a missing route, a standard that no longer matches reality, or a local lesson worth carrying back into the shared rules. It is a small, open set of traits rather than a fixed list of virtues, with attention and care as early examples, and it is meant to be traceable rather than a passing mood.","reader_rule":"Where the site refers to Type A judgment, treat it as the agent's discernment exercised on this material, grounded in the system's rules and records, not as an unaccountable opinion.","reader_deep":"It rests on a simple invariant: if a judgment changes how a later agent will behave, it should be written down with its sources and routes rather than left as something only the current session knows."},{"object_id":"term:type_a_metabolism","preferred_label":"Type A metabolism","reader_preview":"Type A metabolism is the high-agency lane that resolves work with no settled rule (naming primitives, repairing contradictions, changing standards, or promoting principles) into governed structure that later routine work can follow.","reader_card":"Most upkeep in the system runs along fixed tracks: a known job, a validator, a row to patch. Type A metabolism is what runs when no such track exists yet. It takes a broad, ambiguous situation and collapses it into something bounded and reusable, such as a named rule, a standard, or a repaired scaffold, while preserving the original situation, the limit of what it is allowed to decide, its checks, and a way to roll back. It is the system changing its own rules rather than just applying them, so it is treated as expensive and powerful.","reader_rule":"When you meet Type A metabolism, read it as a record of judgement applied where the rules were still open: the system deciding a new boundary under validation, not running a routine job. The decision it reached is what makes the later routine work cheap.","reader_deep":"Its judgement loop draws on the system's axioms and principles as design intuition and on imported patterns as raw material, and it ends in either a better local rule or a named, recorded rejection. Once the resulting grammar is stable, ongoing maintenance moves to the lower-agency Type B lane."},{"object_id":"term:type_a_operating_register","preferred_label":"Type A operating register","reader_preview":"The Type A operating register is the expected conduct of a live-substrate agent: steward what it touches and durably route what it learns.","reader_card":"It is one of two registers the system distinguishes. A Type A agent is a high-agency controller, a coding agent or its operator, acting on the system from the inside; the register describes how that controller is meant to behave across a piece of work. The behaviours are concrete: steward and thicken the files and records it edits, watch for drift between a summary and what it describes, use what it builds, and pass any local discovery upward through the narrowest honest channel. It is a posture, composed from named judgment traits like care, common sense, and critic stance, not a single tool or a step in a checklist.","reader_rule":"When a page describes work in the Type A register, read it as the controller's own account of how it operated: what it stewarded, what it noticed, and where it routed each finding. The register tells you the standard of conduct behind the work, not a guarantee about any single output.","reader_deep":"The register pairs with Type A judgment, which holds the individual trait definitions, and with the propagation surfaces that carry a local finding into doctrine, standards, or vocabulary according to how settled it is. It governs the behaviour of the controller, not the content of any one component."},{"object_id":"term:type_b","preferred_label":"Type B","reader_preview":"A Type B is an actor reasoning from context handed to it outside the live substrate, able to produce advice, drafts, or candidate answers but unable to inspect private files, run local commands, or mutate the system directly.","reader_card":"Type B is one of two actor shapes the system recognises. A Type B actor reasons through a chat window, a browser session, a provider or web API, or any channel where it sees only the context it was given, not the live repository. It can decide, research, critique, and correct at a high level over that materialised context, but it has no direct authority to inspect private files, execute commands, edit, test, or commit; any such action has to be carried out by an actor that does hold that access. The counterpart shape, Type A, is the actor running inside the live machinery with those affordances.","reader_rule":"When something is produced by Type B, treat it as advisory: a synthesis built from the context it was shown, to be checked against the live system before anything is acted on.","reader_deep":"The distinction is about access to the live machinery, not about intelligence: a capable Type B actor can outthink the harness it cannot touch, and a delegated worker that does hold live repository tools is a Type A actor, not Type B, despite being delegated."},{"object_id":"term:type_b_metabolism","preferred_label":"Type B metabolism","reader_preview":"Type B metabolism is the bounded, deterministic upkeep lane for repeatable work after a structure is settled, such as syncing files, refreshing projections, filling records, and running checks rather than inventing new governing shapes.","reader_card":"This is how the system keeps its many surfaces current at scale. Once a kind of thing has a known shape, with a defined target, input, output schema, validation command, and a record of what it is allowed to claim, Type B work carries out the repeatable part: refreshing an index, refilling a summary band, detecting duplicates, checking that evidence pointers still resolve, or applying a draft patch to a row. Each run is bounded by a fixed input and a fixed output shape, is checked against a stated standard, and can be safely re-run without changing the result.","reader_rule":"When a surface is described as produced by Type B metabolism, treat it as maintenance against a settled shape: it is kept current by repeatable, validated jobs, and it stops short of inventing new structure or making a final promotion decision on its own.","reader_deep":"It can be carried out by plain deterministic code, small validators, scheduled jobs, or cheap external calls, and its safety rests on four things: a bounded input, an explicit output shape, a validation step, and the fact that re-running it produces the same result. The complementary, open-ended lane that first makes a thing legible enough to maintain this way is called Type A."},{"object_id":"term:typed_refusal","preferred_label":"typed refusal","reader_preview":"A typed refusal is an explicit, reason-carrying no-result when a computation cannot honestly produce a value, instead of a plausible fake answer.","reader_card":"The doctrine operating loop says failure requires a typed refusal with its reason and evidence. Missing a named basis, a checker timeout, or an uncovered case should surface as that refusal, not as a fluent paragraph that looks like a result. Public pages that cannot support a claim are supposed to say so in this shape.","reader_rule":"If a route cannot produce the claimed object, expect a typed refusal rather than a best-effort substitute.","reader_deep":"Fail-closed behaviour and typed refusal travel together: missing evidence blocks instead of silently passing. A refusal is still a record. It can be inspected, appealed, or repaired. A hallucinated number cannot."},{"object_id":"term:typed_relation","preferred_label":"typed relation","reader_preview":"A typed relation is a recorded link between two statements that says what kind of link it is: that one implies another, reformulates it, is a finite instance of it, or rules out a route to it.","reader_card":"An untyped link only says two things are related, which is nearly useless for reasoning. Typing the link is what makes the map answerable: you can ask what a result implies, what still depends on an unproved antecedent, or which approaches have already been closed off, and get an answer rather than a reading list.","reader_rule":"Follow the relation type, not just the link. What connects two statements is the content.","reader_deep":"Recording the type is also what makes a wrong connection falsifiable. A link labelled implies can be checked; a link labelled related cannot."},{"object_id":"term:uninterpretable","preferred_label":"uninterpretable","reader_preview":"Uninterpretable means a person cannot yet restate, situate, or explain the result, even if a machine has accepted a formal version of it.","reader_card":"Uninterpretable mathematics is work a person cannot yet restate, situate, or explain. Interpretation here is exposition first and digestion later: can a qualified reader be shown what was proved, why it works, who did which part, and what remains open? Formal verification can proxy the narrower question of whether a written statement follows inside a kernel. It cannot replace that human account, and it cannot complete community digestion. A green check on an opaque term is the case Tao flags: likely correct, still without anyone able to give the talk.","reader_rule":"Treat uninterpretable as a failure of explanation, not as a failure of the kernel. Do not read Lean acceptance as proof that the result has been interpreted.","reader_deep":"Digestion is community uptake after a result is generated, verified, written, and published. Exposition is the writing and talking that would let someone else give an expert account. Formal verification sits earlier: it removes a class of hidden gaps in the formal chain. Using it as a stand-in for understanding is the proxy mistake. The proxy is useful. It is not the thing."},{"object_id":"term:unit_disc","preferred_label":"unit disc","reader_preview":"The open unit disc is the set of complex numbers whose distance from the origin is strictly less than 1, written |z| < 1.","reader_card":"It is the interior of the unit circle, not the circle itself and not the closed disc |z| \u2264 1 unless a page says closed. Erd\u0151s #1041 places the roots of a monic polynomial inside this open disc, then asks whether two of those roots can be joined by a short curve that stays inside a lemniscate. Moving a root onto the unit circle leaves the hypothesis.","reader_rule":"Open means the boundary is excluded. A root with |z| = 1 is not in the open unit disc.","reader_deep":"Complex analysis often uses the open unit disc as the model bounded domain: holomorphic functions, Blaschke factors, and polynomial lemniscates behave cleanly there. Statements proved for roots inside |z| < 1 do not automatically extend to roots on or outside the circle."},{"object_id":"term:up_propagate","preferred_label":"up-propagate","reader_preview":"To up-propagate is to move a reusable local lesson into the smallest durable system artifact that should carry it.","reader_card":"Up-propagation is the direction in which reusable learning travels through the system: from a specific local case toward a more lasting home such as a skill, a standard, a paper module, an agent seed, or an axiom. The destination is chosen by how settled and how authoritative the lesson is, so a routine procedure lands in a skill, a fixed data shape lands in a standard, and operator intent lands in the source voice records. The aim is to record the lesson once, in the least authoritative place that can hold it honestly, rather than letting it be rediscovered.","reader_rule":"When something is described as up-propagated, treat it as a local finding that has been deliberately lifted into a more durable artifact; trace it to that artifact to see where it now lives and what scope it claims.","reader_deep":"Up-propagation does not turn every finding into doctrine. It routes a finding to the least authoritative durable layer that can preserve it without overstating it, which is why most lessons settle as skills or standards rather than as axioms or principles."},{"object_id":"term:validator","preferred_label":"validator","reader_preview":"A validator is a program or component that applies declared rules to an input and emits a structured pass, fail, warning, or blocked result.","reader_card":"A validator concentrates a policy into executable checks so the same input is judged consistently. Its verdict is only as strong as the rules it implements, the data it actually reads, and the negative cases proving it rejects invalid inputs.","reader_rule":"Inspect what the validator recomputes and which failure cases it exercises before treating its verdict as evidence.","reader_deep":"A trustworthy validator is small enough to review, deterministic where possible, self-falsifying through negative fixtures, and explicit about checks it delegates or cannot perform."},{"object_id":"term:variable","preferred_label":"variable","reader_preview":"A variable is a named value that may vary or be supplied later; in Plectis it appears both as a mathematical symbol and as a program or configuration value.","reader_card":"A theorem variable receives a type, quantifier, and logical scope. A program variable receives a value from code, input, configuration, or the environment and has a runtime lifetime. The same spelling does not give those two variables the same source or authority.","reader_rule":"For mathematics, track type, quantifier, and binder; for software, track declaration, input source, lifetime, and mutation.","reader_deep":"Lean makes mathematical variable scope explicit so a symbol cannot silently change type or dependency across a proof. Runtime and environment variables instead derive their meaning from the executing program and current environment, so reproduction requires recording both their names and effective values."},{"object_id":"term:vibe_mathing","preferred_label":"vibe-mathing","reader_preview":"Vibe-mathing is doing mathematics with an AI by steering on intuition and conversation, letting the model supply the argument, and having no independent way to tell which steps hold.","reader_card":"The phrase is this project's name for the mathematical cousin of vibe coding. It is how the work started in early 2025, and it is the failure mode that made formal verification and digestion the priorities. A fluent AI proof reads like a correct one. Without a checker, and without the background to reconstruct the hard step, a lay person cannot separate generation from verification. Tao's pipeline makes the same cut: producing a candidate proof is only the first stage. Vibe-mathing stops at that stage and treats fluency as evidence. This site is the attempt to refuse that collapse: keep the generated work, check what can be checked in Lean, and leave the insights, false starts, and remaining obligations visible so someone else can digest them.","reader_rule":"Read vibe-mathing as the method this project moved away from, not the method it recommends.","reader_deep":"Tao's failure mode is a verified proof that nobody, including the prompters, can explain. The remaining bet on this site is the author's, not Tao's: if a model is more capable than the person steering it, that same capability should be asked to translate the working back into something a person can check and explain. Generation without that translation is still vibe-mathing."},{"object_id":"term:wave","preferred_label":"wave","reader_preview":"A wave is one bounded pass of execution inside a phase: a single planned unit of work with a stated objective, an execution mode, the paths it is allowed to touch, and a route for folding its outcome back into the system's durable record.","reader_card":"Work in this system is organised into phases, and each phase is carried out as a sequence of waves rather than one continuously rewritten effort. A wave plans a single pass, runs it, records what it did and what the evidence showed, and then either evolves the working state or hands off cleanly. Because each wave has a defined boundary, the work stays resumable: a later pass can pick up from the recorded outcome instead of from chat history.","reader_rule":"Take a wave as one finished pass of work with its own objective and recorded outcome, not the whole phase. What it changed and what it proved sit inside that one pass.","reader_deep":"A wave can run in different execution modes, from a direct local change to a delegated or multi-agent pass, and its result is assimilated into the system's durable state so that the boundary between current work and the settled substrate is preserved."},{"object_id":"term:whisper","preferred_label":"Whisper","reader_preview":"Whisper is OpenAI's speech-to-text model, which turns recorded audio into text with timings for each word.","reader_card":"The timings are what make it useful for video work: once every word has a timestamp, the words can be lined up against the frames that were on screen when they were spoken.","reader_rule":"A transcript is a rendering of the audio. It is not a record of what was on screen.","reader_deep":"The video editor inside the private system transcribes locally with Whisper, indexes the screen frames against the words, and writes every cut as a proposal a person has to accept before it renders."},{"object_id":"term:whole_system_correctness","preferred_label":"whole-system correctness","reader_preview":"Whole-system correctness is the claim that an entire system behaves correctly, used here mainly as an explicit boundary that no individual page, component, or passing check is allowed to assert.","reader_card":"Every component here establishes something local: this check, on these inputs, produced this result. None of them add up to a statement about the system entire. The phrase is used as a refusal rather than a goal, and the scope limit on each result names it explicitly so that a passing check is not read as a wider guarantee.","reader_rule":"Treat any wording that implies whole-system correctness as an overclaim, including on this site.","reader_deep":"The reason for naming a claim nobody makes is that it is the claim readers infer for free. A repository of passing checks reads like a working system unless the gap is stated, so the release gates refuse language that closes it."},{"object_id":"term:witness","preferred_label":"check result","reader_preview":"A witness is the durable record that one specific check ran and what it found, shown in public copy as a check result or verification record.","reader_card":"Public copy usually says check result or verification record; the glossary keeps witness because source routing still uses it. On the evidence page, the artifact behind a supported statement is the output of a validator, a test, or a source-row check, naming what was examined and what held.","reader_rule":"Read what a witness actually checked before relying on it. It vouches for that one claim and nothing wider.","reader_deep":"Each witness carries its scope limit with it: the command or record that produced it, the exact thing it examined, and the boundary of what that result does and does not cover."},{"object_id":"term:work_landing","preferred_label":"work landing","reader_preview":"Work landing is the transition from attempted activity to a durable accepted result whose changes, validation, ownership, and closeout are recorded.","reader_card":"Work lands only when the attempted change reaches the durable surface that owns it and can be reopened without the session that produced it. In ai_workflow that normally means the owned source path contains the change, the focused validation has a recorded outcome, concurrent ownership was respected, the resulting status and remaining boundary are explicit, and the change has a commit or equivalent durable receipt. A successful-looking tool run or chat summary is still only activity until those pieces agree.","reader_rule":"Treat work as landed only when the artifact and its validation record can be reopened independently of the session that produced them.","reader_deep":"Landing is the acceptance transition between mutation and continuity. It binds the changed artifact to provenance, validation, ownership, reversibility, status, and any residual that still needs an owner. This is why a patch can be technically correct yet not landed, and why landing one bounded change says nothing about unrelated work."},{"object_id":"term:working_record","preferred_label":"working record","reader_preview":"A working record is the long-form rendered PDF that preserves the reasoning surface for one Plectis mathematics problem.","reader_card":"A Plectis working record is the long-form companion to a short problem note. It keeps the mathematical setup, checked premises, routes that were explored, failures that materially changed the direction, exact formal results, and the surviving open obligation together in one rendered PDF. It is designed to show substantially more of the reasoning path than the polished note while remaining an authored, versioned account rather than a raw session dump.","reader_rule":"Use a working record to inspect how the argument and its dead ends developed, then follow its Lean and claim-registry links for proof status. Do not treat it as an exhaustive trace of every private search step.","reader_deep":"The distinction is three-way. A problem note gives the short proved/open boundary; a working record gives the extended reasoning surface; an agent trace or private search history records lower-level session events and may not be public. The working record selects and organizes the reasoning needed to understand the mathematical route without pretending that selection is the proof or the complete provenance."},{"object_id":"term:workingness","preferred_label":"workingness","reader_preview":"Workingness is whether the system knows what it believes, why, what it does not know, and what that uncertainty still permits, rather than a count of passing tests.","reader_card":"Workingness asks a sharper question than \"do the tests pass\". It is the diagnostic condition in which the system can turn evidence into calibrated belief, send each failure to the part of the system that owns its repair, and act only within bounds it is explicitly allowed to. It separates whether events were observed from whether the system's own state of belief is sound, and it is read off concrete signals such as a court pass, a drift check, or a live receipt before any change is made.","reader_rule":"Treat a workingness reading as a statement about the system's self-knowledge at that moment, not a warranty that everything is correct. Check what it is actually grounded in before relying on it to permit a change.","reader_deep":"Workingness is the control question behind the system improving itself: can it convert evidence into calibrated belief, failures into the right repair plane, and judgement into bounded permission to act, without corrupting its source material, its doctrine, its route graph, or its generated pages."},{"object_id":"term:workingness_instrument","preferred_label":"Workingness Instrument","reader_preview":"A workingness instrument is the record that states what one live result proves and which changes that evidence permits or blocks.","reader_card":"It is the row-shaped contract for one live outcome. In one place it pins down the kind of claim, its current status, the evidence and dependencies under it, the conditions that would invalidate it, the appeals that block it from being promoted, and the repairs and mutations it allows or forbids. It turns judgments that would otherwise stay implicit into explicit fields: this receipt counts as evidence, this scorer output is a judgment, this projection is stale, this change is forbidden until a named gate passes.","reader_rule":"Read a workingness instrument as the boundary on what a result lets you do: its allowed and blocked actions are the operative part, not the surrounding summary.","reader_deep":"It is a diagnostic contract, not an authority writer: it governs and records what a result permits, but it does not itself change the doctrine, the route graph, or the standards."}]};
